  ($q$ii-649$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A scientist measured the number of bacteria colonies on plates treated with three cleaners: Cleaner A, 12 colonies; Cleaner B, 4 colonies; no cleaner, 85 colonies. She claims that both cleaners reduce bacteria, with Cleaner B more effective.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports her claim?$q$, $q$["Cleaner A plates had 12 colonies and Cleaner B plates had 4, both far below the 85 on untreated plates.","Cleaner B plates had 4 colonies.","Untreated plates had 85 colonies.","Three treatments were used."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both cleaners' counts (12 and 4) are far below the 85 on untreated plates, and Cleaner B's count is lower than Cleaner A's; the other choices give only one figure or a fact about the setup.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-650$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A town's population was 8,200 in 2000, 9,100 in 2010, and 10,400 in 2020. A planner claims that the town's population grew faster between 2010 and 2020 than between 2000 and 2010.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the planner's claim?$q$, $q$["The population was 9,100 in 2010.","The town had 10,400 residents in 2020.","Population is measured every decade.","The population rose by 1,300 from 2010 to 2020, compared with 900 from 2000 to 2010."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$1,300 > 900.$q$, null),
