  ($q$ii-653$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A study compared reading scores of students who attended a summer program (average gain 8 points) with those who did not (average gain 2 points). The organizers claim that the program was beneficial.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the organizers' claim?$q$, $q$["Some students did not attend.","The program lasted six weeks.","Reading scores were measured.","Students in the program gained 8 points on average, compared with 2 for students who did not attend."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The larger gain suggests benefit.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-654$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A table lists the percentage of customers who returned within a month after buying at each of three stores: Store 1, 42%; Store 2, 58%; Store 3, 49%. A manager claims that Store 2 has the strongest repeat business.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the claim?$q$, $q$["Three stores were studied.","Store 3 had 49%.","Store 2's 58% return rate exceeds Store 1's 42% and Store 3's 49%.","Store 1 had 42%."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$58% is highest.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-655$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$Data show that in a city, 240 of 800 cyclists wore helmets in 2015 and 450 of 900 wore helmets in 2022. An advocate claims that helmet use increased over that period.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the advocate's claim?$q$, $q$["450 cyclists wore helmets in 2022.","240 cyclists wore helmets in 2015.","Helmet use rose from 30 percent of cyclists in 2015 to 50 percent in 2022.","More cyclists were counted in 2022."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$240/800 = 30%; 450/900 = 50%.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-656$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A forest ranger recorded the number of tree seedlings per acre in two areas: burned area, 1,200 in the first year and 3,600 three years later; unburned area, 800 in the first year and 900 three years later. She claims that the burned area recovered faster.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports her claim?$q$, $q$["Seedlings in the burned area tripled from 1,200 to 3,600, while the unburned area increased only from 800 to 900.","Two areas were studied.","The unburned area had 900.","The burned area had 3,600 seedlings."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Tripling vs. a small increase.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-657$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$In a drug trial, 120 participants received the drug and 120 received a placebo. Symptoms improved in 84 of the drug group and 60 of the placebo group. A researcher claims that the drug was effective.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the claim?$q$, $q$["The trial included placebo.","60 participants in the placebo group improved.","70 percent of the drug group improved, compared with 50 percent of the placebo group.","120 participants received the drug."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$84/120 = 70%; 60/120 = 50%.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-658$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A table shows the average commute time (minutes) in four cities: Alder 24, Birch 31, Cedar 18, Dover 27. A student claims that the difference between the longest and shortest commute is 13 minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice is best supported by the data?$q$, $q$["Dover's commute is the longest.","Alder's commute is the shortest.","Cedar's commute is the longest.","Birch's 31-minute commute exceeds Cedar's 18-minute commute by 13 minutes."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$31 − 18 = 13.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-659$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A bar graph shows that Class A collected 45 cans, Class B collected 60 cans, and Class C collected 52 cans. A teacher claims that Class B collected the most cans.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the claim?$q$, $q$["Class A collected 45 cans.","Class B collected 60 cans, more than Class A's 45 and Class C's 52.","Class C collected 52 cans.","Three classes participated."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$60 is the highest.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-660$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$The table shows monthly savings of a household: January $120, February $150, March $90, April $210. A budgeter claims that savings in April were more than double those in March.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the claim?$q$, $q$["Savings vary by month.","April's $210 is more than twice March's $90.","February saved $150.","January saved $120."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$2 × 90 = 180 < 210.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-661$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$An experiment recorded the percentage of germinated seeds at three soil temperatures: 10°C, 18%; 20°C, 74%; 30°C, 52%. A student claims that germination is greatest at moderate temperatures.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively supports the claim?$q$, $q$["Germination at 10°C was 18%.","Three temperatures were tested.","Germination at 30°C was 52%.","Germination peaked at 74% at 20°C, higher than at 10°C (18%) or 30°C (52%)."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The middle temperature has the highest rate.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-662$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A pollster found that 55 of 100 randomly selected voters in Town A and 330 of 600 randomly selected voters in Town B support a measure. An analyst claims that support is nearly the same in both towns.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the analyst's claim?$q$, $q$["Support is 55 percent in Town A and 55 percent in Town B.","Town B surveyed more voters.","Both towns hold elections.","Town A surveyed 100 voters."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$55/100 = 55% and 330/600 = 55%.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-663$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A coach records the number of points scored in five games: 12, 15, 18, 21, 24. The coach claims the player's scoring increased steadily.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the claim?$q$, $q$["The player scored 24 points in the last game.","The player scored 12 in the first game.","The player played five games.","The player's points rose by 3 in each successive game."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Each game adds 3 points.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-664$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$The farmer noticed that the corn near the irrigation ditch grew taller than the corn at the far edge of the field. The far edge receives water only when it rains.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The ditch contains fertilizer only.","The reliable water supply probably helped the corn near the ditch grow taller.","The far edge has better soil.","Rain makes corn shrink."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Reliable irrigation is the likely difference.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-665$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Whenever the lighthouse beam stops turning, the harbor master sends a repair crew. Last night the beam stopped.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The harbor master closed the harbor forever.","Ships were told to use the beam.","A repair crew was probably sent to the lighthouse.","The lighthouse was torn down."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The established practice implies a crew was sent.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-666$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$In a taste test, most participants preferred the juice labeled with a gold star over the identical juice labeled with a plain circle, though the two were poured from the same bottle.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["Participants disliked juice.","The juices tasted different.","The label seems to have influenced participants' preferences.","The gold star juice was fresher."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Identical juice with different preferences points to the label.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-667$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$A surveyor found that rivers in the valley run cloudy after heavy rain but clear during dry weeks. The valley's hillsides were recently cleared of trees.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["Soil washing from bare hillsides may be clouding the rivers after rain.","Trees make rivers cloudy.","The rivers are frozen.","Dry weather clouds the water."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Runoff from cleared slopes plausibly causes cloudiness.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-668$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$A study found that crops grown near a certain wetland suffered fewer pest outbreaks. Researchers also recorded larger populations of bats and dragonflies in the surrounding fields than in fields farther from water.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["Wetlands cause pests.","Dragonflies are pests.","Predators attracted by the wetland may help limit crop pests.","Bats eat only crops."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Bats and dragonflies eat insects, so they may suppress pests.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-669$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Archaeologists found that pottery in the northern settlement was decorated with spirals, while pottery in the southern settlement showed geometric lines. In the oldest layers, both settlements used spirals.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The settlements never had contact.","Spirals were invented later than lines.","The southern settlement likely adopted new decorative styles over time.","The northern settlement never made pottery."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both started with spirals; only the south changed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-670$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$A beekeeper noticed that her hives near the lavender field produced darker, stronger-tasting honey than hives placed near the orchard. Both sets of hives were the same age and size.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The flowers the bees visit probably influence the flavor of the honey.","Hive age determines honey color.","Lavender hives are larger.","Orchards produce no nectar."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since hive age and size were equal, the nectar source likely explains the difference.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-671$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Ornithologists observed that a species of warbler nests earlier each spring than it did forty years ago. Over the same period, the caterpillars on which the warblers feed have begun to hatch earlier as well.$q$, $q$Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The warblers no longer eat caterpillars.","Spring has become shorter.","Caterpillars now hatch later than before.","The warblers may be adjusting their nesting time to match the earlier caterpillar supply."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The parallel shift suggests the warblers track their food supply.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-672$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Many commuters assume that driving is always faster than cycling in the city. A recent experiment, however, sent riders on identical downtown routes by car, bike, and bus. For trips under three miles during rush hour, the cyclists arrived first.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Buses are the fastest option downtown.","Cycling is faster than driving on all trips.","Commuters dislike cycling.","For short downtown trips at rush hour, cycling can be faster than driving."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The experiment contradicts the assumption for short rush-hour trips.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-673$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$A table shows the average number of hours slept by students in three groups: Group 1, 6.1; Group 2, 7.4; Group 3, 8.2. A health teacher claims that Group 3 slept the most.$q$, $q$Which choice best supports the teacher's claim?$q$, $q$["Group 2 averaged 7.4 hours.","Group 3 averaged 8.2 hours, more than Group 1's 6.1 or Group 2's 7.4.","Group 1 averaged 6.1 hours.","Three groups were compared."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$8.2 is the largest average.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-674$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A student claims that the author found the hike rewarding despite its difficulty. The author writes: 'My legs ached and my lungs burned, but when the clouds parted at the summit, I forgot every step it had cost me.'$q$, $q$Which quotation best supports the student's claim?$q$, $q$["'We began the hike at dawn.'","'My legs ached and my lungs burned, but when the clouds parted at the summit, I forgot every step it had cost me.'","'The trail was marked with orange flags.'","'I packed two liters of water.'"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The quotation acknowledges difficulty but emphasizes the reward.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-675$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Archaeologists excavating a Roman villa in England found coins from many different parts of the Roman Empire, including Egypt and Syria. They also found pottery made in Spain and glass made in Italy. These findings suggest that the villa's residents ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["had access to goods from distant parts of the empire.","were born in Egypt or Syria before moving to England.","made all of their own pottery and glass at the villa.","rarely used money to pay for the goods they needed."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Coins and goods from faraway regions suggest trade connections. Owning such coins doesn't mean the residents were born there.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-676$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$The Japanese art of kintsugi repairs broken pottery with lacquer mixed with gold. Rather than hiding the cracks, kintsugi highlights them, turning the repair into part of the object's beauty. Many people see the practice as a reminder that damage and history can add value rather than take it away.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Kintsugi is a method of repairing pottery so that its cracks are hidden.","Kintsugi was once common in Japan but is no longer practiced there today.","Kintsugi uses lacquer mixed with gold to make new pottery from scratch.","Kintsugi treats the repair of broken pottery as adding beauty and meaning."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text explains that kintsugi highlights cracks and gives them value. It doesn't hide them.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-677$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$A study found that people who kept a gratitude journal, writing down three things they were thankful for each day, reported feeling happier after a month than people who did not keep one. The study suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["keeping any kind of daily journal can be harmful.","writing every day makes most people feel tired.","focusing on positive things may improve mood.","people who are already happy never keep journals."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Journal keepers felt happier, suggesting that focusing on gratitude may lift mood.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-678$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A literary critic argues that in the novel The Orchard, the narrator's feelings about her hometown change from resentment to affection over the course of the story.$q$, $q$Which pair of quotations from the novel would best support the critic's argument?$q$, $q$["Early: \"I counted the days until I could leave this dull town behind.\" Late: \"Only now do I see how much this place has given me.\"","Early: \"My grandfather planted the orchard the year he arrived.\" Late: \"The apple trees bloomed in May.\"","Early: \"I loved the town's quiet streets as a girl.\" Late: \"I still love its quiet streets.\"","Early: \"I hated the long, gray winters here.\" Late: \"I hate those winters still.\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The critic claims a shift from resentment to affection. Only one pair shows dislike early and appreciation late.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-679$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Riding a bike to school has many benefits. It gives students exercise, cuts down on car traffic near schools, and can be faster than waiting for a bus.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Biking to school is dangerous.","All students should be required to bike to school.","Buses are always slower than bikes.","Biking to school offers several advantages."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text lists benefits of biking to school. It doesn't say biking should be required.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-680$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Weekly Hours of Physical Activity and Reported Mood</caption><thead><tr><th>Hours of activity</th><th>Students reporting good mood (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0–1</td><td>48</td></tr><tr><td>2–3</td><td>61</td></tr><tr><td>4–5</td><td>72</td></tr><tr><td>6 or more</td><td>74</td></tr></tbody></table>A health researcher noted that students who were more active generally reported good moods more often, although the increase leveled off at the highest activity levels.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the researcher's note?$q$, $q$["74% of students with 6 or more hours of activity reported good moods.","Good moods rose from 48% to 61% to 72% with more activity, then only to 74%.","More students with 4–5 hours reported good moods than those with 2–3 hours.","Students with 0–1 hours of activity were least likely to report good moods."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The note has two parts: an overall rise and a leveling off. Only one choice shows both the steady increase and the small final gain.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-681$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Library Programs: Average Attendance</caption><thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Before advertising</th><th>After advertising</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Story time</td><td>18</td><td>21</td></tr><tr><td>Coding club</td><td>9</td><td>27</td></tr><tr><td>Book club</td><td>12</td><td>14</td></tr></tbody></table>The library's director argues that advertising had the greatest impact on the coding club.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the director's argument?$q$, $q$["Coding club attendance tripled, from 9 to 27, while others rose by 2 or 3.","Coding club attendance after advertising was 27, the highest of any program.","Book club attendance rose from 12 to 14 people after the advertising began.","Story time had the highest average attendance before the advertising began."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The greatest impact is shown by comparing the coding club's increase with the other programs' smaller increases.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-682$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Economists once believed that people always make rational decisions about money. But psychologist Daniel Kahneman showed that people often rely on mental shortcuts that lead to predictable errors. For example, people tend to fear losing $100 more than they value gaining $100, even though the amounts are the same.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Economists have always understood that people make irrational choices about money.","People tend to value gaining $100 more than they fear losing the same amount.","Daniel Kahneman was an economist whose work confirmed that people act rationally.","Research has shown that predictable biases often shape people's financial decisions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text contrasts the old belief in rational decisions with Kahneman's findings about predictable errors. The example reverses one of the wrong choices.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-683$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$An art historian claims that the painter Kerry James Marshall deliberately includes Black figures in scenes traditionally dominated by white subjects in Western art.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the art historian's claim?$q$, $q$["Marshall, who was born in Alabama in 1955, grew up partly in Los Angeles.","Marshall has said he paints Black figures in classic scenes to correct their absence from art history.","Marshall's paintings are often very large, some more than ten feet wide.","Many major museums have purchased Marshall's paintings for their collections."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Marshall's own statement of intent directly supports the claim that the choice is deliberate.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-684$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$The city recently replaced its old streetlights with LED lights. The new lights use about half as much electricity and last much longer. City officials expect the change to save millions of dollars over the next ten years.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["City officials had originally planned to remove all the streetlights.","The city's new LED streetlights should save energy and money.","The new LED lights cost more to run than the city's old lights.","The city's new LED streetlights are much brighter than the old ones."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes LED lights' lower energy use and expected savings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-685$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Many cities have started planting "pocket forests"—small, dense patches of native trees, sometimes no larger than a tennis court. Because the trees are planted close together, they grow quickly as they compete for light. Within a few years, these tiny forests can provide shade, absorb rainwater, and attract birds and insects.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Trees grow more slowly when they are planted close together in small spaces.","Pocket forests are usually much larger than tennis courts and take decades to grow.","Pocket forests are planted mainly to provide wood for nearby city residents.","Small, dense urban forests can quickly provide several environmental benefits."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes pocket forests and the benefits they provide within a few years.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-686$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$Biologists hypothesize that the bright red patches on the wings of male red-winged blackbirds help them defend their territories.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the biologists' hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Female red-winged blackbirds do not have the red wing patches that males have.","Red-winged blackbirds are found in marshes throughout most of North America.","Males with bright red patches sing more often in the spring than in the fall.","Males whose red patches were dyed black lost their territories far more often."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Covering the patches led to territory loss, which directly ties the patches to territorial defense.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-687$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A researcher claims that drinking water before meals can help people eat less.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Lunch is often the busiest meal of the day at restaurants in large cities.","Drinking enough water each day is important for keeping the body hydrated.","People who drank two glasses of water before lunch ate 13 percent fewer calories.","Many people say they prefer drinking cold water to warm water with meals."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the finding about eating fewer calories after drinking water supports the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-688$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Honeybee colonies make decisions as a group. When a colony needs a new home, scout bees inspect possible sites and return to perform dances advertising them. Other scouts visit the sites, and over time more bees dance for the best location. Once enough scouts favor one site, the swarm moves there.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Honeybee colonies choose new homes through a collective process.","A single queen bee decides where the colony will move next.","Honeybee colonies prefer to stay in the same home for their whole lives.","Scout bees never return to a site that another scout has visited."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text explains how many scouts gradually reach agreement, a group decision.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-689$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A student argues that the author of an essay values hard work.$q$, $q$Which quotation from the essay would best support the student's argument?$q$, $q$["\"Nothing I am proud of came easily; every success took long hours.\"","\"I enjoy traveling to new places and meeting people from other cultures.\"","\"My favorite season has always been autumn, when the leaves change.\"","\"I grew up in a small town in Ohio, the youngest of four children.\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the quotation about success built on long hours expresses the value of hard work.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-690$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Survival of Seedlings After One Year (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Seedling type</th><th>Watered weekly</th><th>Not watered</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oak</td><td>82</td><td>41</td></tr><tr><td>Maple</td><td>76</td><td>52</td></tr><tr><td>Pine</td><td>88</td><td>79</td></tr></tbody></table>A forester concluded that pine seedlings are less dependent on weekly watering than oak or maple seedlings are.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the forester's conclusion?$q$, $q$["Oak seedlings that were watered weekly had a survival rate of 82%.","Without watering, pine survival fell only 9 points, but oak fell 41 and maple 24.","Pine seedlings had the highest survival rate when watered weekly, at 88%.","Unwatered maple seedlings survived at a higher rate (52%) than unwatered oaks (41%)."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The conclusion is about dependence on watering, which is shown by how much survival drops without water. Pine's small drop compared with the others' large drops supports it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-691$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$An ecologist claims that a new fence is keeping deer out of the forest preserve.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the ecologist's claim?$q$, $q$["The fence around the preserve is eight feet tall and made of strong wire.","Deer are common throughout the region, including in towns near the preserve.","Cameras recorded 200 deer in the preserve before the fence was built and 12 after.","The preserve is home to many kinds of birds as well as foxes and rabbits."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sharp drop in deer after the fence was built supports the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-692$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Commute Time (minutes)</caption><thead><tr><th>City</th><th>2010</th><th>2020</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>City P</td><td>24</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>City Q</td><td>28</td><td>27</td></tr><tr><td>City R</td><td>22</td><td>29</td></tr></tbody></table>A transportation analyst noted that, of the three cities, only City Q saw its average commute time decrease from 2010 to 2020.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the analyst's statement?$q$, $q$["City Q had the longest average commute of the three cities in 2010, at 28 minutes.","City R's average commute rose by 7 minutes, from 22 minutes to 29 minutes.","City P had the longest average commute of the three cities in 2020, at 31 minutes.","City Q's commute fell from 28 to 27 minutes, while P's and R's each rose by 7."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$To show that only City Q decreased, the data must show City Q falling and both other cities rising.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-693$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Moths are drawn to artificial lights at night. Ecologists recently found that moth populations near brightly lit highways have declined more than populations in darker rural areas. Because many plants rely on moths for pollination, these findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["plants near highways produce more seeds than plants elsewhere.","moths prefer to live near highways.","plants near brightly lit areas may be pollinated less often.","artificial lights help moths find flowers."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Fewer moths near bright lights means fewer pollinators, so plants there may be pollinated less.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-694$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Arctic terns fly from the Arctic to Antarctica every year. When it is winter in the Arctic, it is summer in Antarctica. Terns can therefore ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["experience two summers each year.","avoid flying over oceans.","stay in the Arctic all year.","live only in cold winter weather."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Flying between the poles lets terns follow summer, so they experience two summers a year.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-695$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A scientist claims that a new fertilizer helps tomato plants produce more fruit.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the scientist's claim?$q$, $q$["The new fertilizer costs less than most other brands sold in garden stores.","The new fertilizer is made from recycled materials and is safe for home gardens.","Tomato plants need plenty of sunlight and regular watering to produce fruit.","Plants given the new fertilizer produced 40 percent more tomatoes than unfertilized plants."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the finding about the number of tomatoes addresses fruit production.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-696$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Although deserts receive little rain, many desert plants have adapted to make the most of it. Some cacti have shallow, widespread roots that quickly absorb water after a storm. Others, like mesquite trees, grow roots more than 100 feet deep to reach underground water.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Mesquite trees can grow only near rivers, where water is plentiful.","Desert plants have adapted so that they no longer need water to survive.","Desert plants have developed different root systems to obtain scarce water.","All desert plants grow deep roots to reach water far underground."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text gives two contrasting root strategies that help plants get water in deserts.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-697$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Hours Volunteered per Month</caption><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Before program</th><th>After program</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Students</td><td>3.2</td><td>6.5</td></tr><tr><td>Retirees</td><td>8.4</td><td>8.9</td></tr><tr><td>Working adults</td><td>2.1</td><td>2.4</td></tr></tbody></table>Organizers of a volunteering program concluded that the program was especially successful at increasing volunteering among students.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the organizers' conclusion?$q$, $q$["Retirees volunteered more hours than any other group both before and after.","Students volunteered 6.5 hours per month after the program was introduced.","Students doubled their hours, from 3.2 to 6.5, while others rose by under an hour.","Working adults volunteered 2.4 hours a month after the program, up from 2.1."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$To show the program worked especially well for students, their gain must be compared with the smaller gains of other groups.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-698$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A researcher suggests that cats can recognize their owners' voices.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researcher's suggestion?$q$, $q$["Cats reacted more to their owners' voices than to strangers' voices.","Cats can see well in dim light, which helps them hunt at night.","Some cats meow much more often than others, especially when they are hungry.","Cats sleep for about 15 hours a day and are most active at dawn and dusk."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A stronger reaction to owners' voices than to strangers' suggests recognition.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-699$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$For many years, scientists assumed that the deep ocean floor was nearly lifeless, since sunlight cannot reach it. Then, in 1977, researchers exploring hydrothermal vents near the Galápagos Islands discovered thriving communities of giant tube worms, clams, and crabs. These animals depend not on sunlight but on bacteria that convert chemicals from the vents into energy.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["A discovery showed that deep-sea life can thrive on chemical energy instead of sunlight.","Giant tube worms are the largest animals that live anywhere in the deep ocean.","Sunlight reaches the deep ocean floor near the hydrothermal vents of the Galápagos.","Most scientists still believe that the deep ocean floor is nearly lifeless."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes how the vent communities overturned the idea of a lifeless deep sea and explains their chemical energy source.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-700$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$The following text is from a 1904 short story. When Mr. Pell entered the shop, the clerk straightened his tie, hid his newspaper, and hurried to greet him. When other customers came in, the clerk barely looked up. It can most reasonably be inferred that the clerk ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["regards Mr. Pell as more important than other customers.","dislikes his job at the shop and wants to leave it.","has never seen Mr. Pell in the shop before that day.","is a close friend of Mr. Pell's from outside the shop."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clerk's special attention to Mr. Pell, compared with his indifference to others, shows he considers Mr. Pell important. Friendship isn't indicated.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-701$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$A museum's records show that visits to its dinosaur exhibit doubled after a popular dinosaur movie came out. Visits to its other exhibits stayed about the same. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the movie may have increased interest in the dinosaur exhibit.","the museum closed its other exhibits after the movie came out.","visitors stopped coming to the museum's other exhibits entirely.","the dinosaur movie was filmed inside the museum's exhibit."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the dinosaur exhibit's visits rose after the movie, suggesting the movie sparked interest in it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-702$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$When wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone National Park in 1995, they did more than reduce the elk population. Elk began avoiding open valleys where they were easy targets, allowing willow and aspen trees to regrow along streams. The trees, in turn, provided habitat for beavers and songbirds.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The return of wolves to Yellowstone set off a chain of ecosystem changes.","Beavers returned to Yellowstone first and caused the streamside trees to regrow.","Elk populations in Yellowstone increased after the wolves were brought back.","Wolves were removed from Yellowstone in 1995 to protect the elk population."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text traces a chain of effects from wolves to elk behavior to trees to other animals.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-703$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Astronomers have observed that a distant star dims slightly at regular intervals every 12 days. Each dimming lasts a few hours. One explanation is that a planet is orbiting the star. If this explanation is correct, the planet most likely ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["orbits the star once every few hours rather than every 12 days.","gives off its own light, which outshines the star at times.","is larger than the star itself and blocks all of its light.","passes in front of the star once every 12 days."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Regular dimming every 12 days suggests a planet blocking some starlight on each 12-day orbit. The few hours are the length of each pass, not the orbit.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-704$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$For years, the empty lot on Pine Street was full of trash. Then a group of neighbors cleaned it up and planted vegetables. Now the lot is a community garden where families grow tomatoes, beans, and peppers.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Neighbors turned an empty lot into a community garden.","The city plans to build new houses on the lot on Pine Street.","Tomatoes grow more easily in city gardens than beans or peppers.","Most families on Pine Street were not interested in gardening."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes the lot's transformation from a trash-filled space to a garden.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-705$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$The following text is adapted from a 1911 novel. Mrs. Wilcox walked slowly across the lawn, stopping now and then to touch a flower or straighten a fallen branch. She did not seem to be in a hurry to go anywhere. To her, the garden was not a place to pass through but a place to belong.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Mrs. Wilcox is a professional gardener.","Mrs. Wilcox is looking for something she lost in the garden.","Mrs. Wilcox has a deep, unhurried connection to the garden.","Mrs. Wilcox dislikes spending time outdoors."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Her slow movements and the final sentence ("a place to belong") show her close bond with the garden.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-706$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Writer Lin-Manuel Miranda was inspired to create the musical Hamilton after reading a biography of Alexander Hamilton on vacation. He saw in Hamilton's story—an immigrant who rose through ambition and writing—themes that fit naturally with hip-hop, a genre often centered on personal struggle and wordplay.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Miranda wrote a biography of Alexander Hamilton before creating his musical.","Hip-hop rarely deals with themes of personal struggle, ambition, or wordplay.","Miranda saw parallels between Hamilton's life and hip-hop that inspired him.","Alexander Hamilton was a musician who wrote songs about his own life."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text explains that Miranda connected Hamilton's story to hip-hop's themes.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-707$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$The following text is from a short story. When the phone rang, Grandpa leaped from his chair faster than anyone had seen him move in years. "That must be Lucy," he said, grinning. Lucy was his granddaughter, who had moved overseas. It can most reasonably be inferred that Grandpa ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["was very eager to hear from Lucy.","was annoyed by the phone ringing.","did not want to talk to Lucy.","was expecting a call from his doctor."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Leaping up quickly and grinning shows his excitement about hearing from Lucy.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-708$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Lemonade Stand Sales</caption><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Cups sold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Friday</td><td>35</td></tr><tr><td>Saturday</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>Sunday</td><td>64</td></tr></tbody></table>The students noticed that they sold more than twice as many cups on Saturday as on Friday.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support this observation?$q$, $q$["The students sold more cups on Sunday than on Friday.","The students sold 80 cups on Saturday and 35 cups on Friday.","The students sold 80 cups on Saturday and 64 cups on Sunday.","The students sold 64 cups on Sunday."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$80 is more than twice 35, so only the choice comparing Saturday with Friday supports the observation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-709$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$In a study, two groups of shoppers were shown the same jar of jam. One group was told the jam was made by a small family farm; the other was told nothing about its origin. Shoppers in the first group said they would pay about 20 percent more for the jam. The study suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["shoppers always prefer to buy the cheapest product on the shelf.","information about a product's origin can affect how people value it.","jam made on family farms tastes better than jam made by companies.","most shoppers do not read the labels on the products they buy."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The jam was the same, so the difference in willingness to pay must come from the origin information. Nothing shows the taste differed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-710$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A student claims that the short story's narrator distrusts his neighbor.$q$, $q$Which quotation from the story would best support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["\"Mr. Grady often worked in his garden on Saturday mornings.\"","\"I lived on Elm Street for ten years, two doors down from Mr. Grady.\"","\"Mr. Grady's house, painted bright yellow, stood right across from mine.\"","\"Whenever Mr. Grady smiled at me, I checked to make sure my wallet was still in my pocket.\""]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Checking his wallet whenever the neighbor smiles shows the narrator's distrust.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-711$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$In winter, Arctic foxes grow thick white fur that helps them stay warm and blend in with the snow. In summer, their fur turns brown or gray to match the rocks and plants around them.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The Arctic fox's fur changes with the seasons to help it survive.","Arctic foxes keep the same white fur throughout the year.","Arctic foxes hunt only during the winter, when their fur is white.","Arctic foxes live mainly in warm places with little snow."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text describes how the fox's fur changes color and thickness to match each season.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-712$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$Some anthropologists argue that early humans in East Africa traded with distant groups as long as 300,000 years ago.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support this argument?$q$, $q$["Tools from a 300,000-year-old Kenyan site were made of obsidian from over 50 miles away.","Obsidian is a type of volcanic glass that breaks into pieces with sharp edges.","Early humans in East Africa used sharp stone tools for cutting meat and plants.","Some 300,000-year-old sites in Kenya contain animal bones along with tools."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Tools made from distant materials suggest exchange with faraway groups.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-713$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Sculptor Edmonia Lewis, born in 1844 to an Ojibwe mother and a Haitian father, moved to Rome in 1865 to pursue her art. At the time, few women—and even fewer women of color—were able to work as professional sculptors. Lewis not only succeeded but earned international recognition, with works such as The Death of Cleopatra drawing large crowds at the 1876 Centennial Exhibition in Philadelphia.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Edmonia Lewis overcame major barriers to win international fame as a sculptor.","Edmonia Lewis was the first sculptor of any kind to work professionally in Rome.","The Death of Cleopatra was the only sculpture that Lewis ever exhibited publicly.","Most professional sculptors working in the 1860s were women of color like Lewis."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text emphasizes the obstacles Lewis faced and her international success. The other choices misstate details.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-714$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A historian claims that the town grew quickly after the railroad arrived in 1870.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian's claim?$q$, $q$["The town's railroad station, built in 1870, was made of red brick.","Many other towns in the region also had railroads by the 1870s.","The town's population rose from about 800 in 1870 to more than 6,000 in 1880.","The town's first school opened in 1850, twenty years before the railroad."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A large population increase after 1870 directly supports rapid growth.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-715$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Most volcanic eruptions release ash that blocks some sunlight. After the 1991 eruption of Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines, average global temperatures dropped by about 1°F over the next two years before returning to earlier levels. This suggests that the eruption's effect on climate was ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["to raise global temperatures.","limited to the Philippines.","permanent.","significant but temporary."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Temperatures fell worldwide and then returned to earlier levels, so the effect was real but short-lived.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-716$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Before the transcontinental railroad was completed in 1869, traveling from New York to San Francisco could take months by wagon or ship. Afterward, the same journey took about a week. The railroad also made it far cheaper to ship goods across the country, helping businesses reach new markets.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The transcontinental railroad greatly cut travel times and shipping costs.","Few people chose to travel on the transcontinental railroad after 1869.","The railroad made the trip from New York to San Francisco take months.","The transcontinental railroad was completed well before the year 1860."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text contrasts months of travel before with a week after and notes cheaper shipping.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-717$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$The axolotl, a type of salamander from Mexico, can regrow lost body parts. If it loses a leg, it can grow a new one in a few weeks. It can even repair parts of its heart and brain.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The axolotl loses its legs more often than other salamanders do.","The axolotl has a remarkable ability to regrow parts of its body.","The axolotl's brain is larger than the brains of most other salamanders.","The axolotl is the only kind of salamander that is native to Mexico."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Every detail in the text describes the axolotl's ability to regrow body parts.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-718$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Daily Screen Time (hours)</caption><thead><tr><th>Age group</th><th>Weekdays</th><th>Weekends</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8–12</td><td>4.4</td><td>6.1</td></tr><tr><td>13–18</td><td>7.2</td><td>8.9</td></tr></tbody></table>A researcher noted that both age groups spent more time on screens on weekends than on weekdays.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the researcher's note?$q$, $q$["Teens ages 13–18 averaged 8.9 hours on weekends, more than any other group.","Children ages 8–12 averaged 4.4 hours on weekdays, the least of any group.","Ages 8–12 rose from 4.4 to 6.1 hours on weekends, and teens from 7.2 to 8.9.","Teens spent more time on screens on weekdays than children did on weekends."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The note is about both age groups, so the data must show weekend time exceeding weekday time for each group.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-719$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Many people assume that introverts dislike being around others. But researchers say introversion is better understood as a preference for quieter, less stimulating settings. Introverts may enjoy socializing, but they often need time alone afterward to recharge.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Introverts recharge after socializing by spending more time in crowds.","Introverts dislike being around others and never enjoy socializing.","Most people are introverts who need time alone after socializing.","Introversion is a preference for less stimulation, not a dislike of people."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text corrects the assumption about introverts and redefines introversion as a preference for quiet settings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-720$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$The following text is adapted from a 1915 novel. Agnes folded the letter carefully and placed it in the drawer with the others, all unopened. She had not opened a letter from her brother in three years, yet she had not thrown a single one away. It can most reasonably be inferred that Agnes ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["has forgotten that she has a brother at all.","reads each letter as soon as it arrives.","has conflicted feelings about her brother.","wants her brother to stop writing to her."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Not opening the letters suggests distance, but keeping them all suggests she still cares, so her feelings are mixed. Nothing shows she wants him to stop.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-721$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Weight of Newborn Sea Turtles (grams)</caption><thead><tr><th>Nest temperature</th><th>Weight</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>27°C</td><td>24.1</td></tr><tr><td>29°C</td><td>22.8</td></tr><tr><td>31°C</td><td>20.5</td></tr></tbody></table>Researchers studying sea turtle nests found that hatchlings from warmer nests tended to be ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["lighter, with average weights falling from 24.1 grams to 20.5 grams.","lighter at 29°C than at 31°C, though heaviest of all at 27°C.","heavier, with average weights rising from 20.5 grams to 24.1 grams.","about the same weight, averaging close to 22.8 grams at each temperature."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Weight decreases as temperature rises, from 24.1 to 22.8 to 20.5 grams.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-722$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$For centuries, the origins of the Stonehenge bluestones puzzled researchers, since the nearest similar rock is about 150 miles away in Wales. Using chemical analysis, geologists recently matched several bluestones to specific outcrops in the Preseli Hills, confirming that the stones were transported over a remarkable distance.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The bluestones used at Stonehenge were quarried from rock near the site itself.","Researchers still have no idea where the Stonehenge bluestones came from.","Chemical analysis confirmed that some Stonehenge stones came from distant Wales.","Stonehenge was originally built in Wales and later moved to its current site."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes how geologists traced the bluestones to Wales, resolving a long-standing question.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-723$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Annual Snowfall (inches)</caption><thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>1990s</th><th>2010s</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Alder</td><td>62</td><td>48</td></tr><tr><td>Birch</td><td>55</td><td>51</td></tr><tr><td>Cedar</td><td>70</td><td>44</td></tr></tbody></table>A climate scientist observed that snowfall declined in all three towns but declined most sharply in Cedar.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the scientist's observation?$q$, $q$["Cedar had the most snowfall of the three towns in the 1990s, at 70 inches.","Snowfall fell 26 inches in Cedar, compared with 14 in Alder and 4 in Birch.","Birch had the most snowfall of the three towns in the 2010s, at 51 inches.","Alder's snowfall fell from 62 inches in the 1990s to 48 in the 2010s."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the choice comparing the declines in all three towns shows that each declined and Cedar declined most.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-724$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rate of Seeds (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Storage condition</th><th>After 1 year</th><th>After 5 years</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Room temperature</td><td>91</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>Refrigerated</td><td>94</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>Frozen</td><td>93</td><td>89</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist claims that freezing preserves seeds' ability to sprout better over time than the other storage methods tested.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the botanist's claim?$q$, $q$["All three groups of seeds had germination rates above 90% after one year of storage.","After one year, refrigerated seeds had the highest germination rate of the three groups, at 94%.","Seeds stored at room temperature still germinated at 91% after one year of storage.","After five years, frozen seeds germinated at 89%, versus 80% refrigerated and 38% at room temperature."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The claim is about preservation over time, so the five-year results are key. Frozen seeds had the highest rate after five years.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-725$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Some species of ants grow fungus gardens, feeding them leaves and eating the fungus that grows. Scientists have found that the ants also carry bacteria on their bodies that produce chemicals harmful to molds that attack the fungus. The bacteria most likely help the ants by ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["protecting their food supply from harmful molds.","making the fungus grow without needing any leaves.","helping the ants digest leaves without the fungus.","attracting more ants from other colonies to theirs."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bacteria harm molds that attack the fungus, which the ants eat, so they protect the ants' food.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-726$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A student argues that the novel's main character, Ella, is brave.$q$, $q$Which quotation from the novel would best support the student's argument?$q$, $q$["\"Ella's house stood alone at the end of a long, quiet road outside of town.\"","\"Ella's favorite color was blue, the color of the lake behind her house.\"","\"Though her hands shook, Ella stepped into the dark cave to find her lost brother.\"","\"Ella liked to sit by the fire in the evening and read her favorite books.\""]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Entering a dark cave despite fear shows bravery. The other quotations describe unrelated details.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-727$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$In the 1960s, many experts predicted that global food production would not keep up with population growth, leading to widespread famine. Instead, new crop varieties, fertilizers, and irrigation methods—often called the Green Revolution—dramatically increased harvests. Between 1960 and 2000, wheat yields in developing countries roughly tripled.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Population growth slowed sharply after the 1960s, reducing the demand for food.","Wheat was the only crop whose yields were affected by the Green Revolution.","Widespread famine occurred in the 1960s, just as experts had predicted.","Agricultural advances allowed food production to exceed earlier predictions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text contrasts the famine predictions with the large production increases from the Green Revolution.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-728$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$On Monday, the river near the town was calm and low. After three days of heavy rain upstream, the river rose over its banks and flooded several roads. The flooding was most likely caused by ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["a long drought in the region.","the heavy rain that fell upstream.","people building new roads nearby.","the river freezing over that week."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The river rose after days of heavy upstream rain, so the rain likely caused the flooding.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-729$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Bird Species Recorded at Three Wetland Sites</caption><thead><tr><th>Site</th><th>Before restoration</th><th>After restoration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Site 1</td><td>34</td><td>51</td></tr><tr><td>Site 2</td><td>29</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Site 3</td><td>41</td><td>63</td></tr></tbody></table>An ecologist claims that restoration had the largest effect on bird diversity at Site 3.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the ecologist's claim?$q$, $q$["Site 3 had the most species before restoration, with 41 species.","Site 2 had 30 species after restoration, up from 29 species before.","Site 3 gained 22 species, compared with 17 at Site 1 and 1 at Site 2.","Site 1's number of species rose by 17, from 34 to 51 species."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The largest effect is the largest increase. Site 3's gain of 22 is the biggest. Having the most species beforehand doesn't show the effect of restoration.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-730$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Coffee was first grown in Ethiopia. From there, it spread to Yemen, where people began roasting and brewing the beans. By the 1600s, coffeehouses had opened across the Middle East and Europe.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["People in Yemen grew coffee beans but did not drink coffee.","Coffeehouses opened in the Middle East but were banned in Europe.","Coffee spread from Ethiopia to many parts of the world over time.","Coffee was first grown in Europe and spread to Ethiopia in the 1600s."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text traces coffee's spread from Ethiopia through Yemen to the Middle East and Europe.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-731$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$A city installed speed cameras on ten streets. Over the following year, accidents on those streets fell by 30 percent. However, accidents also fell by 25 percent on similar streets without cameras during the same period. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the cameras alone caused the entire 30 percent drop in accidents.","factors other than the cameras may explain much of the drop.","the cameras caused accidents to increase on nearby streets.","accidents actually rose throughout the city during that year."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Since accidents fell nearly as much without cameras, much of the decline likely came from other causes.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-732$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Hours of Sleep per Night</caption><thead><tr><th>Age group</th><th>Hours</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6–12 years</td><td>9.5</td></tr><tr><td>13–18 years</td><td>8.1</td></tr><tr><td>19–30 years</td><td>7.3</td></tr></tbody></table>A researcher notes that, in the study, average sleep decreased as age increased.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the researcher's statement?$q$, $q$["Children ages 6–12 averaged 9.5 hours, the most of any age group.","Sleep fell from 9.5 hours (ages 6–12) to 8.1 (13–18) to 7.3 (19–30).","Adults ages 19–30 averaged 7.3 hours of sleep, less than 8 hours.","Teens ages 13–18 averaged 8.1 hours of sleep per night in the study."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the choice showing all three groups demonstrates the decrease across ages.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-733$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A city planner claims that adding more trees to a neighborhood can make it cooler in the summer.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the planner's claim?$q$, $q$["Some residents of the neighborhood dislike raking the trees' leaves in the fall.","Streets with many trees were 5°F cooler on summer afternoons than streets with few trees.","The neighborhood's tree-lined streets include many houses built before 1950.","Trees on the neighborhood's streets provide homes for many species of birds."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the temperature comparison connects trees with cooler summer conditions.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-734$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Marie Curie was the first person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences: physics in 1903 and chemistry in 1911. Her research on radioactivity changed both fields and led to new medical treatments.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Marie Curie was the only scientist of her time to study radioactivity.","Marie Curie won Nobel Prizes in physics and in medicine.","Marie Curie's research on radioactivity had little effect on medicine.","Marie Curie's research won her Nobel Prizes in two sciences."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text highlights her two Nobel Prizes and the impact of her research.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-735$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$When the town library started lending tools, residents were surprised by how popular the program became. Within a year, people had borrowed drills, ladders, and garden tools more than 3,000 times. Many said the program saved them money and helped them finish home projects.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The library stopped lending books so that it would have room to store tools.","Before the program, residents had rarely been able to finish home projects.","The library's tool-lending program became popular and useful to residents.","Drills were borrowed more often than any other tool the library offered."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes how often tools were borrowed and how residents benefited. The other choices aren't stated.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-736$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$In many cities, the number of people working from home rose sharply after 2020. During the same period, sales at downtown lunch restaurants fell, while sales at restaurants in residential neighborhoods increased. These trends suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["some lunch spending may have moved from downtown to residential areas.","people who work from home prefer to cook every meal themselves.","people in these cities stopped eating lunch at restaurants after 2020.","downtown restaurants lost customers because of poor food quality."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With fewer people downtown and more at home, lunch spending likely moved to residential areas. The rising neighborhood sales rule out the other options.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-737$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Animals Seen During a Park Survey</caption><thead><tr><th>Animal</th><th>Number seen</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deer</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>Rabbits</td><td>42</td></tr><tr><td>Foxes</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Turkeys</td><td>23</td></tr></tbody></table>Park rangers reported that foxes were the least commonly seen animal in the survey.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the rangers' report?$q$, $q$["18 deer were seen, which was fewer than the number of turkeys.","More turkeys (23) were seen than deer (18) during the survey.","Only 5 foxes were seen, fewer than any other animal listed.","42 rabbits were seen, more than any other animal in the survey."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$5 is the smallest number in the table.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-738$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Hummingbirds have the highest metabolic rate of any bird, burning energy extremely quickly. At night, many hummingbirds enter a state called torpor, in which their body temperature and heart rate drop dramatically. Torpor most likely helps hummingbirds ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["fly faster during the day while they are feeding.","stay cool when the weather is very hot.","find more flowers to feed on during the night.","conserve energy when they cannot feed."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Burning energy quickly and being unable to feed at night means they need to save energy; lowering heart rate and temperature does that.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-739$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Books Read by Students in One Month</caption><thead><tr><th>Student</th><th>Books read</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ana</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Ben</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Chloe</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>David</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table>A teacher noticed that one student read more books than any other student in the class last month.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to identify that student?$q$, $q$["Chloe, who read 8 books","Ana, who read 6 books","David, who read 4 books","Ben, who read 3 books"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Chloe's 8 books is the highest number in the table.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-740$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$A survey of a city's residents found that people who lived within a 10-minute walk of a park visited parks about four times as often as people who lived farther away. City planners who want more residents to use parks would therefore most likely ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["consider building parks in neighborhoods that lack them.","build all new parks at the edges of the city.","close the parks that are farthest from residents' homes.","reduce the number of sidewalks that lead to existing parks."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If closeness increases park visits, building parks near more homes would increase use.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-741$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Votes for School Mascot</caption><thead><tr><th>Mascot</th><th>Votes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hawks</td><td>145</td></tr><tr><td>Tigers</td><td>212</td></tr><tr><td>Wolves</td><td>198</td></tr></tbody></table>The student council announced that the Tigers won the mascot vote.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the announcement?$q$, $q$["The Tigers got 212 votes, more than the Hawks (145) or Wolves (198).","The Wolves and the Hawks together received more votes than the Tigers.","The Wolves received 198 votes, more than the Hawks received (145).","The Hawks received the fewest votes of the three mascots, with 145."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the choice comparing the Tigers' votes with both other mascots shows they won.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-742$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Length of Fish Species (centimeters)</caption><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Length</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Species A</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Species B</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Species C</td><td>18</td></tr></tbody></table>A biologist states that Species B is the longest of the three species.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the statement?$q$, $q$["Species C averages 18 centimeters, 6 more than Species A.","Species A averages 12 centimeters, the shortest of the three.","Species B averages 30 cm, longer than Species A (12) and C (18).","Species C, at 18 centimeters, is longer than Species A."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the choice comparing Species B with both others shows it is the longest.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-743$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A psychologist claims that people are more likely to remember information if they explain it to someone else.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the psychologist's claim?$q$, $q$["Teachers often have to explain the same material several times to a class.","Students who reviewed alone finished their review faster than those who taught.","Students who taught a lesson to a classmate scored higher a week later than solo reviewers.","Many students say that they prefer studying with friends to studying alone."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the comparison of test scores between explainers and solo reviewers supports the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-744$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$Researchers studying Atlantic puffins hypothesize that warming ocean temperatures have reduced the number of small fish the birds feed to their chicks, leading to lower chick survival rates.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' hypothesis?$q$, $q$["In warmer years, puffin parents brought fewer fish to their nests, and fewer chicks survived.","Puffin colonies are found on rocky islands throughout the North Atlantic Ocean.","Chick survival rates were similar in years with warm and cool surface waters.","Puffins can dive more than 200 feet below the surface to catch the fish they eat."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The hypothesis links warmer water, fewer fish, and lower chick survival. Only one finding connects all three. Similar survival rates in warm and cool years would weaken the hypothesis instead.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-745$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Pets Owned by Students in a Class</caption><thead><tr><th>Pet</th><th>Number of students</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dog</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Cat</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Fish</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Bird</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table>A student wants to show that dogs were the most common pet in the class.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Twelve students owned dogs, more than owned any other pet.","Four students owned fish, and two students owned birds.","More students owned cats (9) than owned fish (4).","Nine students owned cats, more than owned fish or birds."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Twelve is the largest number, so only that choice shows dogs were most common.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-746$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Yield of Corn by Planting Density (bushels per acre)</caption><thead><tr><th>Plants per acre</th><th>Field A</th><th>Field B</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>20,000</td><td>140</td><td>135</td></tr><tr><td>30,000</td><td>175</td><td>170</td></tr><tr><td>40,000</td><td>168</td><td>160</td></tr></tbody></table>An agronomist concluded that, in both fields, yield was highest at a density of 30,000 plants per acre.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the agronomist's conclusion?$q$, $q$["At 40,000 plants per acre, Field A yielded 168 bushels and Field B yielded 160.","Both fields yielded the most at 30,000 plants per acre: 175 and 170 bushels.","At 20,000 plants per acre, Field B yielded 135 bushels, its lowest yield.","Field A had a higher yield than Field B at every one of the three densities."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the choice showing that 30,000 produced the highest yield in both fields supports the conclusion.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-747$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Researchers studying an ancient settlement found large numbers of fish bones and fishing hooks in the oldest layers of soil. In the newer layers, fish bones were rare, while the bones of sheep and goats became common. These findings suggest that, over time, the settlement's residents ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["moved the settlement closer to the sea to fish more easily.","raised sheep and goats long before they began to fish.","shifted from relying mainly on fishing to raising animals.","stopped eating meat and turned to growing crops instead."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Fish remains dominate the older layers and livestock bones the newer ones, suggesting a shift from fishing to herding. The order of the layers also rules out the idea that herding came first.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-748$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$The library's new reading program offers prizes to students who read ten books over the summer. Last year, before the program existed, 40 students read ten books. This year, 115 students did. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["most students dislike reading during the summer months.","the library now has fewer books than it did last year.","the library plans to end the program after next year.","the prizes may have encouraged more students to read."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The number of students reading ten books rose sharply after prizes were offered, suggesting the program helped.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-749$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A team of economists claims that a city's new public transit pass, which offers unlimited rides for a flat monthly fee, has encouraged more residents to use buses and trains.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the economists' claim?$q$, $q$["Most residents who bought the pass already rode transit daily before it existed.","After the pass was introduced, ridership rose 18 percent while the population held steady.","The city added three new bus routes in the year before the pass was introduced.","The monthly pass costs less than a monthly parking permit in the downtown area."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A large rise in ridership without population growth supports the claim. Finding that pass buyers were already daily riders would suggest the pass didn't change behavior.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-750$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A student claims that in the play, the character Marcus hides his true feelings from his family.$q$, $q$Which quotation would best support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["Marcus tells his younger sister, \"Don't worry, I'll help you pack your room.\"","Marcus admits to his father, \"I'm worried about leaving all of my friends.\"","Marcus says cheerfully, \"At least our new house has a big backyard.\"","Marcus tells his mother, \"I'm thrilled about the move,\" then, alone, says, \"I dread it.\""]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Saying he's thrilled to his mother but admitting dread in private shows him hiding his feelings. Telling his father he's worried shows the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-751$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A sociologist claims that people who live in neighborhoods with more parks tend to feel more connected to their neighbors.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the sociologist's claim?$q$, $q$["Residents of park-rich neighborhoods felt about as connected to neighbors as other residents.","Residents of neighborhoods with many parks exercised more often than other residents.","Residents who visited parks often said that they enjoyed the fresh air and quiet.","Parks were more common in the city's newer neighborhoods than in its older ones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$To weaken the claim, evidence must show no link between parks and connection. Only the finding of similar connection levels does that.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-752$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Cactus plants store water in their thick stems. They also have spines instead of wide, flat leaves, which helps reduce water loss. These features suggest that cactuses are well suited to ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["places with very little sunlight.","places with heavy rainfall every day.","growing underwater.","places where water is scarce."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Storing water and reducing water loss are adaptations for dry places.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-753$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Plastic Recycled by a City (tons)</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Plastic collected</th><th>Plastic recycled</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2019</td><td>5,000</td><td>1,500</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>5,200</td><td>2,600</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>5,100</td><td>3,570</td></tr></tbody></table>A city report claims that the city became much more effective at recycling the plastic it collected from 2019 to 2023.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the report's claim?$q$, $q$["The city collected 5,200 tons of plastic in 2021, more than in any other year.","The city recycled 30% of the plastic it collected in 2019 but 70% in 2023.","The city recycled 2,600 tons of plastic in 2021.","The city collected about the same amount of plastic in 2019 and 2023."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Effectiveness at recycling collected plastic means the share recycled. 1,500/5,000 = 30% and 3,570/5,100 = 70%, a large improvement.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-754$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A geologist claims that a nearby lake was formed by a glacier.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the geologist's claim?$q$, $q$["The lake freezes over every winter and stays frozen for several months.","The lake is deeper than most other lakes in the surrounding region.","Rocks around the lake show long, parallel scratches typical of glaciers.","The lake is home to several species of fish, including trout and perch."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Glacial scratches in the rock are direct evidence of past glacier activity. A lake freezing doesn't show how it formed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-755$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$The bakery usually sells out of its cinnamon rolls by 9:00 a.m. On Saturday, though, there were still cinnamon rolls left at noon. Saturday was also the day of a big snowstorm that kept many people at home. The bakery likely ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["made fewer cinnamon rolls than usual that Saturday.","stopped selling its cinnamon rolls before noon.","raised the price of its cinnamon rolls that weekend.","had fewer customers than usual because of the storm."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The storm kept people home, so fewer customers likely explains the leftover rolls.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-756$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Biologists have found that some trees in a forest share nutrients through underground networks of fungi. Older, larger trees appear to send sugars to young seedlings growing in their shade. This suggests that seedlings in shaded areas may ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["not need any sunlight at all in order to grow.","harm the older trees by taking too many of their nutrients.","grow faster than young trees growing in full sunlight.","get help from older trees that offsets their limited sunlight."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Shaded seedlings get less light to make their own sugar, so sugars from older trees can help them. Nothing shows they outgrow sunlit trees or need no light.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-757$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$In the novel Harbor Lights, a critic argues, the town of Port Vell is portrayed as a place that resists change.$q$, $q$Which quotation from the novel would best support the critic's argument?$q$, $q$["\"When a company proposed a modern dock, the town voted it down, as it had every new idea.\"","\"Each morning, the harbor was crowded with fishing boats heading out to sea.\"","\"Many young people moved to Port Vell in those years to start new businesses.\"","\"Port Vell's old stone lighthouse could be seen for miles along the coast.\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rejecting every new idea for fifty years shows resistance to change. Young people moving in to start businesses suggests the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-758$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Owls can turn their heads about 270 degrees. Unlike humans, owls cannot move their eyes within their sockets. Owls most likely turn their heads so far because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["their eyes are too small for them to see well at night.","they prefer to sleep with their heads facing backward.","they cannot hear well enough to locate prey by sound.","they must move their heads to look in different directions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Since owls can't move their eyes, they must turn their heads to look around.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-759$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Maria's houseplant had been wilting for days. After she moved it from a dark corner to a sunny windowsill, its leaves perked up within a week. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the plant was a type that grows best in dark places.","the plant had been getting too much water in the corner.","the plant needed more light than it had been getting.","the windowsill was too cold for the plant to grow well."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The plant improved after moving to a sunnier spot, so it likely needed more light.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-760$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A student claims that the poem's speaker misses her childhood home.$q$, $q$Which quotation from the poem would best support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["\"The clock strikes twelve, and then is still, / and the house grows dark.\"","\"I dream of the porch where I once sat, / and wake to find it gone.\"","\"The city lights are bright tonight / along the busy street.\"","\"I walk to work along the river / as the morning fog lifts.\""]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dreaming of a past porch and finding it gone expresses longing for home.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-761$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Price of a Gallon of Milk</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2000</td><td>$2.78</td></tr><tr><td>2010</td><td>$3.32</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>$3.54</td></tr></tbody></table>An economist noted that the price of milk rose more between 2000 and 2010 than between 2010 and 2020.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the economist's note?$q$, $q$["The price of milk was $2.78 in 2000 and had risen to $3.54 by 2020.","The price of a gallon of milk rose in each of the two decades shown.","The price of a gallon of milk was $3.54 in 2020, its highest level shown.","The price rose $0.54 from 2000 to 2010 but only $0.22 from 2010 to 2020."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Comparing the two increases ($0.54 versus $0.22) directly supports the note.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-762$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$In one region, farmers who planted rows of wildflowers alongside their crop fields found that fewer of their crops were damaged by insect pests. The wildflowers attracted ladybugs and wasps, which feed on those pests. This suggests that the wildflowers reduced crop damage by ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["releasing chemicals that kill all insects.","replacing the crops as a food source for people.","drawing in insects that prey on crop pests.","shading the crops from the sun."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The flowers attracted predators of the pests, which likely reduced the pests and thus the damage.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-763$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Many people think bats are blind. In fact, all bats can see, and some see quite well. Many bats also use echolocation, making sounds and listening to the echoes to find food in the dark.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Bats rely only on their eyesight to find food in the dark.","Bats are not blind, and many also use sound to find food.","Bats are the only animals that use echolocation to find food.","Most people are afraid of bats because they think bats are blind."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text corrects the belief that bats are blind and adds that many use echolocation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-764$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Rainfall in a Town (inches)</caption><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Rainfall</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Winter</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Spring</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Summer</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Fall</td><td>7</td></tr></tbody></table>A student claims that summer is the town's driest season.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["Summer had only 3 inches of rain, less than any other season.","Fall had 7 inches of rain, which was less than spring had.","Spring had 9 inches of rain, which was more than fall had.","Winter had 11 inches of rain, more than any other season."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3 inches is the lowest value, so summer is the driest season.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-765$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$Astronomer Priya Natarajan argues that some of the earliest black holes formed directly from collapsing clouds of gas rather than from the remains of dead stars.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Natarajan's argument?$q$, $q$["Very large black holes existed so early that stars would not have had time to form and die.","Most black holes in today's universe are much smaller than the earliest ones were.","Stars that collapse into black holes are usually many times larger than the Sun.","Large clouds of gas are common in many galaxies that astronomers observe today."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If large black holes existed before stars could have formed and died, they likely formed another way, such as from collapsing gas.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-766$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Squirrels bury thousands of nuts each fall to eat during the winter. They don't find all of them, though. The forgotten nuts often sprout, and over time they grow into new trees. In this way, squirrels help forests spread.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["By burying nuts they later forget, squirrels help new trees grow.","Squirrels remember where they bury nearly every nut they store each year.","Forests would be unable to grow at all if squirrels stopped burying nuts.","Squirrels survive the winter by eating only the nuts they buried in the fall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text explains that forgotten nuts sprout into trees, so squirrels help forests spread. It doesn't say forests depend entirely on squirrels.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-767$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$In a study of 300 households, researchers found that families who ate dinner together at least four times a week reported stronger relationships and lower stress. The researchers caution, however, that families who already get along well may simply be more likely to eat together.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Family dinners are linked to stronger relationships, but the cause is unclear.","Eating dinner together causes families to get along better and feel less stress.","The study found no connection between family dinners and lower stress levels.","Most families in the study ate dinner together at least four times every week."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text reports an association and then a caution that the cause could run the other way. Claiming dinners "cause" better relationships overstates the finding.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-768$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A student claims that regular exercise helps people sleep better.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["Exercising for 30 minutes a day can improve heart health and lower blood pressure.","People who exercise regularly often prefer to do so in the morning rather than at night.","Many people find it hard to find the energy to exercise after a long day of work.","People who exercised 30 minutes a day fell asleep faster and slept longer than others."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The claim links exercise to better sleep. Only the finding about falling asleep faster and sleeping longer addresses sleep.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-769$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A biologist hypothesizes that a certain flower attracts more bees when it is purple than when it is white.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the biologist's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["In a field with both colors, bees visited purple flowers three times as often as white ones.","Bees in the field visited many kinds of flowers besides the purple and white ones.","White flowers of this species bloom earlier in the spring than purple flowers do.","Purple flowers of this species grow taller than white ones and produce more nectar."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The hypothesis compares bee visits to the two colors. Only the finding about visits does so.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-770$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A student claims that in the poem "Winter Field," the speaker finds hope in a bleak setting.$q$, $q$Which quotation from the poem would best support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["\"The wind cuts sharp across the field, / and nothing moves for miles.\"","\"No bird sings in the gray, still air / above the empty road.\"","\"Beneath the frozen, broken ground, / a green shoot waits for spring.\"","\"The snow lies deep on every fence / and buries every path.\""]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The green shoot waiting under frozen ground is an image of hope in a bleak scene. The other lines describe only bleakness.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-771$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Monthly Rent for a One-Bedroom Apartment</caption><thead><tr><th>Neighborhood</th><th>2018</th><th>2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Eastside</td><td>$1,100</td><td>$1,250</td></tr><tr><td>Riverside</td><td>$950</td><td>$1,400</td></tr><tr><td>Hilltop</td><td>$1,300</td><td>$1,360</td></tr></tbody></table>A housing reporter wrote that Riverside, once the least expensive of the three neighborhoods, had become the most expensive by 2023.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the reporter's statement?$q$, $q$["Hilltop had the highest rent of the three neighborhoods in 2018, at $1,300.","Riverside had the lowest rent in 2018 ($950) and the highest in 2023 ($1,400).","Eastside's rent rose by $150, from $1,100 in 2018 to $1,250 in 2023.","Riverside's rent increased by $450 from 2018 to 2023, more than the others."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The statement has two parts: least expensive in 2018 and most expensive in 2023. Only one choice shows both.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-772$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Favorite Lunch Choice</caption><thead><tr><th>Food</th><th>Students</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pizza</td><td>64</td></tr><tr><td>Tacos</td><td>58</td></tr><tr><td>Salad</td><td>21</td></tr><tr><td>Soup</td><td>17</td></tr></tbody></table>A student claims that pizza and tacos were both much more popular than salad or soup.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["Pizza received 64 votes, the most of any lunch choice in the table.","Tacos received 58 votes, while soup received only 17 votes.","Pizza (64) and tacos (58) each got about three times the votes of salad or soup.","Salad received 21 votes, slightly more than soup, which received 17."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the choice comparing both pizza and tacos with both salad and soup supports the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-773$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A marine scientist argues that coral reefs near protected areas recover faster from bleaching events than reefs in unprotected areas.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the scientist's argument?$q$, $q$["Many tourists visit reefs in protected areas to see their colorful fish.","Bleaching events on coral reefs have become more common over the past 30 years.","Protected areas often limit fishing and boating near the reefs they contain.","After the same bleaching event, coral recovered to 80% in protected areas but 45% elsewhere."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the comparison of recovery rates after the same event addresses the argument.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-774$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Many people believe that the Great Wall of China is visible from space with the naked eye. Astronauts, however, report that it is very difficult to see from low Earth orbit, because the wall is narrow and its color is similar to the surrounding land.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Despite a popular belief, the Great Wall is very hard to see from space.","The Great Wall is the only human-made object that can be seen from space.","The Great Wall is wider than most highways, so it stands out from space.","Astronauts report that they can easily see the Great Wall from low orbit."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text corrects a popular belief with astronauts' reports and explains why the wall is hard to see.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-775$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Researchers gave two groups of students the same math problems. One group was told the problems were "a fun puzzle challenge"; the other was told they were "a test of math ability." The first group solved more problems correctly. The researchers suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["students in the first group were simply better at math.","students should never be given tests in their classes.","puzzles are generally easier to solve than math problems.","the way a task is described can affect performance on it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Since the problems were the same, the difference in performance likely came from how the task was described.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-776$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$Researchers claim that a type of seaweed added to cattle feed reduces the amount of methane the animals produce.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["The seaweed added to the feed is grown in warm coastal waters.","Cattle given feed with the seaweed produced about the same amount of methane as cattle given ordinary feed.","Cattle given the seaweed feed gained weight at a normal rate.","Methane released by cattle is a powerful greenhouse gas."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Equal methane output with and without seaweed would undercut the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-777$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Early in her career, astronomer Vera Rubin noticed that stars at the edges of galaxies moved just as fast as stars near the centers. According to existing theories, outer stars should have moved more slowly. Her observations provided some of the strongest evidence for dark matter, an invisible substance that makes up most of the matter in the universe.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Rubin showed that dark matter makes up a small fraction of the universe.","Rubin's observations of star speeds provided key evidence for dark matter.","Rubin's observations confirmed existing theories about galaxies' motion.","Rubin discovered that outer stars move more slowly than inner stars."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Rubin's unexpected findings contradicted theory and supported the existence of dark matter.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-778$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Scientists found fossils of sea creatures high in the Himalaya mountains. Sea creatures live in the ocean. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["sea creatures were once able to climb high mountains.","the Himalaya are the oldest mountains on Earth.","the ocean is rising quickly around the Himalaya today.","the rock in these mountains was once under the ocean."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Ocean fossils in mountain rock suggest that the rock was once underwater.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-779$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$The following text is from a short story. Theo had practiced his speech for weeks. But when he stepped onto the stage and saw hundreds of faces staring back at him, every word disappeared from his mind.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The audience left the room before Theo was able to speak.","Theo enjoyed speaking in front of large crowds of people.","Although he had prepared, Theo forgot his speech on stage.","Theo forgot his speech because he had not practiced it."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Theo practiced for weeks but forgot the words when he saw the crowd.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-780$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$In the 1800s, many scientists believed that disease was caused by "bad air." When cities built sewer systems to carry waste away, rates of diseases like cholera dropped sharply, even though the air in the cities was still polluted by factories. These results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["polluted air from factories was the main cause of cholera.","cholera was more closely linked to waste than to polluted air.","the new sewer systems also made the city air cleaner.","cholera declined for reasons that had nothing to do with sewers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Cholera fell when waste was removed but the air stayed polluted, pointing to waste, not air, as the key factor.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-781$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Some birds, like crows and ravens, are very good at solving problems. In experiments, crows have used sticks to pull food out of narrow tubes. Some have even bent wires into hooks to reach a treat.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Crows prefer using sticks to bending wires when solving problems.","Crows and ravens eat mainly insects that they pull from tubes.","Crows and ravens are skilled problem solvers.","All birds are able to use tools to reach food in experiments."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The examples of using sticks and bending wires show crows' problem-solving skills. The text doesn't say all birds use tools.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-782$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Percentage of Adults Who Read at Least One Book in the Past Year</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Print books</th><th>E-books</th><th>Audiobooks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2012</td><td>65</td><td>23</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>2016</td><td>65</td><td>28</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>65</td><td>30</td><td>23</td></tr></tbody></table>A researcher claims that from 2012 to 2021, the share of adults who read print books stayed steady while the share using audiobooks grew substantially.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["The share of adults using audiobooks rose only slightly, to 14%, by 2016.","The share of adults using e-books rose from 23% in 2012 to 30% in 2021.","Print reading held at 65% from 2012 to 2021, while audiobook use rose from 13% to 23%.","In 2021, more adults read print books (65%) than listened to audiobooks (23%)."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The claim has two parts: steady print reading and substantial audiobook growth. Only one choice addresses both.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-783$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Students Who Bike to School</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Number of students</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2019</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>55</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>71</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>90</td></tr></tbody></table>The principal said that the number of students who bike to school has grown every year since 2019.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the principal's statement?$q$, $q$["55 students biked to school in 2020, up from 40 in 2019.","The number rose each year: 40, then 55, then 71, then 90.","More students biked in 2021 (71) than in 2019 (40).","90 students biked to school in 2022, the most of any year."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Only the choice giving each year shows growth every year.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-784$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Test Scores by Study Method</caption><thead><tr><th>Study method</th><th>Average score (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rereading notes</td><td>68</td></tr><tr><td>Highlighting</td><td>66</td></tr><tr><td>Practice testing</td><td>84</td></tr><tr><td>Summarizing</td><td>73</td></tr></tbody></table>A psychologist suggests that students who quiz themselves tend to perform better than those who use other common study methods.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the psychologist's suggestion?$q$, $q$["Students who summarized averaged 73%, which was higher than students who highlighted (66%).","Practice testing averaged 84%, beating rereading (68%), highlighting (66%), and summarizing (73%).","Students who reread their notes averaged 68%, slightly higher than students who highlighted.","Students who highlighted averaged 66%, the lowest score of any of the four study methods."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Practice testing is quizzing oneself. Only the choice comparing it with all other methods supports the suggestion.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-785$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A teacher claims that students learn vocabulary better when they use new words in their own sentences.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the teacher's claim?$q$, $q$["Students said that they enjoyed learning new words more than doing grammar exercises.","The vocabulary list included 20 words, each with a definition and an example.","Students who wrote sentences with new words remembered more of them a week later.","Most students already knew several of the words on the list before the lesson began."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the finding that writing sentences led to better memory supports the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-786$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$In the school garden, the tomato plants near the fence grew tall and produced many tomatoes. The plants under the large oak tree stayed small and produced few. Since tomatoes need plenty of sunlight, the plants under the tree probably ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["received less sunlight than the plants near the fence.","were watered too often by the students in the garden.","were a different kind of vegetable than the others.","were planted later in the year than the other plants."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Tomatoes need sun, and a large tree casts shade, so the plants under it likely got less light.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-787$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$When the poet Joy Harjo was named U.S. Poet Laureate in 2019, she became the first Native American to hold the position. During her three terms, she created a national project that mapped poems by Native writers across the country, aiming to bring wider attention to their work.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Joy Harjo used her historic role as Poet Laureate to promote Native poets.","Joy Harjo was the first person in history to serve three terms as Poet Laureate.","Native American poets were already widely read before Harjo's mapping project.","Joy Harjo stopped writing her own poetry after she was named Poet Laureate."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text notes her historic appointment and her project to promote Native poets.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-788$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Museum Visitors</caption><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Visitors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>June</td><td>4,200</td></tr><tr><td>July</td><td>5,800</td></tr><tr><td>August</td><td>5,100</td></tr></tbody></table>The museum director said that July was the busiest month of the summer.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the director's statement?$q$, $q$["The museum had 4,200 visitors in June, its first month of summer.","August had 5,100 visitors, which was more than June had.","The museum had 5,800 visitors in July, more than in June or August.","The museum had 5,100 visitors in August and 4,200 in June."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$5,800 is the largest number of visitors shown.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-789$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Energy Sources for a Town's Electricity (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>2015</th><th>2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coal</td><td>48</td><td>22</td></tr><tr><td>Natural gas</td><td>30</td><td>35</td></tr><tr><td>Solar</td><td>5</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Wind</td><td>17</td><td>19</td></tr></tbody></table>A town official stated that solar power had replaced much of the town's coal power between 2015 and 2023.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the official's statement?$q$, $q$["Coal's share fell from 48% to 22% while solar's rose from 5% to 24%.","Natural gas's share rose from 30% to 35%, the most of any source in 2023.","In 2023, solar provided 24% of the town's electricity, more than wind did.","Wind's share changed little from 2015 to 2023, rising from 17% to 19%."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$To support the idea that solar replaced coal, the data must show coal falling and solar rising.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-790$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Lena's soccer team practiced passing drills every day for a month. In their next game, they completed many more passes than they had earlier in the season. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the team's opponents were stronger than they were earlier.","the passing drills may have improved the team's passing.","the team had lost every game it played during that month.","the team had practiced shooting instead of passing drills."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$More completed passes after a month of passing drills suggests the practice helped.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-791$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Trees Planted by Volunteers</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Trees planted</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2020</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>220</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>310</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>180</td></tr></tbody></table>The volunteer group planted the most trees in 2022.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support this statement?$q$, $q$["The group planted 220 trees in 2021, up from 150 trees in 2020.","The group planted more trees in 2021 than it had in 2020.","The group planted 310 trees in 2022, more than in any other year.","The group planted 180 trees in 2023, more than it did in 2020."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$310 is the largest value in the table.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-792$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A nutrition researcher hypothesizes that people eat more when food is served on larger plates.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researcher's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["The buffet in the study offered more than 30 dishes at every meal.","Diners given 12-inch plates ate about 50 percent more than diners given 8-inch plates.","Diners at the buffet said that they preferred plates in different colors.","Larger plates are more expensive for restaurants to buy and to wash."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Eating more with larger plates directly supports the hypothesis.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-793$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A historian argues that the spread of affordable bicycles in the 1890s gave many women greater independence.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["Bicycle factories employed thousands of workers in American cities in the 1890s.","Bicycles made in the 1890s were much heavier than most modern bicycles.","Bicycle racing became a popular spectator sport in the United States in the 1890s.","1890s diaries show many women describing how bicycles let them travel on their own."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Women's own accounts of traveling independently by bicycle directly support the argument.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-794$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 1, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Daily High Temperature (°F)</caption><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Temperature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>March</td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td>April</td><td>63</td></tr><tr><td>May</td><td>72</td></tr><tr><td>June</td><td>81</td></tr></tbody></table>A student claims that the average daily high temperature increased each month from March to June.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["The temperature rose each month: 54°F, 63°F, 72°F, and then 81°F.","The temperature was 63°F in April, which was warmer than in March.","The temperature was 72°F in May, nine degrees cooler than in June.","The temperature reached 81°F in June, the warmest month in the table."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the choice giving all four months shows a steady increase from March through June.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-795$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 1, $q$Jamal noticed that the puddles on his street froze overnight. When he checked the thermometer in the morning, it read 25°F. Water freezes at 32°F. Jamal could reasonably conclude that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the puddles were made of something other than water.","it would most likely snow later in the day.","the thermometer had been broken during the night.","the temperature had dropped below freezing during the night."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The puddles froze and the temperature was 25°F, below water's freezing point, so it got below freezing overnight.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-796$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Share of Trips by Mode in a City (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>2012</th><th>2022</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Car</td><td>71</td><td>58</td></tr><tr><td>Bus or train</td><td>16</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Bicycle</td><td>4</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Walking</td><td>9</td><td>11</td></tr></tbody></table>A city planner argued that, of the alternatives to driving, cycling grew the most between 2012 and 2022.$q$, $q$Which choice best uses data from the table to support the planner's argument?$q$, $q$["Walking's share of trips rose from 9% in 2012 to 11% in 2022, a gain of 2 points.","Cycling rose from 4% to 12%, more than transit (16% to 19%) or walking (9% to 11%).","The share of trips taken by car fell by 13 points, from 71% in 2012 to 58% in 2022.","In 2022, more trips were taken by bus or train (19%) than by bicycle (12%)."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The argument compares growth among alternatives to driving. Cycling's 8-point gain is larger than the gains for transit and walking. Car trips aren't an alternative to driving.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-797$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$The ancient Maya built large cities in what is now Mexico and Central America. They developed a writing system, studied the stars, and created an accurate calendar. Many of their pyramids and temples still stand today.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The ancient Maya built their cities only in what is now Mexico.","The ancient Maya had an advanced civilization with many achievements.","The Maya studied the stars but never created an accurate calendar.","Most Maya pyramids and temples were built in the last few centuries."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text lists Maya achievements in building, writing, astronomy, and timekeeping.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-798$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Pianist Teresa Carreño began performing when she was just eight years old. As a child, she played for President Abraham Lincoln at the White House. She went on to tour the world and became one of the most famous pianists of her time.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Teresa Carreño was the piano teacher of President Abraham Lincoln.","Teresa Carreño was a child performer who became a world-famous pianist.","Teresa Carreño stopped performing in public once she grew up.","Teresa Carreño was hired to write music for the White House."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text traces Carreño from childhood performer to world-famous pianist.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-799$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 1, $q$A researcher claims that a new app helps people save money.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["App users saved $400 more over six months than similar people who didn't use it.","Many users said that the app was easy to use and well designed.","The app is available in 12 languages and works on most smartphones.","The app was downloaded more than one million times in its first year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the comparison showing more savings for app users supports the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$For much of the twentieth century, the dominant view among historians was that preindustrial European peasants lived in isolated villages, rarely traveling more than a few miles from home. Parish records analyzed by historian Clara Brennan tell a different story. Tracing marriages, deaths, and apprenticeships across dozens of English parishes between 1600 and 1750, she found that a large share of villagers had been born elsewhere, often more than twenty miles away, and that many young people left home to work as servants on distant farms before marrying. Brennan concludes that rural society was far more mobile than historians once assumed.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Preindustrial European peasants rarely traveled more than a few miles from the villages where they were born.","Parish records are the most reliable source of information about life in preindustrial English villages.","Young people in preindustrial England often worked as servants on farms before they married and settled down.","Records show that preindustrial English villagers moved around much more than the traditional view held."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Brennan's research contradicts the view of isolated villages, showing substantial mobility. The choice about young people working as servants is a detail supporting the main idea, and the choice saying peasants rarely traveled states the view she overturns.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$When an octopus encounters an unfamiliar object, it often explores it with its arms before looking at it closely. Neuroscientist Hiro Tanaka suggests that this behavior reflects the unusual organization of the octopus nervous system: about two-thirds of its neurons are located in its arms rather than its brain, and each arm can taste, touch, and respond to its surroundings with some independence. Rather than sending all information to a central brain for processing, Tanaka argues, the octopus may "think" in a partly distributed way, with its arms making some decisions on their own.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Tanaka argues that octopuses rely more on vision than on touch when they encounter unfamiliar objects.","Tanaka has found that the octopus brain contains more neurons than the brains of most other invertebrates.","Tanaka proposes that the octopus's arms may process information and act with some independence from its brain.","Tanaka suggests that octopuses explore unfamiliar objects with their arms because their eyesight is poor."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text centers on Tanaka's idea that octopus cognition is partly distributed to its arms. It says nothing about poor eyesight or comparisons of brain neuron counts.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that the Sahara has been a desert for millions of years. In fact, between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, much of the region was covered in grasslands, lakes, and wetlands. Rock paintings from this period show people herding cattle and hunting hippopotamuses, and fossils of fish and crocodiles have been found in areas that are now sand dunes. Scientists attribute these changes to slow shifts in Earth's orbit, which strengthened the monsoon rains that reached North Africa.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Rock paintings in the Sahara show that people living there once herded cattle and hunted hippopotamuses.","The Sahara was much wetter for thousands of years in the relatively recent past, likely because of changes in Earth's orbit.","The Sahara has been a desert for millions of years, although monsoon rains occasionally reach North Africa.","Shifts in Earth's orbit have caused most of the world's deserts to become wetter over the past 11,000 years."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text corrects the assumption of a permanently dry Sahara, describing a wet period and its likely orbital cause. The rock-painting choice is a supporting detail; the choice about most of the world's deserts overgeneralizes.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The composer Florence Ashby wrote more than two hundred songs, but she published fewer than twenty during her lifetime, and most of her manuscripts sat in a relative's attic for decades after her death. When musicologist Daniel Okafor discovered them in 2011, he found that many of the unpublished songs used harmonies and rhythms far more adventurous than those in her published works. Okafor suspects that Ashby kept her most experimental music private, fearing that publishers and audiences of her time would reject it.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Okafor's discovery revealed that Ashby's unpublished songs were more innovative than her published ones, perhaps because she feared rejection.","Okafor discovered Ashby's manuscripts in 2011 in the attic of a relative, where they had been stored for decades after her death.","Ashby published fewer than twenty songs during her lifetime because publishers refused to print music written by women.","Ashby's published songs were more popular with audiences of her time than the songs of most other composers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text focuses on what the discovery revealed about Ashby's unpublished work and Okafor's explanation. The choice about finding the manuscripts in an attic is a narrow detail, and the choice blaming publishers' refusal to print women's music adds a reason the text doesn't give.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Coffee plants originated in the highland forests of Ethiopia, where they grow in the shade of taller trees. As coffee farming spread, many growers cleared forests to plant coffee in full sun, which can produce higher yields in the short term. Ecologist Ruth Mekonnen notes, however, that sun-grown coffee typically requires more fertilizer and pesticides, and that the loss of shade trees removes habitat for birds that eat coffee pests. Over time, she argues, shade-grown coffee can be more sustainable and, in some regions, nearly as productive.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Coffee plants originated in Ethiopia, where they naturally grow in the shade of taller trees in highland forests.","Mekonnen argues that coffee farmers should stop using fertilizer and pesticides on all of their coffee plants.","Sun-grown coffee produces higher yields than shade-grown coffee in every region where coffee is grown.","Mekonnen argues that although sun-grown coffee can yield more at first, shade-grown coffee may be the more sustainable choice."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text contrasts sun-grown coffee's short-term yields with shade-grown coffee's long-term benefits, which is Mekonnen's argument. The claim that sun-grown coffee yields more in every region contradicts "nearly as productive" in some regions.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a novel. Lucia has just arrived at her aunt's apartment in the city after living her whole life on a farm.<br><br>The apartment was on the eleventh floor, and from its single window Lucia could see a hundred other windows, each lit, each holding a life she would never know. At home the nearest neighbor had been a mile away, and at night the only lights were the stars. She had thought the city would make her feel less alone. Instead, standing at the glass with all those strangers glowing back at her, she felt the distance between people more sharply than she ever had in the fields.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Lucia is excited by the many new people she will be able to meet now that she lives in a large city.","Lucia misses the farm because the city's lights prevent her from seeing the stars at night.","Lucia is surprised to find that being surrounded by people in the city makes her feel more isolated than she did on the farm.","Lucia is disappointed that her aunt's apartment is so small and has only a single window."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$She expected the city to make her less lonely but instead feels the distance between people more sharply—surprising isolation amid crowds.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Urban heat islands—areas where cities are significantly warmer than the surrounding countryside—are usually attributed to dark pavement and rooftops, which absorb sunlight, and to the heat produced by cars and air conditioners. Researcher Imani Clarke has found that the effect is not distributed evenly within cities. In the city she studied, neighborhoods with few trees and many parking lots were up to 7°C hotter on summer afternoons than leafy neighborhoods only a few kilometers away, and these hotter neighborhoods were disproportionately home to lower-income residents.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Dark pavement and rooftops absorb sunlight and are the main causes of urban heat islands in most cities.","Clarke found that heat in the city she studied varied widely between neighborhoods, with lower-income areas bearing more of it.","Clarke found that cities are about 7°C hotter than the surrounding countryside on summer afternoons.","Lower-income residents of the city Clarke studied use air conditioners more than other residents do."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Clarke's finding is that heat is uneven within the city and falls hardest on lower-income neighborhoods. The 7°C figure compares neighborhoods, not the city and countryside.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Greek historian Herodotus is often called the "father of history" for his systematic account of the wars between Greece and Persia. He has also been called the "father of lies," since his work includes tales of gold-digging ants and other implausible wonders. Historian Anna Lindqvist argues that both labels miss the point. Herodotus, she notes, frequently reported stories he had heard while explicitly stating that he did not believe them; he saw his task as preserving what people said as well as what he could verify.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Lindqvist argues that Herodotus's inclusion of implausible tales reflects his view of a historian's role rather than carelessness.","Lindqvist argues that Herodotus should be called the \"father of lies\" because he reported many implausible stories.","Herodotus wrote a systematic account of the wars between Greece and Persia, for which he is called the \"father of history.\"","Lindqvist argues that the stories Herodotus reported about gold-digging ants were based on accurate observations."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Lindqvist explains that Herodotus often flagged doubtful stories and saw recording what people said as part of his task—so the tales reflect his view of his role.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In 1911, the Mona Lisa was stolen from the Louvre in Paris. Before the theft, the painting was admired by art experts but was not especially famous among the general public. The theft became an international news sensation: newspapers around the world printed the painting's image, and crowds lined up at the Louvre to stare at the empty space where it had hung. When the painting was recovered two years later, it returned as a celebrity. Some historians argue that the theft did more to make the Mona Lisa the world's most famous painting than anything Leonardo da Vinci did.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The Mona Lisa was stolen from the Louvre in 1911 and recovered two years later.","Art experts in the early 1900s considered the Mona Lisa the greatest painting ever made.","Newspapers in 1911 printed images of the Mona Lisa in order to help find the painting's thief.","The theft of the Mona Lisa may have played a major role in making it world-famous."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text explains how the theft turned the painting into a public sensation and suggests it was key to its fame. The 1911 theft-and-recovery choice is just a detail; the claims about early-1900s experts and about newspapers trying to catch the thief aren't supported.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Ana Petrova studies how plants respond to drought. In her experiments, plants of the same species were divided into two groups. One group experienced a mild drought early in its growth; the other was watered normally. Later, when both groups were exposed to severe drought, the plants that had experienced the earlier mild drought survived at much higher rates. Petrova found that these plants had altered the activity of certain genes after the first drought, and the changes persisted, preparing them for future stress.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Petrova found that plants exposed to severe drought produce seeds that are more resistant to drought than the seeds of other plants.","Petrova found that plants watered normally during their early growth survived severe drought at higher rates than other plants.","Petrova found that plants exposed to mild drought were better able to survive later severe drought because of lasting changes in gene activity.","Petrova divided plants of the same species into two groups in order to compare their responses to different amounts of water."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The central finding is that early mild drought led to persistent gene-activity changes that improved survival of later severe drought. The choice about dividing plants into two groups describes method, not the finding.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$When the Brooklyn Bridge was completed in 1883, it was the longest suspension bridge in the world. Its chief engineer, Washington Roebling, became ill during construction and was confined to his apartment for years. His wife, Emily Warren Roebling, took on an increasingly central role, relaying instructions to the workers, studying engineering herself, and meeting with officials and suppliers. Many historians now credit her with keeping the project on track, though at the time her contributions were largely unrecognized.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Washington Roebling became ill while working as chief engineer of the Brooklyn Bridge and was confined to his apartment.","Emily Warren Roebling played a crucial but long-unrecognized role in completing the Brooklyn Bridge.","The Brooklyn Bridge was the longest suspension bridge in the world when it was completed in 1883.","Emily Warren Roebling was officially named the chief engineer of the Brooklyn Bridge after her husband became ill."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text focuses on Emily Roebling's essential contributions and how they went unrecognized. She was not officially named chief engineer.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The fiddler crab has one claw that can be enormous—in some species, nearly half the weight of its body. Only males have the oversized claw, which they wave to attract females and use to fight rivals. The claw is clumsy for feeding, so males must eat using only their small claw, which takes twice as long as eating with two. Biologists see the fiddler crab as a vivid example of a trait that helps an animal reproduce even as it makes other aspects of survival more difficult.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The male fiddler crab's large claw illustrates how a trait can aid reproduction while imposing other costs.","Male fiddler crabs use their oversized claws mainly for feeding, which helps them grow larger than females.","Female fiddler crabs prefer males that can eat quickly using both of their claws.","The oversized claw of the male fiddler crab can weigh nearly as much as the rest of its body in some species."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text presents the claw as helping reproduction (attracting mates, fighting) while making feeding harder—a trade-off.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In the 1920s, the city of Tulsa, Oklahoma, was home to Greenwood, a thriving Black neighborhood with hotels, theaters, newspapers, and dozens of businesses, so prosperous that it was nicknamed "Black Wall Street." In 1921, a white mob attacked the neighborhood, burning more than thirty-five blocks and killing as many as three hundred people. For decades afterward, the massacre was rarely discussed in schools or newspapers, and many Tulsans grew up unaware that it had happened. Only in recent decades have official investigations and public commemorations brought it to wider attention.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Greenwood was a prosperous Black neighborhood in Tulsa, Oklahoma, with hotels, theaters, newspapers, and businesses.","Schools and newspapers in Tulsa frequently discussed the 1921 massacre in the decades after it occurred.","Official investigations of the 1921 massacre found that it was caused by a dispute between local businesses.","The 1921 destruction of a prosperous Black neighborhood in Tulsa was long overlooked before recently receiving wider attention."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text describes Greenwood, the 1921 massacre, its long neglect, and its recent recognition. The other choices are details, contradictions, or unsupported.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Most people know that the human brain has two hemispheres, but the popular idea that people are either "left-brained" (logical) or "right-brained" (creative) has little scientific support. A study that analyzed brain scans of more than a thousand people found no evidence that individuals tend to use one hemisphere more than the other overall. While certain functions, such as language, are concentrated more on one side, complex tasks of all kinds draw on networks spanning both hemispheres.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Most people know that the human brain is divided into two hemispheres that perform the same functions.","People who are logical use the left hemisphere more, while creative people use the right hemisphere more.","Research does not support the idea that people are dominated by one hemisphere of the brain, though some functions are concentrated on one side.","Language is the only function of the human brain that is concentrated in one hemisphere."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text debunks left/right-brain personality types while acknowledging some lateralization. The choice saying logical people use the left hemisphere and creative people the right is the myth being refuted.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The novelist's early books were praised for their elaborate plots, full of coincidences, secret identities, and dramatic reversals. Her later work, critic Marcus Hale observes, abandons plot almost entirely. In her final novel, nothing much happens: an elderly woman spends a summer tending her garden and remembering her life. Yet Hale argues that this novel is her most suspenseful, because the reader slowly realizes that the woman's memories are not entirely reliable, and the true drama lies in discovering what she has chosen to forget.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Hale argues that the novelist's early books were better than her later books because they had more elaborate plots.","Hale argues that the novelist's final book creates suspense through an unreliable narrator's memories rather than through plot.","The novelist's final book tells the story of an elderly woman who spends a summer tending a garden.","Hale argues that the novelist abandoned plot in her later work because readers disliked coincidences."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Hale's central claim is that the plotless final novel is suspenseful because of the narrator's unreliable memories.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Scientists once believed that the deep ocean floor was a stable environment, unchanged for long periods. Long-term monitoring at a site off the coast of California has challenged that view. Over thirty years, researchers recorded large swings in the amount of food—mainly dead plankton and other organic particles—sinking from the surface. These swings were linked to changes in surface conditions, such as water temperature and currents, and the animals living on the sea floor changed in number and kind as the food supply rose and fell.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Long-term monitoring has shown that the deep sea floor changes in response to conditions at the ocean's surface.","Scientists have found that the animals living on the deep sea floor feed mainly on organic particles from the surface.","The deep ocean floor off the coast of California has remained stable and unchanged for at least thirty years.","Changes in water temperature and currents at the surface are caused by changes on the deep ocean floor."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The main point is that the deep floor isn't stable; it changes with surface conditions through the food supply.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In many cities, the first hour of a rainstorm sends a surge of polluted water—carrying oil, metals, and trash from streets and roofs—into rivers and lakes. Engineer Paulo Mendes advocates "rain gardens," shallow planted depressions that collect runoff and let it soak slowly into the ground, where soil and plant roots filter out many pollutants. In a neighborhood where Mendes helped install dozens of rain gardens, monitoring showed that the amount of polluted runoff reaching a nearby creek fell by more than half.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The first hour of a rainstorm carries oil, metals, and trash from city streets and roofs into rivers and lakes.","Mendes argues that cities should stop building streets and roofs in order to prevent polluted runoff.","Rain gardens are shallow planted depressions that collect runoff and allow plants to grow more quickly.","Mendes promotes rain gardens as a way to reduce polluted runoff, and their use in one neighborhood substantially reduced it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text presents rain gardens as Mendes's solution and reports their success. The choice about the first hour of a rainstorm describes the problem only.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a novel. Darius has been training for months to compete in a national spelling bee.<br><br>He had expected to feel nervous, but standing on the stage, what he felt instead was a strange calm, as if all the months of practice had settled into his body like water into sand. When the judge read his word, he did not reach for it; it simply rose to meet him, letter by letter. It was only afterward, when the audience began to applaud, that his hands started to shake.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Darius feels extremely nervous throughout the competition and struggles to remember how to spell his word.","Darius regrets the months he spent practicing for the spelling bee because he finds his word easy.","Darius unexpectedly feels calm during the competition and becomes emotional only after succeeding.","Darius is embarrassed by the audience's applause because he believes he did not deserve to win."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$He expected nerves but felt calm, and his hands shook only after the applause—calm during, emotion afterward.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have long known that sea turtles return to nest on the beaches where they hatched. A study by biologist Leila Haddad found that the turtles do not always choose the exact same beach: when a beach eroded or became crowded with development, many turtles nested at nearby beaches with similar magnetic signatures instead. Haddad suggests that turtles navigate using Earth's magnetic field and remember the magnetic "address" of their birthplace, which may lead them to similar-looking magnetic locations when their original beach is unsuitable.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Sea turtles always return to the exact beach where they hatched, regardless of erosion or development.","Haddad's research suggests that sea turtles rely on remembered magnetic signatures and can nest at similar sites when their birth beach is unsuitable.","Haddad found that sea turtles avoid nesting on beaches that have magnetic signatures similar to their birthplace.","Erosion and development have caused sea turtles to stop returning to the regions where they hatched."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Haddad found turtles nesting at magnetically similar beaches when needed, supporting magnetic navigation. The other choices contradict the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In the decades after the invention of photography, many portrait painters lost their livelihoods, as people who once paid for a painted likeness turned to the cheaper and faster camera. Yet photography also created new opportunities for artists. Some painters worked as retouchers, hand-coloring photographs or painting out blemishes. Others found that the camera freed them from the demand for exact likeness, encouraging experiments with color and form that would have been unthinkable a generation earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Photography harmed some artists' livelihoods while also opening new opportunities and directions for others.","Portrait painters in the nineteenth century were unable to compete with photographers and abandoned art entirely.","Many painters worked as retouchers, hand-coloring photographs or painting out blemishes in portraits.","Photography encouraged painters to make their portraits more realistic and exact than they had been before."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text balances the losses photography caused with new opportunities it created. The claim that photography pushed painters toward more exact realism reverses the described effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Economists often assume that people make financial decisions by weighing costs and benefits. But studies of retirement savings show that small details of how choices are presented can have large effects. When companies automatically enrolled new employees in a retirement savings plan—allowing them to opt out if they wished—participation rose from under 50 percent to over 85 percent, even though employees could make the same choice either way. The findings suggest that the default option exerts a powerful influence on behavior.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Most employees choose to opt out of retirement savings plans when they are automatically enrolled in them.","Economists assume that people carefully weigh costs and benefits before deciding to save for retirement.","Companies that automatically enroll employees in retirement plans save money on employees' salaries.","Studies of retirement savings show that the default option strongly influences people's financial choices."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Automatic enrollment changed participation dramatically despite identical choices, showing the power of defaults.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The tradition of carving wooden masks in the village of San Isidro dates back at least three centuries. For most of that time, masks were made only for the village's annual festival and were often buried or burned afterward. In the 1970s, collectors began buying the masks, and carvers started producing them for sale. Anthropologist Teresa Gómez found that the commercial market changed the masks themselves: they became more brightly painted and more elaborately detailed, but carvers also continued to make simpler masks, in the old style, for the festival.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The tradition of carving wooden masks in San Isidro dates back at least three centuries.","Gómez found that carvers in San Isidro stopped making masks for the festival after collectors began buying them.","Gómez found that selling masks to collectors changed their style, though carvers kept making traditional masks for the festival.","Collectors prefer the simpler masks made for San Isidro's festival to the brightly painted masks made for sale."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Gómez found that commercial masks changed while festival masks continued in the old style. The claim that carvers stopped making festival masks contradicts the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many plants rely on animals to disperse their seeds. Some produce fleshy fruits that animals eat, later depositing the seeds elsewhere in their droppings. Others, like burdock, have hooked seeds that cling to animals' fur. Biologist Owen Fraser has documented a less familiar strategy: some plants produce seeds with fatty attachments called elaiosomes that attract ants. The ants carry the seeds to their nests, eat the elaiosomes, and discard the seeds in nutrient-rich waste piles, where they are well positioned to sprout.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Most plants depend on animals to eat their fruits and deposit seeds in their droppings.","Fraser has documented how some plants use ants to disperse their seeds by attracting them with fatty attachments.","Burdock produces hooked seeds that cling to the fur of animals that brush against the plant.","Fraser found that ants eat the seeds of many plants and discard only the fatty attachments."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes common dispersal methods and then focuses on Fraser's documentation of ant dispersal via elaiosomes. Ants eat the elaiosomes, not the seeds.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The Japanese poet Matsuo Bashō is best known for his haiku, but he also wrote travel journals that combine prose and poetry. In the most famous, The Narrow Road to the Deep North, Bashō describes a journey of more than two thousand kilometers on foot through northern Japan in 1689. Scholars have shown that some episodes in the journal did not happen as Bashō described them; he rearranged events and invented details to create a more unified literary work. The journal, they argue, should be read as a carefully crafted work of art rather than a factual record.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Scholars argue that Bashō's travel journal is better understood as a crafted literary work than as a factual account.","Bashō's travel journal describes a journey of more than two thousand kilometers on foot through northern Japan.","Bashō is best known for his travel journals, which combine prose and poetry in a single work.","Scholars have shown that Bashō never traveled through northern Japan in 1689."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The central claim is that the journal is crafted art rather than a factual record. Scholars say episodes were altered, not that he never traveled.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In recent decades, the population of a songbird called the cerulean warbler has declined sharply. Researchers initially focused on the loss of forests in the bird's North American breeding range. A study led by ecologist Javier Morales, however, found that the warblers spend the winter in a narrow band of mountain forest in South America, much of which has been cleared for agriculture. Morales argues that protecting the bird will require conservation efforts in both its breeding and wintering ranges.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Researchers have found that the cerulean warbler's population decline is caused entirely by forest loss in North America.","Cerulean warblers spend the winter in a narrow band of mountain forest in South America.","Morales argues that conservation efforts should focus only on the cerulean warbler's wintering range in South America.","Morales's research suggests that conserving the cerulean warbler requires protecting habitat in both North and South America."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Morales's findings about wintering habitat lead him to argue for conservation in both ranges, not only one.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Coral reefs are often called the rainforests of the sea, but their biological richness is puzzling, since the warm tropical waters around them are typically low in nutrients. Marine biologist Sofia Reyes has studied one part of the answer: sponges. Reef sponges, she found, absorb dissolved organic matter released by corals and algae—matter most animals cannot use—and convert it into cells that they shed constantly. Snails, crabs, and other animals eat this sponge debris, so nutrients that would otherwise be lost are passed up the food web.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Sponges are the most numerous animals on coral reefs and are eaten mainly by snails and crabs.","Coral reefs thrive because the warm tropical waters around them are unusually rich in nutrients.","By converting organic matter that most animals can't use into food for others, sponges help explain how reefs thrive in nutrient-poor water.","Reyes found that sponges compete with corals and algae for the dissolved organic matter in reef waters."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Reyes found that sponges recycle unusable organic matter into food, helping explain reef productivity despite nutrient-poor water.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The Swahili coast of East Africa was home to prosperous trading cities such as Kilwa and Mombasa from about 1000 CE onward. Early European visitors often assumed that the cities had been founded by Arab or Persian settlers, since their merchants practiced Islam and traded across the Indian Ocean. Archaeological evidence tells a different story: the cities grew out of earlier African farming and fishing villages, and their residents spoke Swahili, a Bantu language that absorbed many Arabic words through trade.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The Swahili coast's trading cities were founded by Arab and Persian settlers who practiced Islam and traded widely.","Archaeological evidence suggests that the Swahili trading cities developed from local African communities rather than being founded by foreign settlers.","Swahili is an Arabic language that absorbed many Bantu words through trade with African communities.","Early European visitors to East Africa studied the archaeology of the Swahili coast in detail."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text contrasts Europeans' assumption with evidence that the cities grew from African villages. Swahili is described as Bantu, not Arabic.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In the experiment, researchers placed two groups of mice in identical enclosures. One group was given running wheels; the other was not. After a month, the researchers taught both groups to find a hidden platform in a pool of water. The mice with running wheels learned the task faster and, when examined, had grown significantly more new neurons in the hippocampus, a region involved in memory. When the researchers blocked the growth of new neurons in a third group of running mice, those mice learned no faster than the mice without wheels.$q$, $q$Based on the text, what did the researchers' experiment with the third group of mice suggest?$q$, $q$["the benefit of running for learning appeared to depend on the growth of new neurons.","mice that ran on wheels learned to find the hidden platform more slowly than other mice.","the growth of new neurons in the hippocampus slowed learning in the running mice.","running mice learned faster whether or not new neurons grew in their hippocampus."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When neuron growth was blocked, running no longer helped, indicating the benefit depended on new neurons.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$When the writer Mohsin Ali returned to the village where he had spent his childhood, he found that the river he remembered as wide and fast had shrunk to a muddy trickle. Upstream, a new dam diverted water to irrigate cotton fields. In his essay about the visit, Ali does not condemn the dam outright—he acknowledges that it has brought prosperity to many farmers—but he mourns the loss of the river's role in village life: children no longer swim in it, and the fishermen who once supplied the market have moved away.$q$, $q$Based on the text, how does Ali feel about the dam?$q$, $q$["He is angry at the farmers who benefit from the dam and believes it should be removed.","He is indifferent to the dam and focuses instead on describing his childhood in the village.","He is pleased that the dam has made the river safer for children to swim in.","He has mixed feelings, recognizing the dam's benefits while regretting what the village has lost."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Ali acknowledges prosperity but mourns the river's lost role—ambivalence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The red-eyed tree frog lays its eggs on leaves hanging over ponds. When the tadpoles are ready, they hatch and drop into the water. Biologist Karen Warkentin discovered that the embryos can hatch early, up to a third sooner than normal, when they sense danger. If a snake begins to eat a clutch, the vibrations it creates trigger the embryos to hatch within seconds and fall into the water below. Remarkably, the embryos can distinguish the vibrations of a predator from those caused by rain or wind, which do not trigger early hatching.$q$, $q$According to the text, what is true about red-eyed tree frog embryos?$q$, $q$["They hatch early whenever they experience vibrations caused by rain or wind.","They hatch only after they have dropped from the leaves into the water below.","They can detect specific vibrations associated with predators and hatch early in response.","They are unable to hatch until they reach their normal hatching age."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Embryos hatch early in response to predator vibrations but not rain or wind.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many historians have described the eighteenth-century coffeehouses of London as places where people of different social classes mingled and debated freely. Historian Rachel Lam argues that this picture is somewhat idealized. Admission was cheap, she acknowledges, and patrons included tradesmen as well as aristocrats. But the records she studied show that many coffeehouses catered to particular groups—merchants, lawyers, or members of a single political party—and that women, apart from those who worked there, were largely excluded.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Lam argues that coffeehouses were open only to aristocrats and excluded tradesmen entirely.","Lam argues that coffeehouses were less open and socially mixed than they are often portrayed.","Lam argues that coffeehouses charged high admission fees that kept most people away.","Lam argues that women were the most frequent patrons of eighteenth-century coffeehouses."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Lam acknowledges some openness but stresses that many coffeehouses served particular groups and excluded women.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The term "invasive species" usually refers to plants and animals that are introduced to new regions and spread rapidly, harming native ecosystems. Ecologist Mark Davis argues that the term is sometimes applied too broadly. Many introduced species, he notes, cause little or no harm, and some provide benefits—such as nectar for pollinators or habitat for birds. Davis suggests that management efforts should focus on species that cause measurable damage, rather than on all species simply because they are not native.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Davis argues that efforts to manage introduced species should be guided by evidence of harm rather than by origin alone.","Davis argues that all introduced species cause measurable damage to the ecosystems they invade.","Davis argues that native species provide fewer benefits to pollinators than introduced species do.","Davis argues that the term \"invasive species\" should be used to describe all species that are not native."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Davis says many introduced species are harmless or helpful and that management should target those that cause damage.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In her studies of sleep, neuroscientist Yuki Mori found that people who were taught a new skill—such as a sequence of finger movements—performed it better the next day if they had slept in between, even without further practice. Brain scans showed that during sleep, the brain regions active during learning were reactivated in a pattern resembling the original practice. Mori concludes that the brain continues to work on newly learned skills during sleep, strengthening them.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Mori found that people who practiced a skill before sleeping performed it worse the next day.","Mori found that brain regions active during learning are inactive during sleep.","Mori argues that practicing a skill is unnecessary as long as a person sleeps well.","Mori's research suggests that sleep helps strengthen newly learned skills as the brain replays them."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Performance improved after sleep, and learning regions reactivated, suggesting sleep strengthens skills.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a short story. Mr. Okonkwo has run the corner bookstore for forty years.<br><br>People told him that nobody bought books anymore, that he should sell coffee or phone cases or close altogether. He listened politely and changed nothing. Every morning he unlocked the door at eight, swept the step, and set out the cart of secondhand paperbacks at a dollar each. Some days no one came. But on the days someone did—a girl looking for poetry, an old man hunting a novel he had read as a boy—he felt the whole shop come awake around them.$q$, $q$Based on the text, what is true about Mr. Okonkwo?$q$, $q$["He agrees with people who say he should change his business but cannot afford to make changes.","He is bitter that so few customers visit his bookstore and plans to close it soon.","He remains committed to his bookstore despite others' doubts because of the moments of connection it provides.","He is eager to begin selling coffee and phone cases to attract more customers."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$He ignores advice to change, keeps his routine, and cherishes the visits that make the shop "come awake."$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many people believe that goldfish have a memory span of only a few seconds. Experiments have shown otherwise. In one study, goldfish were trained to push a lever to receive food, but only during a particular hour each day. The fish learned to push the lever only during that hour and remembered the schedule for weeks. Other studies have shown that goldfish can learn to respond to specific sounds and can remember them for months.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Goldfish can remember information for only a few seconds, as experiments have confirmed.","Experiments show that goldfish can remember information for much longer than is commonly believed.","Goldfish are able to learn new tasks only if they are rewarded with food.","Goldfish remember sounds for a few weeks but forget schedules after a few seconds."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The experiments refute the few-second myth, showing memory lasting weeks or months.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In the decades after World War II, many American cities built highways through their downtown areas, often demolishing older neighborhoods to make room. Planners believed the highways would revive struggling downtowns by making them easier to reach. Historian Amanda Ruiz argues that the effect was often the opposite: the highways made it easier for residents to move to the suburbs, cut neighborhoods off from one another, and replaced lively streets with noisy, unwalkable corridors. Several cities have since removed downtown highways and seen nearby areas revive.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Ruiz argues that downtown highways often harmed the cities they were meant to help.","Ruiz argues that downtown highways successfully revived struggling downtowns after World War II.","Ruiz argues that cities should build more highways in order to encourage people to live in suburbs.","Ruiz argues that planners in the decades after World War II did not intend to revive downtowns."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ruiz says the highways had the opposite of their intended effect, harming downtowns.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists studying the ancient city of Çatalhöyük in present-day Turkey, inhabited about 9,000 years ago, found that its houses were packed so tightly together that there were no streets; residents entered their homes through openings in the roofs. The houses were remarkably similar in size, and burials beneath their floors contained similar goods. Archaeologist Ian Hodder argues that these findings suggest a society with little difference in wealth or status among households.$q$, $q$Based on the text, what does Hodder conclude about Çatalhöyük?$q$, $q$["Residents of Çatalhöyük entered their homes through doors that opened onto narrow streets.","Wealthy residents of Çatalhöyük were buried with more goods than other residents.","Çatalhöyük had many streets because its houses were spread far apart from one another.","The similarity of houses and burials suggests that Çatalhöyük had relatively little inequality."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Similar houses and burials point to little difference in wealth or status. The other choices contradict the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In many bird species, males are more brightly colored than females. In the eclectus parrot, however, both sexes are vividly colored, but in completely different ways: males are bright green, while females are red and purple. Biologist Robert Heinsohn found that the colors reflect each sex's role. Males, who forage widely in the forest canopy, benefit from green camouflage that hides them from hawks. Females spend much of the year guarding nest holes, where their bright colors may help them advertise ownership of scarce nesting sites to rival females.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Female eclectus parrots are brightly colored in order to attract males while foraging in the canopy.","Male eclectus parrots are more brightly colored than females, as in most other bird species.","The different colors of male and female eclectus parrots appear to suit the different pressures each sex faces.","Both male and female eclectus parrots use green camouflage to hide from hawks."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Heinsohn links each sex's color to its role: males need camouflage while foraging; females advertise nest ownership.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$When the musician Leah Park began composing music for video games, she found that she had to unlearn some habits from writing film scores. A film score, she explains, accompanies scenes whose length and order are fixed. In a game, players control the pace: they may linger in one area for an hour or rush through it in seconds. Park now writes music in layers that can be added or removed as a player's situation changes, so the score adapts seamlessly to whatever the player does.$q$, $q$According to the text, why did Park change how she composes music?$q$, $q$["Park writes music for films in layers that can be added or removed as scenes change.","Park adapted her approach because game music must respond to players' unpredictable actions.","Park believes that film scores are more difficult to compose than music for video games.","Park composes game music that lasts exactly as long as each area of the game."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Because players control pace, Park writes layered music that adapts to their actions.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The Atacama Desert in Chile is so dry that some weather stations there have never recorded rain. Yet after a rare heavy rainfall in 2015, parts of the desert burst into bloom with millions of flowers. Botanists explain that seeds of many desert plants can remain dormant in the soil for years or even decades, waiting for enough rain to germinate. When the rain finally comes, the plants grow, flower, and produce new seeds within a few weeks, before the soil dries out again.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Desert plants' seeds can stay dormant for long periods and then rapidly complete their life cycle when rain arrives.","The Atacama Desert receives heavy rainfall every year, causing millions of flowers to bloom regularly.","Botanists have found that desert plants produce new seeds only once every few decades.","Seeds in the Atacama Desert germinate only in years when no rain falls at all."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bloom is explained by long dormancy and a rapid life cycle triggered by rain.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many historians once believed that the Little Ice Age—a period of cooler temperatures from roughly 1300 to 1850—affected the whole world uniformly. Recent studies using tree rings, ice cores, and historical records from many regions show that the cooling was uneven: it began at different times in different places, and some regions experienced little cooling at all. Climate scientists now describe the Little Ice Age as a collection of regional cool periods rather than a single global event.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The Little Ice Age began at the same time everywhere and lasted from 1300 to 1850.","Tree rings and ice cores show that no region experienced cooling during the Little Ice Age.","Historians have always described the Little Ice Age as a collection of regional cool periods.","New evidence indicates that the Little Ice Age varied by region rather than affecting the world uniformly."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Recent evidence shows uneven, regional cooling, revising the uniform view.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In 2010, a team of researchers began studying why some neighborhoods in a large city had much higher rates of childhood asthma than others. They found that the neighborhoods with the highest rates were near major highways and industrial sites, where air pollution was greatest. However, they also found that within these neighborhoods, children who lived in apartments with mold or pests had higher rates than their neighbors. The researchers concluded that both outdoor pollution and indoor conditions contributed to the differences.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The researchers found that childhood asthma rates were highest in neighborhoods far from highways.","The researchers concluded that indoor conditions had no effect on rates of childhood asthma.","The researchers found that both outdoor and indoor factors were linked to childhood asthma rates.","The researchers found that air pollution was greatest in apartments with mold or pests."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rates were linked both to proximity to pollution and to mold or pests at home.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The novelist's first book, published when she was twenty-three, was a sprawling family saga set across four generations. Critics praised its ambition but complained that it had too many characters. Her second book, published a decade later, focuses on a single afternoon in the life of one woman. In interviews, the novelist has said that she learned more from the criticism of her first book than from its praise.$q$, $q$Based on the text, what is most likely true about the novelist?$q$, $q$["The novelist's second book is a family saga set across four generations, like her first book.","The novelist appears to have responded to criticism of her first book by writing a more focused second book.","Critics complained that the novelist's second book had too many characters.","The novelist has said that she learned more from praise than from criticism of her first book."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After criticism of too many characters, her second book focuses on one woman for one afternoon, and she credits the criticism.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Bats that hunt insects locate their prey by echolocation, emitting high-pitched calls and listening for echoes. Some moths have evolved defenses: tiger moths, for example, produce clicking sounds that interfere with bats' echolocation, causing the bats to miss. Biologist Aaron Corcoran found that bats can counter this defense. In experiments, some bats changed the timing of their calls when hunting tiger moths, reducing the moths' ability to jam their signals.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The interactions between bats and tiger moths show an ongoing contest of defenses and counterdefenses.","Tiger moths produce clicking sounds that help bats locate them more easily in the dark.","Bats are unable to catch tiger moths because the moths' clicks always jam their signals.","Corcoran found that bats stop using echolocation when they hunt tiger moths."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Moths jam bat calls, and bats adjust timing in response—escalating defenses and counterdefenses.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The city of Kyoto, Japan, faced a problem common to popular tourist destinations: crowds at famous temples and shrines were straining local infrastructure and frustrating residents. Rather than limiting the number of visitors, city officials promoted lesser-known sites and encouraged tourists to visit early in the morning or in less popular seasons. Within a few years, visits to several previously quiet neighborhoods increased, while crowding at the most famous sites eased somewhat.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Kyoto limited the number of tourists allowed to visit its famous temples and shrines.","Kyoto's residents encouraged tourists to visit the city only during its most popular seasons.","Kyoto's efforts caused crowding at its most famous sites to increase sharply.","Kyoto addressed overcrowding by spreading visitors across more places and times rather than reducing their numbers."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Kyoto redistributed visitors across sites and times rather than capping numbers.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying elephants in Kenya found that the animals can distinguish between the voices of different human groups. When the researchers played recordings of men from the Maasai community, who have sometimes killed elephants in conflicts over grazing land, the elephants retreated and bunched together defensively. When they played recordings of men from the Kamba community, who rarely come into conflict with elephants, the elephants showed much less concern. Recordings of Maasai women and boys also produced little reaction.$q$, $q$According to the text, what did the researchers find?$q$, $q$["The elephants responded with alarm to recordings of all human voices, regardless of the speaker.","The elephants were more alarmed by recordings of Maasai women than by recordings of Maasai men.","The elephants responded differently to human voices depending on how much of a threat the speakers likely posed.","The elephants were unable to tell the difference between the voices of Maasai and Kamba men."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Elephants reacted defensively to Maasai men but little to Kamba men, women, or boys—matching threat levels.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$For many years, the standard method of restoring old paintings involved removing yellowed varnish with strong solvents, which sometimes damaged the paint beneath. Conservator Elena Rossi has helped develop gels that hold solvents in place, allowing them to act only on the surface layer. The gels can be applied precisely, left for a measured time, and lifted away, removing grime and varnish while leaving the original paint untouched.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Rossi argues that old paintings should never be cleaned because cleaning always damages them.","Rossi has helped develop a more precise and less damaging way of cleaning old paintings.","Rossi has developed new varnishes that prevent old paintings from turning yellow.","Rossi uses strong solvents to remove the original paint from old paintings."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The gels confine solvents to the surface, cleaning without harming paint—more precise and safer.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In the early 1900s, the Arctic explorer Matthew Henson accompanied Robert Peary on several expeditions toward the North Pole. Henson, who was Black, learned the Inuit language, built sledges, and trained dog teams, skills that were essential to the expeditions' survival. When Peary announced in 1909 that they had reached the Pole, Peary received honors and fame, while Henson's role was largely ignored. Only decades later did Henson receive official recognition for his contributions.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Henson made essential contributions to Arctic expeditions but did not receive recognition for them until long afterward.","Henson and Peary both received honors immediately after announcing that they had reached the North Pole in 1909.","Peary learned the Inuit language and trained dog teams, which made the expeditions successful.","Henson's contributions to Arctic exploration were limited to building sledges for the expeditions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Henson's skills were essential, yet he was overlooked for decades.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Mara Lindgren hypothesizes that urban populations of a songbird, the European blackbird, have shifted their breeding season earlier than rural populations because artificial lights in cities extend the apparent length of the day, triggering hormonal changes that prepare the birds to breed.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Lindgren's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Urban blackbirds have access to more food in winter than rural blackbirds do, because people put out bird feeders.","Rural blackbirds that were moved to cities began breeding earlier only after several years of living there.","Urban blackbirds that nest near streetlights produce larger clutches of eggs than urban blackbirds that nest in darker parts of the same parks.","Urban blackbirds kept in enclosures shielded from artificial light at night began breeding at the same time as rural blackbirds."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$If shielding urban birds from artificial light eliminated the early breeding, that would directly link the shift to artificial light. Extra food offers a competing explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Historian Paul Kessler argues that the decline of a medieval trading town was caused mainly by the silting of its harbor, which prevented large ships from reaching the town's docks after about 1450.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Kessler's argument?$q$, $q$["Records show that the town's population was growing steadily until about 1450, after which many merchant families moved to a rival port nearby.","The town's merchants began building smaller, flat-bottomed ships after 1450, though these ships carried far less cargo than the earlier vessels.","Records show that the town's trade with other ports remained steady until 1450 and then fell sharply, while trade over land routes continued unchanged.","A neighboring town with a deeper harbor also declined sharply after 1450 for reasons unrelated to its harbor."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A sharp drop in sea trade after 1450 while land trade continued points specifically to a harbor problem. The other findings don't connect decline to the harbor or even undercut the argument.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A team of researchers claims that a new reading program improves the reading comprehension of elementary school students. In their study, students who used the program for one school year scored higher on a comprehension test than students who did not.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["Students who used the program reported enjoying reading more than students who did not use the program.","Students who used the program had already scored higher on the comprehension test than the other students before the school year began.","Teachers who used the program said that their students seemed more engaged during reading lessons than students in previous years had been.","Students who used the program spent about the same amount of time reading each week as students who did not use the program."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If the program group already scored higher before using it, their higher end-of-year scores may not reflect the program, weakening the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Linguist Sora Park argues that young children learn the meanings of new words more effectively from live interaction with adults than from watching video, even when the video shows the same adult saying the same words.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Park's argument?$q$, $q$["Toddlers who learned words from an adult in person remembered more of them a week later than toddlers who watched a video of that adult saying the words.","Toddlers who watched videos of adults saying words were more attentive during the lessons than toddlers who learned in person.","Older children learned words equally well from live adults and from videos of those adults, though toddlers were not tested in the video condition.","Toddlers who learned words from live adults they already knew remembered more words than toddlers who learned from adults who were strangers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This compares the two conditions with the same adult and finds better retention live—directly supporting Park's claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists studying an ancient settlement have proposed that its residents abandoned it because of a prolonged drought around 1200 CE.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the archaeologists' proposal?$q$, $q$["Pottery found in the settlement's most recent layers was decorated with images of rain and clouds, which were rare in older pottery.","The settlement's population grew rapidly between 1100 and 1150 CE, when many new houses were built near its largest spring.","Residents of the settlement stored grain in large underground pits, some of which were found empty when the site was excavated.","Pollen from lake sediments near the settlement shows that drought-tolerant plants replaced water-loving species around 1200 CE."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A shift to drought-tolerant plants at that time is direct environmental evidence of drought.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Economist Ruth Dalton argues that a city's new tax on sugary drinks reduced consumption of those drinks among city residents.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Dalton's argument?$q$, $q$["Sales of sugary drinks in stores inside the city fell by nearly 40 percent in the year after the tax took effect.","The tax raised millions of dollars, which the city used to fund nutrition programs and free drinking-water stations in schools.","Sales of sugary drinks just outside the city rose after the tax by about as much as sales inside the city fell.","In surveys, many city residents said that they had cut back on sugary drinks since the tax took effect."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If residents simply bought drinks just outside the city, overall consumption may not have fallen, weakening Dalton's argument.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Neuroscientist Hana Oduya hypothesizes that the improvement in memory that people experience after sleep results specifically from a type of brain activity called sleep spindles, brief bursts of electrical activity that occur during certain stages of sleep.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Oduya's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Participants who slept for eight hours after learning remembered more the next day than participants who slept for only four hours.","Participants whose sleep spindles were increased with gentle sound stimulation showed greater memory improvement than participants who slept without stimulation.","Sleep spindles occur more frequently in young adults than in older adults, who also tend to show less memory improvement after sleep.","Participants who napped after learning remembered more than participants who stayed awake, and the nappers spent much of the nap in spindle-rich sleep."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Experimentally increasing spindles and seeing greater gains isolates spindles' role. More sleep overall doesn't single out spindles.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Art historian Teresa Wells claims that a painting long attributed to a seventeenth-century Dutch artist was actually painted in the nineteenth century by an imitator.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Wells's claim?$q$, $q$["Chemical analysis shows that the painting contains a white pigment that was not manufactured until the 1830s.","The painting depicts a scene almost identical to one in a well-documented seventeenth-century Dutch painting held by another museum.","The painting was purchased in the 1890s from an Amsterdam dealer who is known to have sold several forgeries of older works.","The canvas of the painting is made from linen, as were most canvases in the seventeenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A pigment unavailable until the 1830s means the painting must postdate that—directly supporting a nineteenth-century origin.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A biologist claims that a species of desert lizard regulates its body temperature by changing color: darkening in cool mornings to absorb heat and lightening in the afternoon to reflect sunlight.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the biologist's claim?$q$, $q$["Lizards in the desert were darker in the mornings than in the afternoons, when they spent more time in the shade.","Lizards kept in a laboratory at a constant temperature still changed color at the same times each day as wild lizards.","Lizards of the species are darker overall than lizards of related species that live in cooler forests.","Lizards placed in a cool chamber at midday darkened within minutes, while those placed in a warm chamber in the morning lightened."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Color tracking temperature even when the time of day is mismatched directly links color to temperature rather than to a daily rhythm. A daily color cycle at constant temperature would point to a rhythm instead, and a simple morning/afternoon difference could reflect either cause.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Psychologist Andre Lewis argues that people are more likely to donate to a charity when they are shown a photo of a single person in need than when they are shown statistics about many people in need.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Lewis's argument?$q$, $q$["Participants shown statistics about millions of children rated the problem as more serious than participants shown a photo of one child did.","Participants who were shown a photo of a single child said that they found the charity's appeal more emotionally moving than other appeals.","Participants shown a photo of one child donated significantly more than participants shown statistics about millions of children facing the same hardship.","Participants shown statistics were better able to recall the details of the charity's mission."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$This directly compares a single-person photo with statistics and shows higher giving for the photo.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have proposed that a sharp increase in a coastal town's flooding in recent decades was caused mainly by rising sea levels.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researchers' proposal?$q$, $q$["Over the same decades, the town's population nearly doubled, and many residents moved to newer neighborhoods on higher ground.","Over those decades, groundwater pumping made the land sink over a meter, while local sea level rose only a few centimeters.","Sea levels have risen along many coastlines around the world in recent decades, including the coastline where the town is located.","Residents of the town reported that flooding was most common during storms that coincided with unusually high tides."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If land sinking far exceeded sea-level rise, subsidence—not sea level—is likely the main cause, weakening the proposal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the poem "Harbor Lights" by Inés Calderón, the speaker expresses a longing for a home that she knows she can no longer return to.$q$, $q$Which quotation from "Harbor Lights" most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"Each night I watch the ferry leave / for shores I'll never walk again, / and name each light as if it were / a window I once looked out from.\"","\"The harbor smells of salt and tar, / the gulls complain about the rain, / the fishermen unload their nets / and argue over what they've caught.\"","\"Tomorrow I will buy a ticket, / pack my coat and leave this town, / and see again the lemon trees / that grow outside my mother's door.\"","\"I never cared for distant places; / the harbor here is all I need, / its lights enough to read my letters, / its noise enough to help me sleep.\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The "Each night I watch the ferry leave" quotation shows longing (naming lights as remembered windows) and the impossibility of return ("shores I'll never walk again"). The "Tomorrow I will buy a ticket" quotation suggests she can return.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Economist Jonah Rivera claims that a minimum wage increase in a particular state did not lead to job losses in the restaurant industry.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Rivera's claim?$q$, $q$["Many restaurant owners in the state raised menu prices after the minimum wage increased, according to a survey of owners.","Restaurant owners in the state said in surveys before the increase that they expected to hire fewer workers once it took effect.","Restaurant workers in the state reported higher job satisfaction after the increase.","Employment in restaurants in the state rose at about the same rate as in a neighboring state where the minimum wage did not change."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Comparable employment growth with a control state indicates no relative job loss.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A team of ecologists hypothesizes that wolves reduce the number of deer in a forest mainly by changing deer behavior—making them avoid open areas—rather than by killing large numbers of them.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the ecologists' hypothesis?$q$, $q$["In areas where wolves returned, wolves killed about a third of the deer population each year, and deer numbers fell sharply.","Deer in areas with wolves were smaller on average than deer in areas without wolves, and they gave birth to fewer fawns each year.","Where wolves returned, deer numbers held steady, but deer avoided open meadows, where young trees then recovered.","Young trees in open meadows recovered in areas where wolves returned, but only after deer numbers there had fallen by half."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The finding that deer numbers held steady while behavior changed supports a behavioral effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the novel The Weaver's Daughter by Helena Marsh, the narrator often presents herself as indifferent to her sister's success, but the novel suggests that she is deeply envious.$q$, $q$Which quotation from The Weaver's Daughter most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"Clara's loom was the finest in the village, and everyone who saw her cloth agreed that no one had ever woven anything so fine.\"","\"I told Clara I was glad for her, and I was, mostly; I simply could not stay in the room while the others raised their glasses to her.\"","\"I have never wanted what Clara wanted; our paths were different from the start, and I have made my peace with mine.\"","\"When Clara won the prize, I cheered louder than anyone and carried her ribbon home myself.\""]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The narrator claims gladness but can't bear the toasts—revealing envy beneath stated indifference. The "I have never wanted what Clara wanted" quotation only states indifference.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Geologist Erin Cho claims that a series of small earthquakes beneath a region was caused by the injection of wastewater from oil production deep underground, rather than by natural movement along faults.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Cho's claim?$q$, $q$["The earthquakes began shortly after injection started, clustered near the injection wells, and declined after injection was reduced.","The region has experienced occasional small earthquakes for thousands of years, though their frequency has varied over time.","Oil production in the region increased steadily throughout the twentieth century, and wastewater injection began in the 1990s.","The earthquakes occurred along faults that geologists had already mapped before wastewater injection began in the region."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Timing, location, and decline after reduced injection link quakes directly to injection.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a new type of bike lane—separated from traffic by a concrete barrier—made cycling safer in a city.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["After the lanes were built, the total number of cycling injuries on the affected streets remained about the same as before.","After the lanes were built, cyclists surveyed on the affected streets said they felt much safer riding there than before.","The new lanes were built on the streets that had previously had the most cycling injuries in the entire city.","On the affected streets, cycling injuries per mile traveled fell by half, even as the number of cyclists doubled."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Injuries per mile fell sharply even as cycling grew—a direct safety improvement. Total injuries unchanged is ambiguous given more cyclists.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian argues that a particular medieval manuscript was copied by a single scribe over a short period of time.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["The manuscript contains several errors that were corrected in the margins in a handwriting that matches the main text.","The manuscript was owned by a single monastery for several centuries, according to inscriptions on its first and last pages.","The handwriting changes style partway through the manuscript, and the ink in later sections differs chemically.","The ink used throughout the manuscript has the same chemical composition from the first page to the last."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Changing hands and different ink suggest multiple scribes or a long period, weakening the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Marine biologist Leo Santos hypothesizes that a species of reef fish recognizes individual members of its own species by the unique patterns of spots on their faces.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Santos's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["The fish have unusually sharp eyesight and can detect fine patterns that are invisible to most other species of reef fish.","When shown images of familiar fish with their facial spots digitally rearranged, the fish reacted as if the images showed strangers.","The fish live in stable groups of about twenty individuals that stay together for years and defend a shared territory.","The fish respond aggressively to unfamiliar fish of their own species but not to familiar members of their group."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Rearranging spots made familiar fish seem unfamiliar—direct evidence that spots are used for recognition.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the short story "The Last Orchard" by Daniel Arce, the narrator's grandfather is portrayed as stubborn and unwilling to adapt to change.$q$, $q$Which quotation from "The Last Orchard" most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"Offered new trees that would bear twice the fruit, he thanked the co-op and planted his father's old variety.\"","\"He rose before dawn every morning to walk the rows, checking each tree for signs of frost or disease.\"","\"He taught me to graft branches when I was seven, guiding my hands until the cuts were clean.\"","\"In good years, he gave away more apples than he sold, filling the neighbors' baskets without being asked.\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Declining more productive trees to keep the old variety shows resistance to change. The other quotations show diligence, teaching, or generosity.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Anthropologist Kenji Morita argues that a coastal community's traditional fishing rules—which forbid fishing in certain areas during spawning seasons—have helped maintain local fish populations.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Morita's argument?$q$, $q$["Members of the community say that the rules were established by their ancestors centuries ago in response to a period of poor catches.","Fish populations in areas covered by the rules rise and fall from year to year, as do populations in nearby areas without similar rules.","Fishing boats in the community are smaller than those used in nearby commercial fleets, which catch far more fish each year.","Fish populations in areas covered by the rules have remained stable for decades, while those in nearby areas without similar rules have declined."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Stable populations where rules apply versus declines elsewhere directly support the rules' effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a new drug slows the progression of a degenerative disease. In a trial, patients taking the drug declined more slowly than patients taking a placebo.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["Patients who took the drug reported mild side effects such as headaches more often than patients who took the placebo.","The drug was tested on more than five hundred patients over two years at hospitals in several different countries.","Patients given the drug were ten years younger on average than those given the placebo, and the disease progresses more slowly in the young.","Patients who took the drug and patients who took the placebo were similar in age, health, and stage of the disease."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If drug-group patients were younger and younger patients decline more slowly, age—not the drug—may explain the result.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Musicologist Ana Lima argues that a composer's late works were influenced by the folk music of the region where she spent her final years.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Lima's argument?$q$, $q$["The composer's late works were performed more often than her early works, especially at festivals in the region where she lived.","Several melodies in the late works closely match local folk songs, and her letters mention festivals where such songs were sung.","Folk music in the region uses a scale that also appears in the music of several composers who never lived there.","The composer moved to the region in her final years and wrote that she found its landscapes deeply inspiring."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Matching melodies plus documented exposure provide direct evidence of influence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Ravi Menon claims that the invasive plant he studied spreads mainly through seeds carried by birds rather than by wind.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Menon's claim?$q$, $q$["New patches of the plant appear most often beneath trees where birds perch, rather than in open areas downwind of existing patches.","The plant produces thousands of small, light seeds each year, and new patches often appear far from existing ones.","Birds in the region eat the plant's fruits only rarely, preferring the fruits of native shrubs that ripen at the same time of year.","New patches of the plant appear most often in sunny clearings, which are also where wind speeds are highest."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Patches clustering under perches rather than downwind support bird dispersal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A team of sociologists claims that residents of neighborhoods with more parks report higher levels of trust in their neighbors.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the sociologists' claim?$q$, $q$["Residents who live near parks report spending more time outdoors than residents who live far from parks.","The city added three new parks in the past decade, mostly in neighborhoods where residents already reported high levels of trust.","Residents of wealthier neighborhoods, which tend to have more parks, report higher trust in their neighbors.","Residents living within a five-minute walk of a park were more likely to trust their neighbors, even after accounting for income."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$This directly links park proximity to neighbor trust while controlling for income.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Primatologist Yara Costa argues that capuchin monkeys in one population learned to crack nuts with stones by observing older group members, rather than discovering the technique independently.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Costa's argument?$q$, $q$["Young capuchins in the population first tried cracking nuts at about the same age, whether or not skilled adults were nearby at the time.","Capuchins in a distant population that had no contact with the first group also crack nuts with stones.","Young capuchins that spent more time watching skilled adults crack nuts mastered the technique sooner than young capuchins that watched less.","Young capuchins that learned to crack nuts had spent about as much time watching skilled adults as young capuchins that never learned."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A link between watching skilled adults and faster mastery supports social learning. Independent invention elsewhere would, if anything, weaken the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A city official claims that installing speed cameras on a busy avenue reduced the number of serious crashes there.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the official's claim?$q$, $q$["Drivers on the avenue received thousands of speeding tickets in the first year after the cameras were installed, more than in any previous year.","Serious crashes on the avenue had already been falling steadily for five years before the cameras were installed, at the same rate as afterward.","Average driving speeds on the avenue fell after the cameras were installed, according to measurements taken by the city.","Speed cameras were installed on several other avenues in the city at the same time."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If crashes were already falling at the same rate, the cameras may not have caused the decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the poem "Winter Field" by Tomas Linde, the speaker finds unexpected beauty in a landscape that most people would consider bleak.$q$, $q$Which quotation from "Winter Field" most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"The field is bare, the fences gray, / and yet the frost on every stalk / holds the low sun like a jewel / that no one else has stopped to see.\"","\"I crossed the field in early March / and longed for green, for any green, / for any sign the cold would end / and give the farmers back their land.\"","\"The neighbors say the field is cursed, / that nothing grows there anymore; / they hurry past it after dark / and do not look across the snow.\"","\"In summer, wheat stood shoulder high / and larks rose singing from the rows; / the field was gold from fence to fence / and everyone stopped by to see.\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The first quotation acknowledges the bleakness ("bare," "gray") and finds beauty in the frost. The others express longing, others' fear, or summer beauty.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Agricultural scientist Grace Wu hypothesizes that planting clover between rows of corn increases corn yields by adding nitrogen to the soil.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Wu's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Corn grown with clover was taller in early summer and produced higher yields at harvest than corn grown alone in the same fields.","Clover planted between rows of corn reduced the number of weeds, which compete with corn for nitrogen and other nutrients in the soil.","Farmers who planted clover between rows of corn spent less on herbicides.","Corn grown with clover produced higher yields than corn grown alone, but only in fields where soil nitrogen was initially low."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Yield gains only where nitrogen was low point to nitrogen as the mechanism. Weed suppression offers a different mechanism.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a particular bird species migrates by following Earth's magnetic field rather than by using the positions of the stars.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["Birds of the species migrate mostly at night and become noticeably disoriented on cloudy nights, when the stars are hidden from view.","Birds of the species follow the same routes each year and stop to rest at the same sites along the way.","In a planetarium, birds ignored rotated stars and kept their usual heading, but turned when a surrounding magnetic field was shifted.","Young birds raised indoors under an artificial night sky oriented correctly only after the artificial stars were switched on."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ignoring rotated stars but responding to shifted magnetic fields isolates the magnetic cue.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Historian Daniel Moss argues that a nineteenth-century novelist based her depiction of factory life on firsthand observation rather than on secondhand accounts.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Moss's argument?$q$, $q$["The novel was widely read by factory owners when it was published, and several of them praised its accurate portrayal of their mills.","The novelist's diaries record visits to textile mills, with details of machines and workers' talk that reappear almost verbatim in the novel.","Many other novels of the period depicted factory life, often relying on newspaper reports of conditions in the mills.","The novelist was born in a city with many textile mills and lived there until she was in her twenties."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Diary entries matching the novel's details directly show firsthand observation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A nutrition researcher claims that eating breakfast improves students' concentration in morning classes.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Students who were randomly assigned to eat breakfast performed no better on morning attention tests than students who were randomly assigned to skip it.","Students who eat breakfast tend to score higher on morning attention tests than students who skip it, but they also tend to sleep more.","Many students say that they feel hungry and have trouble focusing during morning classes on days when they skip breakfast.","Schools that serve free breakfast to all students report fewer students visiting the nurse with headaches in the morning."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A randomized comparison finding no difference directly undercuts the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the novel The Glass Coast by Rina Patel, the protagonist, a young fisherman named Arun, is portrayed as torn between loyalty to his family and his desire to leave the village.$q$, $q$Which quotation from The Glass Coast most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"Arun was the best swimmer in the village, and his father often said that the sea itself had chosen him to be a fisherman.\"","\"Arun had no interest in the city; he loved the sea and could not imagine living anywhere else.\"","\"Arun's father taught him to read the weather from the clouds over the bay, and Arun never once doubted his father's judgment.\"","\"Each evening Arun mended nets beside his father, and each evening he watched the city bus pull away without him.\""]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Mending nets with his father while watching the city bus leave captures both loyalty and longing.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Hassan Diallo argues that the decline of a frog species in a mountain region was caused primarily by a fungal disease rather than by habitat loss.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Diallo's argument?$q$, $q$["The frog species lays its eggs in streams, and water quality in the region's streams has declined in areas near cleared forests.","Forests in the region have been cleared for farming at a steady rate for decades, and frogs are now absent from many cleared areas.","Frogs declined as sharply in protected parks as in cleared areas, and dead frogs in both places carried the fungus.","The fungus has been found on frogs in the region, including on frogs of species whose populations have remained stable."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Equal declines in protected and cleared areas, with fungus present in both, point to disease over habitat loss.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Psychologist Marta Ruiz claims that people who write down three things they are grateful for each week become happier over time.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Ruiz's claim?$q$, $q$["Participants who wrote gratitude lists each week reported higher well-being after ten weeks than they had at the start of the study.","Participants who wrote weekly gratitude lists reported higher well-being after ten weeks than participants who wrote weekly lists of neutral events.","Participants who were already happy when the study began were more likely to agree to write weekly gratitude lists.","Participants wrote longer lists in the first week of the study than in the last week."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Comparison with a neutral-writing control shows a specific effect of gratitude. Self-selection of happy people would weaken the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologist Leo Brandt argues that a set of stone circles was used as an astronomical calendar.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Brandt's argument?$q$, $q$["Several stones are aligned with the point on the horizon where the sun rises on the summer and winter solstices.","The stones were transported from a quarry more than twenty kilometers away and arranged in circles of nearly identical diameter.","Pottery and animal bones that may have been used in seasonal feasts were found buried near the center of the circles.","The circles were built over several hundred years, and the oldest stones are arranged differently from the newest ones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solstice alignments directly support an astronomical function.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$An economist claims that a city's rent control policy reduced the number of rental units available.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the economist's claim?$q$, $q$["Tenants in rent-controlled units moved less often than tenants in other units and reported higher satisfaction with their housing.","Rents for units not covered by the policy rose after it took effect, as did rents in nearby cities without rent control.","The policy was supported by a majority of tenants in the city.","After the policy began, many landlords converted rental buildings into condominiums, and the city's rental units declined."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Conversions of rentals to condos, with a drop in rental units, directly support the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Linguist Akira Sato hypothesizes that a particular sound change in a language spread from older speakers to younger speakers, rather than the reverse.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Sato's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Younger speakers today use the new pronunciation more often than older speakers do, and older speakers report using it less as they age.","The new pronunciation is more common in cities than in rural areas, and city speakers of all ages use it at about the same rate.","Recordings from several decades show the new pronunciation first among older speakers in the 1970s and only later among younger speakers.","Many speakers are unaware that their pronunciation has changed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Recordings showing the change appearing first among older speakers directly support the direction of spread. Current usage by the young would suggest the opposite.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A team of researchers claims that a new type of roof coating lowers indoor temperatures in summer.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["The coating reflects much more sunlight than standard roofing materials, according to measurements taken on test panels in a laboratory.","In identical buildings, those with the coating had indoor afternoon temperatures several degrees lower than those without it.","Building owners who used the coating reported lower electricity bills in summer, though many also installed new air conditioners.","Building owners who used the coating said they were satisfied with it."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A direct comparison of identical buildings shows lower indoor temperatures.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Historian Sarah Okafor argues that a certain medieval king was more popular among his subjects than later historians have suggested.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Okafor's argument?$q$, $q$["Tax records show that subjects contributed voluntary gifts to the king far more often than they did to his predecessors or successors.","The king commissioned more buildings than any other king of his dynasty, including churches and markets in many of his subjects' towns.","Later historians relied heavily on accounts written by the king's political rivals, who had reason to portray him unfavorably.","The king reigned for more than thirty years, longer than any of his predecessors, and fought several long and costly wars abroad."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Voluntary gifts to the king directly reflect popularity. Reliance on rivals' accounts explains bias but isn't direct evidence of popularity.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Marine scientists claim that a marine reserve, where fishing is banned, has increased the number of fish in nearby fishing areas.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the scientists' claim?$q$, $q$["Fish inside the reserve grew larger and more numerous than fish of the same species in nearby areas where fishing is allowed.","Catches in nearby fishing areas rose after the reserve was established, as did catches in distant areas with no reserve nearby.","Fishers in the region initially opposed the creation of the reserve.","Tagged fish released inside the reserve were often caught in nearby fishing areas, where catches rose after it opened."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Tagged fish moving out and catches rising show spillover benefiting nearby areas.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the essay "Learning to See" by Maya Lindqvist, the author describes how studying painting changed the way she perceives ordinary objects.$q$, $q$Which quotation from "Learning to See" most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"My painting teacher made us redo our sketches several times, insisting that we draw exactly what was in front of us.\"","\"I had always wanted to paint, but I did not take my first lesson until I was forty.\"","\"After a year of drawing lessons, I could no longer look at a coffee cup without noticing the thin crescent of reflected light along its rim.\"","\"The museum near my apartment holds a large collection of still lifes, and I visit it often to study how painters rendered light.\""]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the first quotation describes a change in how she perceives ordinary objects.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Neuroscientist Owen Park claims that playing a musical instrument in childhood leads to lasting improvements in the ability to distinguish speech sounds in noisy environments.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Park's claim?$q$, $q$["Children who play instruments are better at understanding speech in noisy classrooms than children who do not play instruments.","Adults who played an instrument only as children outperformed adults who never played on tests of hearing speech in noise.","Adults who currently play an instrument outperform adults who have never played on tests of understanding speech in noise.","Children who play instruments tend to come from families with higher incomes."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Benefits persisting in adults who stopped playing support a lasting effect. Family income is a confound that would weaken the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a certain species of ant farms fungus by deliberately planting fragments of it in prepared chambers.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["When researchers removed the fungus, ants brought fragments from other nests and set them on beds of chewed leaves, where they grew.","Ants of the species are always found near the fungus in the wild, and the fungus is never found growing outside the ants' nests.","When the fungus in a nest became diseased, ants removed the infected portions and carried them out of the nest.","Ants of the species eat the fungus as their main source of food and feed it to their larvae."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ants actively transplanting fungus onto prepared beds directly demonstrates deliberate planting.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian argues that a famous speech was written by the politician who delivered it rather than by a speechwriter.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["The speech was praised by newspapers for sounding more personal than the politician's other speeches, which were written by staff.","The politician employed several speechwriters during her career, including during the year in which the speech was delivered.","The speech contained several phrases that the politician often used in conversation and in her letters to friends.","Drafts of the speech in the politician's handwriting, with extensive revisions, were found among her personal papers."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Handwritten drafts with revisions are direct evidence of her authorship. Her employing speechwriters would cut against it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Lina Bauer hypothesizes that a species of tree produces chemicals in its leaves that discourage deer from eating them, and that the trees produce more of these chemicals after being browsed.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Bauer's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Deer in the region eat the leaves of many tree species but eat this species' leaves less often than those of most other species.","Trees of the species grow faster in areas without deer, and their leaves there contain about the same levels of the chemicals.","Leaves of browsed trees held more of the chemicals than leaves of unbrowsed trees, and deer avoided them in feeding trials.","The chemicals in the leaves give them a bitter taste, and their levels are similar in browsed and unbrowsed trees."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Higher chemical levels after browsing and avoidance by deer directly support both parts of the hypothesis.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A medical researcher claims that a hospital's new hand-washing program reduced infection rates.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Infection rates at the hospital fell after the program began, and they fell most in the units where staff had received the training first.","During the same period, the hospital also began using new antibacterial surfaces in patient rooms and reduced the average length of patient stays.","Staff members reported washing their hands more often after the program began, according to an anonymous survey.","Other hospitals in the region adopted similar programs."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Simultaneous changes that could also reduce infections make it unclear whether the program caused the decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Musicologist Rosa Ferreira argues that a composer revised her symphony after hearing its first performance, rather than completing it before the premiere.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Ferreira's argument?$q$, $q$["The premiere score differs from the published version in several passages, and letters describe her reworking the finale afterward.","The symphony was performed many times during the composer's lifetime, and audiences at later performances preferred it to the premiere.","The premiere was attended by many leading musicians, several of whom praised the finale in letters written after the concert.","The published version of the symphony is longer than most symphonies of the period, especially in its final movement."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Differences between premiere and published scores plus letters about reworking support post-premiere revision.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the novel The Clockmaker's Apprentice by Jonas Weller, the narrator gradually comes to admire the master he initially resented.$q$, $q$Which quotation from The Clockmaker's Apprentice most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"The master was strict and spoke little, and in my first months I could not understand why he made me polish the same gear for days.\"","\"I resented the master for his silence and his endless corrections, and I never forgave him.\"","\"The master's clocks were sold in shops as far away as Vienna and Paris, and customers often asked for him by name.\"","\"At first I counted the hours until I could leave; by spring I arrived early, hoping he would show me how he balanced the escapement.\""]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first quotation shows resentment turning into eagerness and admiration. The third denies any change.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A team of geologists claims that a lake formed when a landslide dammed a river about 2,000 years ago.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the geologists' claim?$q$, $q$["The lake is deepest near its center and shallowest near its outlet, where the river leaves through a narrow, rocky gorge.","The river that feeds the lake has deposited sediment in the valley for millions of years, long before the lake existed.","Radiocarbon dating of trees buried by the landslide and of the oldest sediments in the lake both indicate dates of about 2,000 years ago.","Trees buried by the landslide date to about 2,000 years ago, but the lake's oldest sediments are about 10,000 years old."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Matching dates for the landslide and the lake's oldest sediments directly support the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Economist Carlos Mendes argues that a program giving cash to low-income families improved children's school attendance.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Mendes's argument?$q$, $q$["Families that received the cash spent much of it on school uniforms, books, and transportation to and from school.","Attendance rose in families given the cash but not in similar families randomly assigned to receive none.","School attendance among children in families receiving cash rose after the program began, as it did across the whole region.","Teachers reported that students in families receiving cash seemed more attentive and better prepared in class."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A randomized comparison showing attendance gains only in recipient families supports the claim. A preexisting upward trend would weaken it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Zoologist Fatima Noor hypothesizes that dolphins use distinctive whistles as names to identify one another.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Noor's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["A dolphin called back when it heard a recording of its own signature whistle but ignored recordings of other dolphins' whistles.","Dolphins in the same group produce nearly identical whistles, which differ noticeably from the whistles produced by other groups.","Dolphins in a group often produce the same whistle at the same time when they are coordinating a hunt.","Dolphins can recognize the whistles of their relatives after being separated from them for many years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Responding selectively to its own whistle supports the idea that whistles function as names.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A researcher claims that a new teaching method improved students' writing skills.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Students taught with the new method wrote longer essays, which graders who knew which method each student had used scored higher.","Students taught with the new method said they felt more confident about writing and reported spending more time revising their essays.","Teachers who used the new method rated their students' essays higher than they had rated essays from students in previous years.","Graders who did not know which method students had received gave higher scores to essays written by students taught the new way."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Blind grading showing higher scores is direct evidence of improved writing. Length alone doesn't show quality.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Historian Grace Lin argues that a medieval trade fair attracted merchants from far beyond the surrounding region.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Lin's argument?$q$, $q$["The fair was held every year for several centuries, and records show that merchants traveled to it from many towns in the region.","The town that hosted the fair grew to about five thousand people, many of whom worked as innkeepers and stable hands.","Coins from more than a dozen distant kingdoms, along with goods such as Baltic amber and Persian silk, have been found at the fair's site.","Local farmers sold grain and livestock at the fair to merchants who resold the goods in nearby market towns."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Distant coins and goods directly indicate merchants from far away.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a certain plant species can sense the sound of flowing water and grow its roots toward it.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["The plant species grows naturally along riverbanks, where its roots spread toward the water's edge.","With no water nearby, potted plants grew roots toward recordings of flowing water but not toward white noise.","Plants grown in pots with moist soil on one side grew roots toward the moist soil even when no sound was played.","Plants grown in pots near speakers playing white noise grew roots in all directions at about the same rate."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Roots growing toward water sounds—but not other noise—without real water present isolate sound as the cue.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Mark Owusu argues that a city's new library branch increased civic participation in the surrounding neighborhood.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Owusu's argument?$q$, $q$["Turnout in the neighborhood rose sharply after the library opened, but it rose by the same amount citywide in that election.","The library hosted dozens of community meetings in its first year, many of them about local elections and city council decisions.","Attendance at neighborhood association meetings rose after the library opened, and many of those meetings were held at the library.","Residents of the neighborhood used the library's computers frequently, including to register to vote."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If turnout rose equally citywide, the library may not explain the neighborhood's increase.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Paleontologist Ana Ruiz claims that a newly discovered dinosaur species was covered in feathers.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Ruiz's claim?$q$, $q$["The dinosaur was closely related to several species known to have been feathered, and it shared their lightweight, birdlike bones.","The dinosaur's skeleton was found in fine-grained sediment that often preserves soft tissue such as skin and feathers.","The dinosaur was about the size of a modern turkey.","Impressions of branching, feather-like structures were preserved alongside the fossil's skeleton, especially along its arms and tail."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Direct impressions of feathers are the strongest evidence. Relatedness or preservation conditions are indirect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian claims that a nineteenth-century newspaper exaggerated the size of a political protest it reported.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian's claim?$q$, $q$["The newspaper strongly supported the protesters' cause and often printed large estimates of turnout at events it favored.","Other newspapers in the city reported the protest but gave no estimate of the size of the crowd.","Police records and photographs of the event show a crowd of about two thousand people, while the newspaper reported more than twenty thousand.","The protest took place in the city's largest public square, which could hold well over twenty thousand people."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Independent records showing a far smaller crowd directly support exaggeration. The paper's sympathies suggest a motive but aren't direct evidence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying a population of lizards on a Caribbean island measured the lengths of the lizards' front and hind legs before and after a major hurricane. Lizards that survived the storm had, on average, longer front legs and shorter hind legs than the population as a whole had before the hurricane. In wind-tunnel experiments, lizards clinging to perches pulled their hind legs close to their bodies, while their front legs gripped the perch; individuals with long hind legs were more likely to be blown off. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["lizards with long hind legs were better able to survive the hurricane because they could run to shelter more quickly.","the hurricane acted as a selective force, favoring lizards whose body proportions helped them hold on during extreme winds.","the hurricane caused surviving lizards' hind legs to shrink as they clung to perches during the storm.","hind-leg length has no effect on whether a lizard survives an extreme storm."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Survivors had shorter hind legs and longer front legs, and the wind-tunnel results explain why: long hind legs caught the wind. This points to the storm selecting for certain proportions. The storm couldn't have changed individuals' bone lengths, and the evidence contradicts the claims that long hind legs helped or that leg length had no effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In many languages, words for colors appear in a predictable order. Languages with only two color terms distinguish dark from light; those with three add a term for red; those with more terms tend to add green or yellow next, then blue. Linguists studying a language with five color terms found that it had words for dark, light, red, green, and yellow. Based on the pattern described, it is reasonable to expect that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the language does not have a separate word for blue.","the language will soon develop a separate word for pink.","speakers of the language cannot perceive the color blue.","the language has a word for blue but not for yellow."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sequence is dark/light, red, green or yellow, then blue. With five terms covering dark, light, red, green, and yellow, the next addition would be blue, so the language likely lacks a blue term. Lacking a word doesn't mean speakers can't perceive the color.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Historians long assumed that a medieval manuscript known as the Codex Varga was copied in a monastery in northern Italy, since its script resembles that used by Italian scribes. A recent analysis, however, found that the parchment was made from the skins of a breed of sheep raised only in the Iberian Peninsula, and that the ink contained a mineral mined only in central Spain at the time. Unless the materials were imported, which was rare for monasteries of the period, the findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["Italian scribes in the medieval period preferred parchment and ink imported from Spain.","the script of the manuscript was copied from an earlier Spanish manuscript rather than an Italian one.","the manuscript was produced in northern Italy using materials that monasteries regularly imported.","the manuscript was more likely produced in Spain, perhaps by a scribe trained in an Italian style of writing."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Both the parchment and ink point to Spain, and importing such materials was rare. That makes Spanish production more likely, which can be reconciled with the Italian-looking script if the scribe was trained in that style. The claim that monasteries regularly imported materials contradicts the stated rarity of imports.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Economist Farah Siddiqui studied two neighboring towns that both received grants to build public parks. In Town A, the parks were built in the town center; in Town B, they were distributed across several outlying neighborhoods. Five years later, property values had risen substantially in Town A's center but only slightly elsewhere, while in Town B they had risen modestly across many neighborhoods. Siddiqui argues that the location of public investments can influence ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["whether residents choose to use public parks at all in their free time.","the total amount of grant money that towns receive for public projects.","how evenly the economic benefits of those investments are shared across a community.","the total value of all property in a town, since parks built in a town center raise values more than parks built anywhere else."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In Town A, benefits concentrated in the center; in Town B, they spread across neighborhoods. Location thus affected how widely the gains were distributed. The text says nothing about park use, grant amounts, or comparisons with large cities.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Many plants that grow in dry regions open the pores on their leaves, called stomata, only at night, when the air is cooler and more humid. This allows them to take in carbon dioxide while losing less water than they would during the day. However, photosynthesis requires sunlight. To get around this problem, these plants store the carbon dioxide they absorb at night in the form of an acid and then ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["release it into the air during the day through their stomata, which they open briefly at midday to absorb additional sunlight.","release it inside their leaves during the day to be used in photosynthesis while their stomata stay closed.","use it to photosynthesize at night with the help of moonlight and the light of the stars.","convert it directly into water, which they store in their leaves for use during dry periods."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The plants need CO₂ for photosynthesis, which requires daylight, but they keep stomata closed in the day to save water. Storing CO₂ at night and releasing it internally during the day solves both problems. Opening stomata in the day defeats the purpose, and photosynthesis by moonlight contradicts the stated need for sunlight.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In the eighteenth century, sailors suffering from scurvy often recovered after eating citrus fruit. In the following century, however, British naval ships switched from lemons to limes from British colonies in the Caribbean, and outbreaks of scurvy became more common again on long voyages. Since scurvy is caused by a lack of vitamin C, this pattern would be best explained if ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the limes supplied to the ships contained substantially less vitamin C than the lemons they replaced.","sailors on nineteenth-century voyages refused to eat the limes that their ships supplied to them.","nineteenth-century voyages were shorter than the voyages of the eighteenth century.","scurvy in the nineteenth century was caused by a different factor than in the eighteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If scurvy results from vitamin C deficiency and outbreaks increased after the switch, the likeliest explanation is that limes supplied less vitamin C. Shorter voyages would reduce scurvy, and a different cause contradicts the stated cause.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Some songbirds learn their songs from adult tutors, while others are born knowing their songs. In an experiment, researchers raised young birds of a learning species in isolation, playing them recordings of songs from a different species. As adults, many of the birds sang songs that blended features of their own species' typical song with features of the recordings. This result suggests that young birds of this species ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["are unable to learn any features of songs that they hear from recordings rather than from live tutors.","will learn the song of whatever species they hear during early development, regardless of which species they belong to.","are born knowing their species' complete song and do not need to learn from tutors at all.","are predisposed to learn certain features of their own species' song even without hearing an adult of their species sing."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Isolated birds never heard their own species, yet their songs included their species' typical features alongside recorded ones—indicating an inborn bias toward those features while still learning from what they heard.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study of hospital emergency departments, researchers found that patients treated by physicians at the end of a long shift were prescribed antibiotics more often for conditions that rarely require them, such as colds, than patients treated earlier in the shift. Prescribing an antibiotic, the researchers note, is often the quickest way to satisfy a patient who expects one, whereas explaining why antibiotics won't help takes time and effort. The findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["patients treated later in a shift are more likely to have serious bacterial infections than patients treated earlier.","physicians at the end of long shifts are more knowledgeable about antibiotics than physicians earlier in their shifts.","physician fatigue may lead to decisions that require less effort, even when those decisions are not medically ideal.","antibiotics are the most effective treatment for colds when they are prescribed late in a physician's shift."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Prescribing is easier than explaining, and it increases late in a shift, suggesting that tiredness pushes doctors toward the easier choice. Nothing indicates later patients are sicker.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Dendrochronologists date wooden objects by matching the pattern of thick and thin rings in the wood to a master record of ring patterns from trees of known ages. A wooden beam from a historic barn has an outermost ring corresponding to the year 1782. However, the outer layers of the log, including the bark, were removed when the beam was shaped. Therefore, the tree from which the beam was cut ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["was felled in exactly 1782, the year of its outermost ring.","was felled no earlier than 1782 and possibly some years later.","began growing in 1782 and was cut down many years afterward.","was felled several years before 1782, when the barn was built."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The outermost remaining ring dates to 1782, but outer rings were removed, so the tree grew for some unknown time after 1782. The felling date is therefore 1782 at the earliest.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a series of experiments, participants were asked to choose between receiving $50 today or $55 in one week. Most chose $50 today. When asked to choose between $50 in fifty-two weeks or $55 in fifty-three weeks, most chose the $55. Since both choices involve waiting one additional week for an extra $5, these results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people weigh a delay more heavily when it begins immediately than when the same delay occurs in the distant future.","people generally prefer larger rewards to smaller ones regardless of how long they must wait to receive them.","participants misunderstood the second question and believed they would receive $55 sooner than $50.","most participants valued the extra $5 more highly in the first choice than in the second choice."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The trade-off is identical (one week for $5), yet choices flip depending on whether the delay starts now. That means immediate delays feel costlier than distant ones. The claim that people prefer larger rewards regardless of the wait contradicts most people choosing $50 now.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Many deep-sea fish produce their own light through bioluminescence. Biologists have noticed that in several species, light-producing organs called photophores are located on the fish's underside and emit a dim blue glow. In the dim ocean depths, where faint sunlight filters down from above, a fish's silhouette can be visible to predators looking up from below. The location and color of these photophores suggest that they help fish ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["attract prey from the darker water below by producing a bright signal that is visible from great distances in the ocean depths.","see prey swimming above them by illuminating the water over their heads.","communicate with other fish of their species through patterns of flashing light.","blend in with the faint light from above, making their silhouettes harder for predators below to see."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Photophores on the underside emitting dim blue light would match faint downwelling light, hiding the fish's silhouette from predators below. The light is dim and directed downward, not a bright long-distance signal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A city introduced a program offering free public transit to residents over age sixty-five. In the program's first year, ridership among older residents rose by 30 percent. However, surveys showed that most of the new trips were made by people who previously had not traveled at all, rather than by people who had previously driven. City planners had hoped the program would reduce traffic congestion. These results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the program failed to attract older residents who had not previously used public transit.","most older residents in the city drove more after the program began than they had before.","the program increased older residents' mobility but did little to reduce the number of cars on the road.","traffic congestion in the city increased because older residents switched from transit to driving."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ridership rose mostly from people who hadn't traveled before, not former drivers, so mobility increased while car traffic likely didn't fall much.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists excavating an ancient settlement found that the oldest layers contained bones of wild deer and gazelle, while later layers contained mostly bones of sheep and goats. Notably, the sheep and goat bones in the later layers came overwhelmingly from young males, while females were found mostly at older ages. Since herders who raise animals for meat and milk typically slaughter most young males and keep females for breeding and milking, the findings suggest that the residents ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["hunted wild sheep and goats more often than they hunted deer and gazelle.","shifted from hunting wild animals to herding domesticated ones.","kept male sheep and goats for breeding and slaughtered most females when young.","abandoned the settlement after the population of wild deer declined."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The shift from wild game to sheep and goats, combined with a culling pattern typical of herders, indicates a move from hunting to herding. The bones show the opposite of keeping males for breeding.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers investigating why some children are picky eaters found that children who were repeatedly offered a disliked vegetable—without being pressured to eat it—gradually began eating more of it, often after eight to fifteen exposures. Children who were pressured or rewarded for eating the vegetable showed less improvement. The findings suggest that parents hoping to expand children's diets should ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["keep offering disliked foods patiently rather than pushing children to eat them.","stop offering foods that children have refused more than once or twice.","reward children whenever they eat a disliked food, since rewards make children more willing to try that food again later.","offer children only foods that they already enjoy until they are older."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Repeated, pressure-free exposure worked better than pressure or rewards, supporting patient repeated offering.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Astronomers can estimate the age of a star cluster by examining its brightest stars. Massive stars burn through their fuel faster than smaller stars and leave the main stage of their lives sooner. In a young cluster, the most massive stars are still shining; in an older cluster, they have already burned out, leaving only smaller stars. If one cluster contains many very massive, bright blue stars and another contains only small, dimmer stars, the first cluster is most likely ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["older than the second cluster.","about the same age as the second cluster.","farther from Earth than the second cluster.","younger than the second cluster."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Massive stars burn out first, so a cluster still containing them is younger than one in which only smaller stars remain.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In medieval Europe, many towns held weekly markets on a fixed day. Historian Lukas Brandt found that neighboring towns rarely held their markets on the same day of the week; instead, market days were usually staggered so that each town in a region held its market on a different day. Brandt argues that this arrangement benefited traveling merchants, who ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["preferred to sell their goods in a single town each week rather than travel between several different towns.","were required by law to attend the market in their own town on the day it was held.","could visit several markets in a single week without having to choose between towns holding markets at the same time.","competed directly with one another by setting up stalls in neighboring towns on the same day."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Staggered market days let a merchant move from town to town across the week. The other choices don't explain why staggering would benefit traveling merchants.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$When the zebra mussel, an invasive species, spread through the Great Lakes, it filtered huge quantities of plankton from the water. The water became much clearer. Surprisingly, populations of some underwater plants increased sharply, even though the mussels did not eat or otherwise directly affect those plants. This is likely because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the mussels released nutrients that the underwater plants needed to grow.","clearer water allowed more sunlight to reach plants growing on the lake bottom.","plankton had previously been eating the underwater plants in large quantities.","the underwater plants fed on the zebra mussels after the mussels died."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The mussels' only stated effect was clearer water; more light reaching the bottom would boost plant growth. The text says the mussels didn't directly affect the plants, ruling out the nutrient explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A 2019 survey found that 70 percent of adults in a certain country said they would be willing to pay more for products made with recycled materials. However, sales data from the same year showed that products labeled as made from recycled materials accounted for less than 10 percent of purchases in categories where such products were available at only slightly higher prices. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people's stated preferences in surveys may not reliably predict their purchasing behavior.","most adults in the country were unaware that recycled products were available to purchase.","products made with recycled materials were much more expensive than other products.","the survey was conducted among a group of people who did not buy any products."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Many said they'd pay more, yet few bought recycled products even when the price difference was small—so stated preferences didn't match behavior. The text says prices were only slightly higher.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Linguists have observed that when two languages come into contact, speakers often borrow nouns from each other readily, while verbs and grammatical structures are borrowed far less often. In one region where two languages have been spoken side by side for centuries, researchers found that each language had borrowed hundreds of nouns from the other but only a handful of verbs. This pattern is consistent with the idea that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["speakers of the two languages borrowed nouns mainly because neither language originally had words for the objects involved.","nouns are generally more difficult to learn than verbs in a second language.","the two languages will soon merge into a single language with a shared grammar.","the grammar of a language tends to be more resistant to outside influence than its vocabulary for things."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Many nouns but few verbs and grammatical structures were borrowed, consistent with grammar resisting borrowing more than vocabulary for things.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers found that bees exposed to a common fungicide were less able to fight off a particular parasite than unexposed bees. The fungicide itself did not harm bees in the absence of the parasite. However, in regions where the fungicide was widely used, beekeepers reported higher colony losses only in years when the parasite was common. Together, these findings suggest that the fungicide ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["is a more important direct cause of colony losses than the parasite is in regions where the fungicide is widely used.","protects bees from the parasite in years when the parasite is especially common.","may harm bee colonies mainly by weakening their defenses against the parasite rather than directly.","has no effect on bee colonies regardless of whether the parasite is present."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The fungicide caused no harm alone but reduced resistance to the parasite, and losses rose only in parasite years—an indirect effect through weakened defenses.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$During the eighteenth century, the price of a book in England was so high that many readers could not afford to buy one. Circulating libraries allowed readers to borrow books for a small fee. Records show that the most frequently borrowed books at these libraries were novels, while sermons and histories, which made up a large share of books sold, were borrowed less often. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["novels were more expensive to buy than sermons and histories, which is why readers were more likely to borrow them from libraries.","the books people chose to borrow may differ from those that sold well, perhaps because buyers and borrowers differed.","circulating libraries refused to lend sermons and histories to readers who paid a fee.","most readers in eighteenth-century England preferred owning books to borrowing them."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Borrowing and buying patterns differed, suggesting that the two groups—those able to buy and those who borrowed—had different tastes or needs. Nothing indicates libraries refused to lend some books.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A study of commuters found that those who biked to work reported higher job satisfaction than those who drove. But the researchers also found that the bikers, on average, lived closer to their workplaces and had more flexible schedules. Before concluding that biking itself increases job satisfaction, the researchers would need to ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["account for the possibility that shorter commutes and flexible schedules, rather than biking, explain the difference.","survey commuters who drove to work about whether they enjoyed driving.","find out whether commuters who biked to work had been more satisfied with their jobs before they began biking than afterward.","show that commuters who drove to work lived farther from their workplaces than those who biked."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Commute distance and schedule flexibility differ between groups and could explain satisfaction; they must be ruled out before attributing the effect to biking.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Scientists have long wondered how the ancient Polynesians, who had no written maps, navigated across thousands of kilometers of open ocean. Studies of traditional navigators who still practice the old methods show that they memorize the rising and setting points of dozens of stars, read the patterns of ocean swells reflected off distant islands, and watch for birds that fly out to sea in the morning and return to land at night. This evidence suggests that Polynesian navigation relied on ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["instruments similar to compasses that allowed navigators to determine direction precisely.","luck and chance discoveries rather than any systematic method of finding land.","maps carved into wood or stone that were carried on long voyages.","detailed knowledge of natural signs that could be learned and passed on without written records."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Navigators used memorized star positions, swell patterns, and bird behavior—natural signs requiring no written maps or instruments.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In the 1950s, a dam was built on a river that had flooded nearby farmland nearly every spring. The flooding ended, and farmers initially welcomed the change. Within two decades, however, crop yields on the farmland had declined, and farmers had to buy increasing amounts of fertilizer. The decline likely occurred because the floods had ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["washed away the fertilizer that farmers had applied to their fields, forcing them to buy more fertilizer every spring.","prevented farmers from planting crops until late in the growing season.","deposited nutrient-rich sediment on the fields each year, which stopped once the dam was built.","carried pests away from the fields that later damaged the farmers' crops."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After the floods stopped, yields fell and farmers needed more fertilizer, implying the floods had been supplying nutrients through sediment.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers placed identical bird feeders in two parts of a park. One area was close to a busy road; the other was in a quiet woodland. Birds at the roadside feeders spent more time looking around and less time eating than birds at the woodland feeders, and they fled sooner when a researcher approached. The researchers suggested that traffic noise may ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["attract more birds to the roadside feeders than to the feeders in the woodland.","make it harder for birds to hear approaching predators, causing them to compensate with greater visual vigilance.","make birds at the roadside feeders less wary of humans, since they have grown used to the noise and movement of traffic.","cause birds at the roadside feeders to eat more food than birds at the woodland feeders."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Roadside birds watched more and fled sooner—behavior consistent with relying on sight because noise masks sounds of predators. They were more, not less, fearful.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Some economists argue that raising the price of a product will always reduce the quantity people buy. Yet for certain luxury goods, such as designer handbags, sales sometimes rise after prices increase. One explanation is that, for these goods, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["a high price itself can make an item more desirable by signaling exclusivity or status.","manufacturers produce fewer items when prices rise, making each item more durable.","most buyers are unaware of the prices of the items they purchase.","the materials used to make the items become cheaper when prices rise."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If higher prices increase sales, the price itself must add appeal, as with status goods. The other choices don't explain increased demand.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Mammals that hibernate dramatically lower their body temperatures and heart rates during winter, surviving for months on stored fat. Researchers studying a species of ground squirrel found that individuals periodically warm up to normal body temperature for about a day during hibernation before cooling down again, even though these arousals use a large share of their stored energy. Since the arousals are costly, they likely ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["occur because the squirrels have used up all of their stored fat.","happen only in squirrels that failed to store enough fat before winter.","allow the squirrels to save energy compared with remaining cold throughout the winter.","serve some essential function that cannot be performed at low body temperatures."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$If arousals consume much energy yet still occur, they must provide some necessary benefit unavailable at low temperatures. They cost energy, so they don't save it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study of reading comprehension, students read a passage either on paper or on a screen. Both groups scored similarly on questions about specific details. However, students who read on paper scored higher on questions requiring them to recall the order of events in the passage. The researchers suggested that the physical experience of turning pages may help readers ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["remember specific details more accurately than readers who use screens.","read more quickly than readers who use screens for the same passage.","build a mental map of a text's structure, which supports memory of sequence.","avoid distractions from notifications and advertisements on screens."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Paper readers were better only at recalling order, which suggests that physical page-turning supports a sense of the text's structure. Detail recall was similar.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The town's historical society holds the diaries of two sisters who lived in the town during the 1918 influenza pandemic. One sister, a nurse, describes overcrowded hospitals and dozens of deaths each week. The other, who lived on a farm outside town, mentions the epidemic only once, noting that a neighbor had fallen ill. A historian relying only on the second sister's diary might ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["conclude that the pandemic began in the countryside and spread to the town.","underestimate how severely the pandemic affected the town.","assume that both sisters worked as nurses during the pandemic.","overestimate the number of deaths in the town during the pandemic."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The farm sister barely mentions the epidemic, so relying on her account alone would understate its severity in town.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers trained dogs to sit in an MRI scanner while they were shown either their owners or strangers giving a hand signal associated with a food reward. The dogs' brain regions associated with reward were more active when the signal came from their owners. Because the reward was identical in both cases, this result suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the dogs found something rewarding about their owners beyond the food itself.","the dogs could not tell the difference between their owners and strangers.","the dogs expected to receive more food from their owners than from strangers.","the dogs were trained more thoroughly by their owners than by the researchers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since the food reward was the same, the greater reward-region activity for owners suggests the owners themselves were rewarding.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In many cities, buildings painted white or covered with reflective materials stay cooler in summer than darker buildings. But in a study of a city in a cold climate, researchers found that white roofs increased total annual energy use for some buildings. This would be expected if ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the buildings with white roofs used air conditioning more often in summer.","darker buildings reflected more sunlight than white buildings in winter.","the city's summers were longer and hotter than its winters.","the extra heating needed in winter outweighed the cooling saved in summer."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$White roofs save cooling energy but reflect heat that would help in winter. In a cold climate, the added heating could outweigh the summer savings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying a group of mountain villages found that villages located farther from roads had more distinct local dialects than villages close to roads. Older residents in the remote villages also reported that young people who moved to cities often lost their village dialect within a few years. Taken together, these observations suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["villages close to roads were founded more recently than the remote villages.","young people in remote villages speak their village dialect more than older residents do.","regular contact with outsiders tends to reduce differences between local ways of speaking.","dialects in remote villages change more rapidly than dialects in villages near roads."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Isolation correlates with distinct dialects, and moving to cities erodes them—both suggest that contact with outsiders makes speech more uniform.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Some trees in the forests of eastern North America produce large crops of nuts only every few years, with very small crops in between. Ecologists propose that this pattern helps the trees reproduce: in years with small crops, populations of nut-eating animals decline, so in the next large-crop year ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["animals have more nuts available than they need and stop eating them altogether.","there are too few animals to eat all the nuts, and more of them survive to sprout.","the trees produce nuts that are too small for animals to eat easily.","animal populations increase so quickly that they eat every nut the trees produce."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Small crops shrink animal populations; then a large crop overwhelms the remaining animals, leaving more nuts to germinate.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A researcher studied a set of letters written by a nineteenth-century merchant to his business partner. In letters written before 1850, the merchant frequently mentioned goods shipped by sailing vessel and complained about delays caused by weather. After 1850, he mentioned steamships more often, and complaints about weather nearly disappeared, though he began to mention the rising cost of coal. These changes suggest that the shift to steamships ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["made shipping less dependent on weather while introducing a new kind of expense.","made shipping more expensive than it had been before 1850 in every respect.","caused the merchant to stop doing business with his partner after 1850.","did not change how the merchant's goods were transported."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Weather complaints faded while coal costs appeared—steam reduced weather dependence but added fuel expense.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In an experiment, researchers gave two groups of people the same plain cookies. One group was told the cookies were made with expensive organic ingredients; the other was given no information. The first group rated the cookies as tastier and said they would pay more for them. This finding suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["organic ingredients make cookies taste better than conventional ingredients do.","people who buy organic food are more likely to enjoy cookies than other people are.","the two groups were given cookies that were made from different ingredients.","people's judgments of how food tastes can be influenced by information about the food."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The cookies were identical, so the difference in ratings must come from the information given, not the ingredients.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$When a forest is cleared for farming, the soil often loses much of its organic carbon within a few decades. Researchers studying abandoned farmland that had been allowed to regrow into forest found that soil carbon recovered, but slowly—after eighty years, levels were still well below those in forests that had never been cleared. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["farmland that is abandoned quickly loses even more soil carbon than active farmland.","soil carbon in regrown forests will eventually exceed that in uncleared forests, since the regrowing trees absorb carbon quickly.","protecting existing forests may preserve soil carbon more effectively than relying on regrowth after clearing.","clearing forests for farming has no lasting effect on the carbon stored in soil."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Carbon is lost quickly but recovers slowly, so preventing loss is more effective than relying on regrowth to restore it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The novel's narrator repeatedly insists that she has forgiven her brother for selling the family farm. Yet she describes the new owners' changes to the property in bitter detail, refuses to attend her brother's birthday dinner, and keeps the deed she was supposed to have handed over. These details suggest that the narrator ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["is unaware that her brother sold the family farm to new owners.","has not truly forgiven her brother, despite what she claims.","approves of the changes the new owners have made to the property.","has forgiven her brother and wants to repair their relationship."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Her actions—bitterness, avoidance, keeping the deed—contradict her claim of forgiveness.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Studies of early human settlements in a river valley show that the earliest farmers grew a single variety of wheat. Over the next thousand years, the number of wheat varieties grown in the valley increased to more than a dozen, each suited to a different soil type or growing season. Over the same period, the valley's population grew substantially. One possible explanation linking these developments is that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["growing varieties suited to different conditions allowed farmers to cultivate more land and feed more people.","a larger population forced farmers to rely on a single high-yielding variety of wheat that could be grown in every soil type.","farmers stopped growing wheat altogether as the valley's population increased.","the earliest farmers in the valley grew more wheat than later farmers did."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Varieties adapted to diverse soils and seasons would let farmers use more land, supporting a larger population.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A team of researchers measured how quickly glaciers in a mountain range were shrinking. Glaciers on north-facing slopes, which receive less direct sunlight, were shrinking more slowly than those on south-facing slopes. However, a few north-facing glaciers were shrinking just as quickly as south-facing ones; these were all covered with a layer of dark dust blown from a nearby desert. This observation suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["north-facing glaciers shrink more slowly because the cold desert winds that carry dust keep them cooler than south-facing ones.","dust from the desert slows the melting of glaciers on south-facing slopes.","the direction a glacier faces has no effect on how quickly it shrinks.","dark dust may speed melting by causing glaciers to absorb more sunlight, offsetting the advantage of a shaded slope."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Dust-covered north-facing glaciers melted as fast as sunlit ones, suggesting dark dust increased heat absorption and canceled the shading advantage.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Paleontologists have found fossilized dinosaur eggs arranged in tight circular clutches, with the narrow ends of the eggs pointing toward the center. In modern birds, this arrangement is associated with species in which a parent sits on the eggs to keep them warm, since it allows the parent's body to cover them evenly. Crocodiles, which bury their eggs and leave them, lay eggs in loose, irregular piles. The arrangement of the fossil eggs therefore suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the dinosaurs that laid them buried their eggs and left them, as crocodiles do with their own eggs.","the eggs were moved into circular arrangements by water or sediment after they had been laid.","the dinosaurs that laid them may have incubated their eggs by sitting on them, as many birds do.","the dinosaurs that laid them were more closely related to crocodiles than to modern birds."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The tight circular clutch matches the pattern of birds that sit on eggs, unlike crocodiles' loose piles, suggesting similar brooding behavior.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In an effort to reduce the number of people who skipped medical appointments, a clinic began sending text reminders two days before each appointment. Missed appointments fell by 15 percent. The following year, the clinic changed the wording of the reminders to mention that skipping an appointment meant another patient could not be seen in that slot. Missed appointments fell by an additional 20 percent. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["patients are more likely to attend appointments when reminders are sent two days in advance rather than one.","reminding patients of how their absence affects others can be more effective than a simple reminder alone.","the text reminders became less effective in the second year than they had been in the first year.","most patients who skipped appointments did so because they had forgotten when their appointments were."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Adding a message about effects on others produced a further decline beyond the plain reminder.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying a remote island found that its population of a small flightless bird had declined sharply after rats arrived on supply ships. Once the rats were eradicated, the bird population recovered within a decade. On a neighboring island where rats had never arrived, the bird population remained stable throughout the same period. Together, these observations most strongly suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the rats were responsible for the decline of the bird population on the first island.","the bird population on the neighboring island would have declined without the eradication program.","the birds on the first island recovered because they migrated from the neighboring island.","supply ships brought the birds to the first island along with the rats."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The decline followed the rats' arrival and reversed after their removal, while the rat-free island stayed stable—pointing to the rats as the cause.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Art historians have long debated whether the painter Jan Verhaegen created a particular portrait himself or whether it was painted by one of his students. Infrared imaging recently revealed a detailed underdrawing beneath the paint, with many corrections and changes in the position of the figure's hands. Since Verhaegen's students typically copied finished compositions without making changes, while Verhaegen himself often revised his designs as he worked, the underdrawing suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the portrait is a copy of an earlier painting that Verhaegen had already finished.","one of Verhaegen's students corrected mistakes that Verhaegen had made in the portrait.","Verhaegen's students made more corrections to their paintings than Verhaegen did.","the portrait is more likely to have been painted by Verhaegen than by one of his students."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Revisions in the underdrawing match Verhaegen's habit of revising, unlike students who copied without changes.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$When researchers analyzed the remains of meals at an ancient coastal settlement, they found that shellfish made up most of the diet in the earliest layers. In later layers, the shells were noticeably smaller on average, and species that live in deeper water became more common. These changes are consistent with the possibility that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["residents had developed a preference for smaller shellfish because they found the larger ones less flavorful.","the deeper-water species had grown larger over time, replacing the shallow-water species in the diet.","residents had overharvested the easily reached shellfish, forcing them to gather smaller ones and to seek food farther from shore.","the settlement's population had grown so large that residents began to prefer deeper-water species, which they could catch in greater numbers."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Shrinking shell size and a shift to deeper species fit a pattern of depleting nearby stocks. A smaller population would ease pressure, not cause these changes.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Some economists have argued that automation will eliminate many jobs. Economist Lars Eriksson notes, however, that when automated teller machines became common in the 1980s and 1990s, the number of bank tellers in the United States actually increased. ATMs reduced the number of tellers needed per branch, which lowered the cost of operating a branch, and banks responded by opening many more branches. This example suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["automated teller machines were less reliable than human tellers, so banks hired additional tellers at every branch to serve as backups.","automation can reduce the labor needed for a task while increasing demand for workers by making related activities cheaper.","the number of tellers needed per branch increased after banks began to install automated teller machines.","banks opened more branches in the 1980s and 1990s for reasons unrelated to the cost of operating them."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$ATMs cut tellers per branch but made branches cheaper, so banks opened more, raising total employment.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Historians studying the diaries of soldiers in a nineteenth-century war noticed that entries written in the first months of the war frequently described battles as glorious adventures. Entries written by the same soldiers a year later rarely used such language and focused instead on hunger, illness, and the deaths of friends. This shift suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the soldiers' direct experience of war changed how they understood and described it.","the soldiers who wrote the later entries were different people from those who wrote the earlier ones.","the war became more glorious as it continued and the soldiers grew more confident.","the soldiers were ordered by their officers to stop describing battles in their diaries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The same soldiers' language changed from glory to hardship after a year, suggesting experience reshaped their view.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In an experiment on decision-making, participants chose among jams at a grocery store display. When the display offered six flavors, about 30 percent of people who stopped bought a jar. When it offered twenty-four flavors, more people stopped to look, but only about 3 percent bought a jar. These results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people are more likely to buy a product when a store offers a wider variety of options.","most shoppers dislike jam and are unlikely to buy it regardless of how many flavors are offered.","the larger display attracted fewer shoppers than the smaller one did.","offering more choices can attract interest while making people less likely to decide."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$More people stopped at the larger display but far fewer bought, so variety drew interest yet discouraged choosing.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Scientists studying a population of wild chimpanzees found that individuals who spent more time grooming others had more allies who supported them in conflicts. When the researchers tracked the chimpanzees over several years, they found that those who groomed more also tended to rise in the group's social hierarchy. These observations suggest that, for chimpanzees, grooming may ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["be a sign of low rank within the group's social hierarchy.","reduce the number of conflicts that occur within the group.","help build relationships that provide social advantages.","be performed mainly to remove parasites from other chimpanzees."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Grooming correlated with more allies and rising rank, suggesting it builds useful relationships.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A small river town experienced severe flooding roughly once every ten years throughout the nineteenth century. In the 1920s, upstream forests were cleared for farming, and the town began flooding nearly every three years. Since forests absorb rainfall and slow the flow of water into rivers, the change in flood frequency was likely caused by ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["a decrease in the amount of rain that fell in the region after the 1920s.","faster runoff of rainwater into the river after the upstream forests were removed.","the town's construction of new buildings closer to the river in the nineteenth century.","the upstream farms absorbing more rainwater than the forests had absorbed before."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Forests slow runoff; removing them would speed water into the river, raising flood frequency.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study of bilingual speakers of Spanish and English, participants described a video of a person accidentally knocking over a vase. When describing the event in English, participants usually said "She broke the vase." When describing it in Spanish, they more often used a construction meaning "The vase broke." Later, participants were asked to remember who had caused various accidents. Those who had described events in Spanish were less likely to remember who caused them. The findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the way a language describes events may influence what speakers remember about them.","Spanish speakers are generally less observant than English speakers when watching videos.","participants remembered accidents better when they described them in Spanish than in English.","English and Spanish describe accidental events using the same grammatical construction."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The grammatical framing (agent vs. no agent) corresponded with what speakers later remembered about who caused events.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A company tested two versions of its website. In one, the price of a product was shown as $99; in the other, it was shown as $100. The $99 version produced substantially more sales, even though the difference in price was only one dollar. A behavioral economist suggested that shoppers ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["preferred the $100 version because they believed higher prices indicated higher quality.","were unable to see the price on the version of the website showing $100.","calculated the exact difference in price and decided that one dollar was a significant saving.","may focus heavily on the first digit of a price, perceiving $99 as much cheaper than $100."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A one-dollar difference wouldn't usually matter much, so the large effect suggests shoppers perceive $99 as substantially lower because of its leading digit.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying monarch butterflies raised one group of caterpillars on milkweed plants with high levels of a toxin and another group on milkweed with low levels. As adults, butterflies from the first group were rarely eaten by birds in feeding experiments, while those from the second group were eaten more often. Since monarchs store the toxin from milkweed in their bodies, these results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["birds prefer to eat monarch butterflies that have high levels of toxin in their bodies.","monarch caterpillars that eat high-toxin milkweed grow into larger adult butterflies that birds find more difficult to capture.","the protection monarchs have against predators depends partly on the plants they ate as caterpillars.","the toxin in milkweed has no effect on whether birds eat monarch butterflies."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Butterflies from high-toxin plants were avoided by birds, so protection depends on the caterpillars' food.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$For centuries, the canals of Venice were cleaned by periodically draining sections and removing accumulated mud by hand. When this practice was largely abandoned in the twentieth century, residents began noticing that the water in many canals had become shallower and that some buildings' foundations were exposed to more frequent flooding at high tide. These developments suggest that the cleaning practice had ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["caused the flooding of building foundations by removing too much mud from the canals.","helped maintain the depth of the canals, which the city's buildings and drainage depended on.","been abandoned because the canals no longer accumulated mud in the twentieth century.","made the canals shallower over time by disturbing the mud at the bottom of the water."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After cleaning stopped, canals grew shallower and flooding increased, suggesting cleaning had maintained depth.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study of workplace productivity, employees who worked from home one day a week were compared with employees who worked in the office every day. The home-workers completed slightly more tasks, but the researchers noted that the home-workers had volunteered for the arrangement, while the office workers had not been given a choice. Because of this difference, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the study cannot determine whether working from home itself caused the difference in productivity.","the study shows that working from home reduces productivity for most employees.","the office workers must have been more productive than the home-workers in the study.","the home-workers would probably have completed even more tasks if they had been allowed to work from home every day of the week."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Volunteers may differ from non-volunteers (e.g., more motivated), so the study can't isolate the effect of working from home.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Fossil pollen from lake sediments shows that, around 4,000 years ago, the plant life near a particular lake changed from mostly oak trees to mostly grasses. Charcoal in the same layers increased sharply at the time of the change, and archaeological sites nearby show that farming communities arrived shortly before. Together, these findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["a natural decline in oak trees caused farming communities to move to the area.","grasses near the lake were burned by early farmers to make room for oak trees.","the climate near the lake became wetter, allowing grasses to replace oak trees.","early farmers may have used fire to clear forests around the lake for agriculture."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Farmers arrived, charcoal increased, and oaks gave way to grasses—consistent with burning forests to clear land.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a laboratory study, people who were told that a painkiller was expensive reported greater pain relief than people told that the same pill was cheap. In fact, both groups received a pill with no active ingredient. This result suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["expensive painkillers contain more active ingredients than inexpensive ones do.","people who pay more for medicine are less likely to experience pain.","expectations about a treatment can influence how effective people perceive it to be.","the participants could tell that the pills contained no active ingredient."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both groups received inactive pills, so the difference reflects expectations created by price.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Studies of ancient Egyptian mummies have revealed high rates of hardened arteries, a condition often associated with modern diets and sedentary lifestyles. However, most of the mummies studied were of wealthy individuals, who likely ate diets rich in meat and fat and did little physical labor. The findings therefore suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["ancient Egyptians of all social classes had higher rates of hardened arteries than people today.","the condition may be linked to diet and activity levels rather than being unique to modern life.","hardened arteries were caused mainly by the process of mummification itself.","poor ancient Egyptians had higher rates of hardened arteries than wealthy Egyptians."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Wealthy Egyptians with rich diets and little labor had the condition, supporting a link to diet and activity rather than modernity. The sample doesn't tell us about all classes.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$When the city of Seoul removed an elevated highway in 2003 and restored the stream that had been buried beneath it, traffic engineers predicted severe congestion. Instead, traffic in the surrounding area changed little. Researchers found that many drivers had switched to public transit or changed their routes, and some had stopped making certain trips altogether. This outcome suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["traffic levels may adjust to the capacity of roads, rather than remaining fixed.","the elevated highway had carried very few cars before it was removed.","public transit in Seoul became more expensive after the highway was removed.","traffic engineers accurately predicted the effects of removing the highway."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Drivers adapted by changing modes, routes, or trips, so traffic fell to match reduced capacity rather than overwhelming streets.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers found that people who were shown a list of words including "bed," "rest," "tired," and "dream" often later "remembered" seeing the word "sleep," even though it was never on the list. This suggests that memory ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["records every word a person sees with complete accuracy.","tends to remember words with similar meanings less accurately than words with unrelated meanings.","works better for long lists of words than for short lists.","may reconstruct information based on associations rather than recording it exactly."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$People recalled a related word that wasn't shown, indicating memory fills in from associations.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Linguists studying a language spoken by about two thousand people found that the language has more than a dozen words for different types of snow but only one word for "car." The community lives in a mountainous region and relies on sled travel in winter; cars became common there only in the last few decades. This pattern suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the language will soon replace its words for snow with words for different types of cars.","speakers of the language are unable to distinguish among different types of cars.","a language's vocabulary tends to reflect distinctions that matter in its speakers' daily lives.","the community has always relied more on cars than on sleds for travel in winter."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Detailed snow vocabulary in a sled-dependent community, and little car vocabulary for a recent technology, suggest vocabulary tracks what matters locally.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Many coastal towns built seawalls to protect beaches from erosion. Geologists later observed that beaches in front of seawalls often became narrower over time, while beaches without seawalls in the same areas remained stable. Waves striking a wall, they noted, reflect back toward the sea, carrying sand with them. These observations suggest that seawalls ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["prevent the loss of sand from beaches by stopping waves before they reach the shore.","can protect buildings behind them while accelerating the loss of sand from the beaches in front of them.","have no effect on beaches because waves reflect back toward the sea in all locations.","cause beaches on either side of the walls, where there are no seawalls, to become narrower over time as well."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Reflected waves carry sand away, so beaches in front of walls narrow, while unwalled beaches remain stable.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A study tracked people who won large lottery prizes. A year later, their reported happiness was only slightly higher than before their win, and some reported enjoying everyday pleasures less than they had previously. The researchers suggested that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people may adapt to major positive changes, so their effect on happiness fades over time.","winning a lottery prize reduces people's happiness over time, since most winners become less satisfied with their lives.","lottery winners were unhappier than most people before they won their prizes.","everyday pleasures become more enjoyable after a person wins a large prize."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Happiness returned near baseline and everyday pleasures dimmed, consistent with adaptation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers placed cameras near water holes in an African savanna. They found that zebras and wildebeest visited the water holes mostly during the middle of the day, when lions—which hunt mainly at night and at dawn—were resting. However, during the driest months, when many water holes dried up, zebras and wildebeest visited the remaining water holes at all hours. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["lions changed their hunting habits during the driest months, hunting at midday when zebras and wildebeest gathered at water holes.","zebras and wildebeest prefer to drink at night when lions are most active.","the number of lions near the water holes increased during the driest months.","the animals' need for water during droughts may outweigh the risk of encountering predators."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Normally the animals avoid lion hours, but in drought they come at all hours, suggesting thirst overrides predator avoidance.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study, researchers gave one group of students a difficult puzzle and told them it measured intelligence. Another group received the same puzzle but was told it was a game. Students in the "game" group spent more time on the puzzle and solved it more often. This finding suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the students in the \"intelligence\" group were less intelligent than the other students.","games are generally more difficult to solve than tests of intelligence.","framing a task as a test of ability can make people less willing to persist at it.","telling students that a task measures intelligence makes them more motivated to succeed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$With the same puzzle, the "intelligence" framing reduced persistence and success, while the "game" framing increased them.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Ecologists found that a species of wildflower produced fewer seeds in areas where bumblebee populations had declined, even when the flowers received plenty of visits from other insects such as flies. The ecologists noted that the flower's pollen is released only when it is vibrated at a particular frequency, which bumblebees produce by buzzing their flight muscles. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["flies are more effective pollinators of the wildflower than bumblebees are.","the wildflower depends on bumblebees in particular for effective pollination.","the wildflower produces more seeds when it receives visits from many kinds of insects.","bumblebee populations declined because the wildflower produced fewer seeds."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Seed production fell with bumblebees despite other visitors, and only bumblebees' buzzing releases the pollen—so the flower relies on them.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Before the invention of refrigeration, ice harvested from frozen lakes in New England was shipped as far as India and the Caribbean. Much of the ice melted during the voyage, yet the trade remained profitable for decades. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the ice that survived the journey was valuable enough to make up for what was lost.","ships carrying ice traveled much faster than other ships of the period.","ice in India and the Caribbean was cheaper than ice from New England.","very little of the ice melted during the long voyages, since ships carrying ice were insulated well enough to prevent most melting."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite heavy melting, the trade stayed profitable, so the remaining ice must have commanded high prices.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Ornithologists noticed that city-dwelling great tits, a type of songbird, sing at a higher pitch than great tits living in forests. Urban noise from traffic and machinery is concentrated at low frequencies. Researchers suggested that urban great tits ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["sing at higher pitches because they are physically smaller than great tits in forests.","are unable to hear low-pitched songs because of the noise in cities.","sing at higher pitches to imitate the sounds of traffic and machinery.","may sing at higher pitches so that their songs can be heard above low-frequency city noise."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$City noise is low-pitched, so singing higher avoids being masked.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers asked people to estimate the number of deaths caused each year by various hazards. Participants greatly overestimated deaths from dramatic, widely reported events such as shark attacks and plane crashes, while underestimating deaths from common causes such as diabetes and falls. This pattern suggests that people's estimates of risk ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["are generally accurate for common causes of death but not for rare ones.","are based mainly on careful study of official statistics about causes of death.","may be shaped by how easily examples come to mind rather than by actual frequency.","tend to underestimate the dangers of dramatic and widely reported events."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Vivid, widely reported events were overestimated and mundane causes underestimated, consistent with relying on ease of recall.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In 1883, the eruption of Krakatoa in Indonesia released enormous quantities of ash and gas into the upper atmosphere. For several years afterward, people in Europe reported unusually vivid red sunsets, and average global temperatures fell slightly. These observations suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the eruption caused temperatures in Europe to rise for several years afterward.","material from the eruption spread widely through the atmosphere and affected sunlight far from Indonesia.","the vivid sunsets in Europe were caused by local weather patterns that developed independently of the eruption in Indonesia.","the eruption affected only the region immediately surrounding Krakatoa."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Red sunsets in Europe and global cooling indicate that eruption material spread worldwide and altered sunlight.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A team of researchers compared two groups of fifth-grade classrooms. In one group, teachers spent the first five minutes of each math class reviewing material from previous weeks; in the other, teachers began immediately with new material. At the end of the year, students in the review group scored higher on questions about material taught early in the year but no differently on material taught near the end. This suggests that the brief reviews ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["helped students retain older material without affecting how well they learned new material.","made it harder for students to learn the new material taught near the end of the year.","improved students' scores on all of the material covered during the year.","had no effect on how well students remembered material from early in the year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Review groups did better only on early material and the same on late material—improved retention without hurting new learning.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Medieval European maps often placed Jerusalem at the center and depicted distant regions with imaginary creatures. Historians note that these maps were rarely used for navigation; merchants and sailors relied instead on written route descriptions and charts showing coastlines and distances. This suggests that the medieval maps ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["were the main tools that merchants and sailors used to find their way between ports.","accurately depicted the geography of distant regions as it was known at the time.","were replaced by written route descriptions because they were too expensive to produce.","served purposes, such as expressing religious and cultural views, other than practical navigation."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Since navigators used other tools, and the maps centered Jerusalem and showed imaginary creatures, the maps likely expressed worldviews rather than serving navigation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a series of experiments, researchers found that people who wrote briefly about their worries before a major exam performed better than those who did not. The effect was largest among students who reported high levels of test anxiety; for students with low anxiety, writing made little difference. The findings suggest that writing about worries may help by ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["teaching students new information that is directly relevant to the exam.","making students with low anxiety more anxious, which offset the benefits that the writing provided to students with high anxiety.","reducing the burden that anxious thoughts place on students' attention during the exam.","giving students extra time to complete the exam after they finished writing."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The benefit was concentrated among anxious students, suggesting writing reduced anxiety's drain on attention.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying an ancient trade route found glass beads made in Egypt at sites thousands of kilometers away, in what is now Scandinavia. The beads appeared in graves dating to around 1400 BCE, but no Egyptian objects of other kinds were found there, and no evidence suggests that Egyptians traveled that far north. The researchers proposed that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["Egyptian traders established settlements in Scandinavia around 1400 BCE.","the beads may have reached Scandinavia through a chain of trade passing through many intermediaries.","the beads were made in Scandinavia by craftspeople who had learned Egyptian glassmaking techniques from travelers.","Scandinavians traveled directly to Egypt to buy the beads and other goods."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Egyptian beads arrived without other Egyptian objects or evidence of travel, consistent with passing hand to hand through intermediaries.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers measured how much food people ate at a buffet using plates of different sizes. People given larger plates served themselves more food and ate more, even though they did not report feeling hungrier. This finding suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["environmental cues such as plate size can influence how much people eat independent of hunger.","people given larger plates were hungrier than people given smaller plates.","plate size has no effect on how much food people serve themselves at a buffet.","people with smaller plates ate just as much food as people with larger plates, because they returned to the buffet more often."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Larger plates led to more eating without more hunger, showing that cues influence intake.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Tensile Strength of Four Fibers (megapascals)</caption><thead><tr><th>Fiber</th><th>Dry</th><th>Wet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fiber W</td><td>814</td><td>542</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber X</td><td>744</td><td>571</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Y</td><td>652</td><td>384</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Z</td><td>668</td><td>330</td></tr></tbody></table>A materials scientist measured the strength of four natural fibers under dry and wet conditions. Based on the results, the materials scientist concluded that Fiber X is better suited to use in wet environments than the other fibers tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under dry conditions, Fiber W had a higher strength (814) than any other fiber, including Fiber X (744).","Fiber X had a higher strength under dry conditions (744) than under wet conditions (571).","Under wet conditions, Fiber Y had a lower strength (384) than Fiber W did (542).","Under wet conditions, Fiber X had a higher strength (571) than any other fiber, and it lost the least from dry conditions to wet conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The conclusion is about performance under wet conditions relative to the other fibers. Only the correct answer shows that Fiber X outperformed every other fiber under wet conditions and lost the least going from dry conditions to wet conditions. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under dry conditions, a drop that every fiber experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Fiber X.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Yield of Four Wheat Varieties (tons per hectare)</caption><thead><tr><th>Variety</th><th>Normal rainfall</th><th>Drought</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Variety K</td><td>5.3</td><td>3.7</td></tr><tr><td>Variety M</td><td>6.2</td><td>4.5</td></tr><tr><td>Variety R</td><td>5.2</td><td>4.9</td></tr><tr><td>Variety T</td><td>5.9</td><td>4.0</td></tr></tbody></table>An agronomist tested four wheat varieties under normal rainfall and under drought conditions. Based on the results, the agronomist concluded that Variety R is better suited to drought than the other varieties tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under normal rainfall, Variety M had a higher yield (6.2) than any other variety, including Variety R (5.2).","Variety R had a higher yield under normal rainfall (5.2) than under drought (4.9).","Under drought, Variety R had a higher yield (4.9) than any other variety, and it lost the least from normal rainfall to drought.","Under drought, Variety K had a lower yield (3.7) than Variety M did (4.5)."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The conclusion is about performance under drought relative to the other varieties. Only the correct answer shows that Variety R outperformed every other variety under drought and lost the least going from normal rainfall to drought. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under normal rainfall, a drop that every variety experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Variety R.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rate of Seeds Stored at Two Temperatures (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Seed type</th><th>Room temperature</th><th>Freezing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>89</td><td>39</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>48</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>70</td><td>67</td></tr><tr><td>Type 4</td><td>84</td><td>51</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist stored seeds of four plant types at room temperature and at freezing temperatures for a year and then measured how many germinated. Based on the results, the botanist concluded that Type 3 is better suited to long-term storage at freezing temperatures than the other seed types tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under room temperature, Type 1 had a higher germination rate (89) than any other seed type, including Type 3 (70).","Under freezing, Type 3 had a higher germination rate (67) than any other seed type, and it lost the least from room temperature to freezing.","Type 3 had a higher germination rate under room temperature (70) than under freezing (67).","Under freezing, Type 2 had a lower germination rate (38) than Type 1 did (39)."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The conclusion is about performance under freezing relative to the other seed types. Only the correct answer shows that Type 3 outperformed every other seed type under freezing and lost the least going from room temperature to freezing. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under room temperature, a drop that every seed type experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Type 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Number of Pollinator Visits per Hour to Four Flower Species</caption><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Sunny day</th><th>Cloudy day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Species P</td><td>37</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Species Q</td><td>32</td><td>29</td></tr><tr><td>Species S</td><td>24</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Species V</td><td>23</td><td>18</td></tr></tbody></table>An ecologist counted pollinator visits to four flower species on sunny and cloudy days. Based on the results, the ecologist concluded that Species Q is especially attractive to pollinators in cloudy weather than the other species tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under a cloudy day, Species Q had a higher number of visits (29) than any other species, and it lost the least from a sunny day to a cloudy day.","Under a sunny day, Species P had a higher number of visits (37) than any other species, including Species Q (32).","Species Q had a higher number of visits under a sunny day (32) than under a cloudy day (29).","Under a cloudy day, Species S had a higher number of visits (16) than Species P did (6)."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The conclusion is about performance under a cloudy day relative to the other species. Only the correct answer shows that Species Q outperformed every other species under a cloudy day and lost the least going from a sunny day to a cloudy day. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under a sunny day, a drop that every species experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Species Q.$q$, null),
