  ($q$ii-h195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Yield of Four Wheat Varieties (tons per hectare)</caption><thead><tr><th>Variety</th><th>Normal rainfall</th><th>Drought</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Variety K</td><td>4.0</td><td>2.8</td></tr><tr><td>Variety M</td><td>2.8</td><td>2.5</td></tr><tr><td>Variety R</td><td>3.1</td><td>3.0</td></tr><tr><td>Variety T</td><td>5.3</td><td>2.1</td></tr></tbody></table>An agronomist tested four wheat varieties under normal rainfall and under drought conditions. Based on the results, the agronomist concluded that Variety R is better suited to drought than the other varieties tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under normal rainfall, Variety T had a higher yield (5.3) than any other variety, including Variety R (3.1).","Under drought, Variety R had a higher yield (3.0) than any other variety, and it lost the least from normal rainfall to drought.","Variety R had a higher yield under normal rainfall (3.1) than under drought (3.0).","Under drought, Variety K had a higher yield (2.8) than Variety T did (2.1)."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The conclusion is about performance under drought relative to the other varieties. Only the correct answer shows that Variety R outperformed every other variety under drought and lost the least going from normal rainfall to drought. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under normal rainfall, a drop that every variety experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Variety R.$q$, null),
