  ($q$ii-h197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Tensile Strength of Four Fibers (megapascals)</caption><thead><tr><th>Fiber</th><th>Dry</th><th>Wet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fiber W</td><td>472</td><td>305</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber X</td><td>293</td><td>247</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Y</td><td>786</td><td>789</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Z</td><td>549</td><td>370</td></tr></tbody></table>A materials scientist measured the strength of four natural fibers under dry and wet conditions. A colleague claimed that every fiber tested had a lower strength under wet conditions than under dry conditions.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that weaken the colleague's claim?$q$, $q$["Fiber Z had a strength of 549 under dry conditions, compared with 370 under wet conditions.","Fiber X had the lowest strength of any fiber under wet conditions, at 247.","Fiber W had a strength of 472 under dry conditions, lower than that of Fiber Y under the same conditions.","Fiber Y had a strength of 789 under wet conditions, compared with 786 under dry conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The claim is that every fiber declined under wet conditions. Fiber Y is a counterexample: its strength rose from 786 to 789. The other choices describe declines or comparisons that are consistent with the claim.$q$, null),
