  ($q$ii-h213$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Books Checked Out at Four Library Branches (thousands)</caption><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>2016</th><th>2024</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>North Branch</td><td>48</td><td>85</td></tr><tr><td>South Branch</td><td>101</td><td>201</td></tr><tr><td>East Branch</td><td>55</td><td>84</td></tr><tr><td>West Branch</td><td>57</td><td>125</td></tr></tbody></table>A city report examined changes in the number of books checked out between 2016 and 2024. The report noted that, relative to its 2016 level, West Branch grew more than any of the other branches listed.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the report's claim?$q$, $q$["South Branch's number of books checked out rose by 100, a larger increase in absolute terms than that of any other branch in the table.","In 2024, South Branch had the highest number of books checked out of any branch in the table, at 201.","West Branch's number of books checked out in 2016 (57) was lower than South Branch's number of books checked out in 2016 (101).","West Branch's number of books checked out rose from 57 to 125, an increase of about 119%, the largest percentage increase of any branch in the table."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The claim concerns growth relative to the 2016 level—percentage growth. West Branch grew by about 119%, more than any other. South Branch had the largest absolute increase, which is a different measure, and the other choices don't address growth relative to the starting level.$q$, null),
