  ($q$ii-h261$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Yield of Four Wheat Varieties (tons per hectare)</caption><thead><tr><th>Variety</th><th>Normal rainfall</th><th>Drought</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Variety K</td><td>3.5</td><td>3.2</td></tr><tr><td>Variety M</td><td>2.9</td><td>3.0</td></tr><tr><td>Variety R</td><td>6.0</td><td>2.7</td></tr><tr><td>Variety T</td><td>3.1</td><td>4.4</td></tr></tbody></table>An agronomist tested four wheat varieties under normal rainfall and under drought conditions. The results illustrate that the variety that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Variety T had the highest yield of any variety under drought, at 4.4.","Variety M had the lowest yield of any variety under normal rainfall, at 2.9.","Variety R had a higher yield under normal rainfall than under drought, as did most of the other varieties.","Variety R had the highest yield of any variety under normal rainfall (6.0) but the lowest under drought (2.7)."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The example must show a variety that is best under one condition but not under another. Variety R was best under normal rainfall yet worst under drought. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h262$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Reading Comprehension Scores Under Two Conditions (out of 100)</caption><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Silence</th><th>Background music</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Group 1</td><td>88</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Group 2</td><td>90</td><td>56</td></tr><tr><td>Group 3</td><td>66</td><td>73</td></tr><tr><td>Group 4</td><td>59</td><td>71</td></tr></tbody></table>A psychologist tested four groups of students who used different study methods, measuring reading comprehension in silence and with background music. The results illustrate that the group that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Group 3 had the highest score of any group under background music, at 73.","Group 4 had the lowest score of any group under silence, at 59.","Group 2 had the highest score of any group under silence (90) but the lowest under background music (56).","Group 2 had a higher score under silence than under background music, as did most of the other groups."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The example must show a group that is best under one condition but not under another. Group 2 was best under silence yet worst under background music. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h263$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rate of Seeds Stored at Two Temperatures (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Seed type</th><th>Room temperature</th><th>Freezing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>71</td><td>48</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>93</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>89</td><td>62</td></tr><tr><td>Type 4</td><td>91</td><td>64</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist stored seeds of four plant types at room temperature and at freezing temperatures for a year and then measured how many germinated. The results illustrate that the seed type that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Type 4 had the highest germination rate of any seed type under freezing, at 64.","Type 2 had the highest germination rate of any seed type under room temperature (93) but the lowest under freezing (38).","Type 1 had the lowest germination rate of any seed type under room temperature, at 71.","Type 2 had a higher germination rate under room temperature than under freezing, as did most of the other seed types."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The example must show a seed type that is best under one condition but not under another. Type 2 was best under room temperature yet worst under freezing. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
