  ($q$ii-h265$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Reading Comprehension Scores Under Two Conditions (out of 100)</caption><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Silence</th><th>Background music</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Group 1</td><td>68</td><td>73</td></tr><tr><td>Group 2</td><td>86</td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td>Group 3</td><td>75</td><td>91</td></tr><tr><td>Group 4</td><td>71</td><td>82</td></tr></tbody></table>A psychologist tested four groups of students who used different study methods, measuring reading comprehension in silence and with background music. The results illustrate that the group that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Group 3 had the highest score of any group under background music, at 91.","Group 1 had the lowest score of any group under silence, at 68.","Group 2 had a higher score under silence than under background music, as did most of the other groups.","Group 2 had the highest score of any group under silence (86) but the lowest under background music (54)."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The example must show a group that is best under one condition but not under another. Group 2 was best under silence yet worst under background music. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h266$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Tensile Strength of Four Fibers (megapascals)</caption><thead><tr><th>Fiber</th><th>Dry</th><th>Wet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fiber W</td><td>632</td><td>863</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber X</td><td>863</td><td>648</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Y</td><td>750</td><td>762</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Z</td><td>428</td><td>758</td></tr></tbody></table>A materials scientist measured the strength of four natural fibers under dry and wet conditions. The results illustrate that the fiber that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Fiber W had the highest strength of any fiber under wet conditions, at 863.","Fiber Z had the lowest strength of any fiber under dry conditions, at 428.","Fiber X had the highest strength of any fiber under dry conditions (863) but the lowest under wet conditions (648).","Fiber X had a higher strength under dry conditions than under wet conditions, as did most of the other fibers."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The example must show a fiber that is best under one condition but not under another. Fiber X was best under dry conditions yet worst under wet conditions. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
