  ($q$ii-h268$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Yield of Four Wheat Varieties (tons per hectare)</caption><thead><tr><th>Variety</th><th>Normal rainfall</th><th>Drought</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Variety K</td><td>2.7</td><td>5.4</td></tr><tr><td>Variety M</td><td>5.5</td><td>2.0</td></tr><tr><td>Variety R</td><td>3.8</td><td>5.6</td></tr><tr><td>Variety T</td><td>3.7</td><td>5.2</td></tr></tbody></table>An agronomist tested four wheat varieties under normal rainfall and under drought conditions. The results illustrate that the variety that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Variety M had the highest yield of any variety under normal rainfall (5.5) but the lowest under drought (2.0).","Variety R had the highest yield of any variety under drought, at 5.6.","Variety K had the lowest yield of any variety under normal rainfall, at 2.7.","Variety M had a higher yield under normal rainfall than under drought, as did most of the other varieties."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The example must show a variety that is best under one condition but not under another. Variety M was best under normal rainfall yet worst under drought. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
