  ($q$ii-h270$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rate of Seeds Stored at Two Temperatures (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Seed type</th><th>Room temperature</th><th>Freezing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>62</td><td>53</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>34</td><td>48</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>91</td><td>35</td></tr><tr><td>Type 4</td><td>81</td><td>67</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist stored seeds of four plant types at room temperature and at freezing temperatures for a year and then measured how many germinated. The results illustrate that the seed type that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Type 4 had the highest germination rate of any seed type under freezing, at 67.","Type 2 had the lowest germination rate of any seed type under room temperature, at 34.","Type 3 had the highest germination rate of any seed type under room temperature (91) but the lowest under freezing (35).","Type 3 had a higher germination rate under room temperature than under freezing, as did most of the other seed types."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The example must show a seed type that is best under one condition but not under another. Type 3 was best under room temperature yet worst under freezing. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h271$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Battery Capacity Remaining After 500 Charge Cycles (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Battery design</th><th>At 20°C</th><th>At 45°C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design A</td><td>95</td><td>49</td></tr><tr><td>Design B</td><td>81</td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td>Design C</td><td>41</td><td>69</td></tr><tr><td>Design D</td><td>49</td><td>92</td></tr></tbody></table>An engineer tested four battery designs by charging and discharging them 500 times at 20°C and at 45°C. The results illustrate that the battery design that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Design D had the highest remaining capacity of any battery design under 45°C, at 92.","Design A had the highest remaining capacity of any battery design under 20°C (95) but the lowest under 45°C (49).","Design C had the lowest remaining capacity of any battery design under 20°C, at 41.","Design A had a higher remaining capacity under 20°C than under 45°C, as did most of the other battery designs."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The example must show a battery design that is best under one condition but not under another. Design A was best under 20°C yet worst under 45°C. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h272$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rate of Seeds Stored at Two Temperatures (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Seed type</th><th>Room temperature</th><th>Freezing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>43</td><td>71</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>39</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>35</td><td>70</td></tr><tr><td>Type 4</td><td>58</td><td>35</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist stored seeds of four plant types at room temperature and at freezing temperatures for a year and then measured how many germinated. The results illustrate that the seed type that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Type 4 had the highest germination rate of any seed type under room temperature (58) but the lowest under freezing (35).","Type 1 had the highest germination rate of any seed type under freezing, at 71.","Type 3 had the lowest germination rate of any seed type under room temperature, at 35.","Type 4 had a higher germination rate under room temperature than under freezing, as did most of the other seed types."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The example must show a seed type that is best under one condition but not under another. Type 4 was best under room temperature yet worst under freezing. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h273$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Reading Comprehension Scores Under Two Conditions (out of 100)</caption><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Silence</th><th>Background music</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Group 1</td><td>77</td><td>70</td></tr><tr><td>Group 2</td><td>76</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>Group 3</td><td>62</td><td>78</td></tr><tr><td>Group 4</td><td>89</td><td>62</td></tr></tbody></table>A psychologist tested four groups of students who used different study methods, measuring reading comprehension in silence and with background music. The results illustrate that the group that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Group 2 had the highest score of any group under background music, at 80.","Group 3 had the lowest score of any group under silence, at 62.","Group 4 had a higher score under silence than under background music, as did most of the other groups.","Group 4 had the highest score of any group under silence (89) but the lowest under background music (62)."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The example must show a group that is best under one condition but not under another. Group 4 was best under silence yet worst under background music. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h274$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Number of Pollinator Visits per Hour to Four Flower Species</caption><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Sunny day</th><th>Cloudy day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Species P</td><td>6</td><td>45</td></tr><tr><td>Species Q</td><td>47</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Species S</td><td>30</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Species V</td><td>22</td><td>25</td></tr></tbody></table>An ecologist counted pollinator visits to four flower species on sunny and cloudy days. The results illustrate that the species that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Species P had the highest number of visits of any species under a cloudy day, at 45.","Species P had the lowest number of visits of any species under a sunny day, at 6.","Species Q had the highest number of visits of any species under a sunny day (47) but the lowest under a cloudy day (16).","Species Q had a higher number of visits under a sunny day than under a cloudy day, as did most of the other species."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The example must show a species that is best under one condition but not under another. Species Q was best under a sunny day yet worst under a cloudy day. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h275$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Tensile Strength of Four Fibers (megapascals)</caption><thead><tr><th>Fiber</th><th>Dry</th><th>Wet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fiber W</td><td>800</td><td>251</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber X</td><td>205</td><td>616</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Y</td><td>370</td><td>829</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Z</td><td>201</td><td>683</td></tr></tbody></table>A materials scientist measured the strength of four natural fibers under dry and wet conditions. The results illustrate that the fiber that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Fiber Y had the highest strength of any fiber under wet conditions, at 829.","Fiber W had the highest strength of any fiber under dry conditions (800) but the lowest under wet conditions (251).","Fiber Z had the lowest strength of any fiber under dry conditions, at 201.","Fiber W had a higher strength under dry conditions than under wet conditions, as did most of the other fibers."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The example must show a fiber that is best under one condition but not under another. Fiber W was best under dry conditions yet worst under wet conditions. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h276$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Battery Capacity Remaining After 500 Charge Cycles (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Battery design</th><th>At 20°C</th><th>At 45°C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design A</td><td>96</td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td>Design B</td><td>91</td><td>83</td></tr><tr><td>Design C</td><td>73</td><td>96</td></tr><tr><td>Design D</td><td>47</td><td>69</td></tr></tbody></table>An engineer tested four battery designs by charging and discharging them 500 times at 20°C and at 45°C. The results illustrate that the battery design that performs best under one condition is not necessarily the best under another. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?$q$, $q$["Design A had the highest remaining capacity of any battery design under 20°C (96) but the lowest under 45°C (54).","Design C had the highest remaining capacity of any battery design under 45°C, at 96.","Design D had the lowest remaining capacity of any battery design under 20°C, at 47.","Design A had a higher remaining capacity under 20°C than under 45°C, as did most of the other battery designs."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The example must show a battery design that is best under one condition but not under another. Design A was best under 20°C yet worst under 45°C. The other choices each describe only one condition or a general pattern, so they don't illustrate the point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h277$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$For years, conservationists tried to protect a rare orchid in Florida by fencing off the few sites where it grew. The orchid continued to decline. Botanist Rosa Delgado discovered why: the orchid's seeds can germinate only with the help of a specific soil fungus, which in turn depends on periodic fires that clear competing plants. By preventing disturbance, the fences had inadvertently allowed shrubs to crowd out both the fungus and the orchid.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Delgado found that the orchid's decline was caused mainly by fires that destroyed the few sites where it grew, despite the fences put up to protect them.","Delgado found that well-intended protection harmed the orchid by excluding the disturbances its life cycle depends on.","Delgado found that fencing is generally an ineffective way to protect rare plants from extinction.","Delgado found that the orchid's seeds could germinate without the soil fungus if shrubs were removed."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The fences prevented the fires that maintain the fungus the orchid needs. The choice beginning "Delgado found that the orchid's decline…" reverses the role of fire, the choice beginning "Delgado found that fencing…" overgeneralizes from one case, and the choice beginning "Delgado found that the orchid's seeds…" contradicts the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h278$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1915 novel.<br><br>Every spring, Mr. Hale announced that this would be the year he finally repaired the barn roof. He bought nails and borrowed a ladder from the Dawsons. He studied the roof from every angle, discussing pitch and shingles with anyone who would listen. And every autumn the rain came through the same holes as before, and Mr. Hale remarked, without any sign of distress, that next year would certainly be the year.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Mr. Hale is frustrated that his neighbors refuse to help him repair the roof of his barn.","Mr. Hale repeatedly prepares and talks about a task that he never actually completes, and he seems untroubled by this.","Mr. Hale has finally repaired his barn roof after many years of careful preparation, discussion with neighbors, and borrowing tools from the Dawsons.","Mr. Hale is so distressed by the leaking roof that he talks about it constantly to his neighbors."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text shows a yearly cycle of preparation without completion, ending with Mr. Hale's calm promise of "next year." The neighbors lend a ladder, the roof still leaks, and he shows no distress.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h279$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many economists predicted that the spread of ATMs in the 1980s and 1990s would eliminate jobs for bank tellers. Instead, the number of tellers in the United States grew. Economist James Bessen explains that ATMs lowered the cost of operating a branch, so banks opened more branches. Tellers, meanwhile, shifted from counting cash to tasks such as advising customers on loans and other services.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Banks opened more branches in the 1980s and 1990s because customers preferred tellers to ATMs.","Contrary to predictions, ATMs did not eliminate teller jobs, because they changed both the economics of branches and the nature of tellers' work.","ATMs eliminated most bank teller jobs in the United States during the 1980s and 1990s.","Economists correctly predicted that ATMs would cause banks to open more branches and tellers to spend more time advising customers on loans and other services."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text explains why teller employment grew despite ATMs: cheaper branches and changed duties. The choice beginning "ATMs eliminated most bank…" contradicts the text, the choice beginning "Banks opened more branches…" gives a reason the text doesn't, and the predictions were about job losses.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h280$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Linguists once thought that the click sounds of some southern African languages were rare oddities. Researcher Thandiwe Mokoena has shown that clicks function like ordinary consonants in these languages, distinguishing words just as "b" and "p" do in English. One language, ǃXóõ, has more than eighty click sounds. Mokoena argues that clicks are a full part of these languages' sound systems, not exotic additions.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Mokoena argues that click sounds are rare oddities that serve no function in distinguishing words in southern African languages.","Mokoena argues that English speakers can easily learn to pronounce click sounds.","Mokoena argues that click sounds play the same basic role in some languages that other consonants do.","Mokoena argues that ǃXóõ has more click sounds than any other language in the world."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Mokoena shows that clicks distinguish words like ordinary consonants and are integral to the sound systems. The ǃXóõ figure is a supporting detail (and no ranking is given), the choice beginning "Mokoena argues that click sounds are…" reverses her view, and pronunciation isn't discussed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h281$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Historians have traditionally portrayed the medieval Mongol Empire as a destructive force that spread terror across Eurasia. Historian Jack Weatherford does not deny the violence of the conquests but emphasizes another side of Mongol rule: the empire protected trade routes, issued paper money, established a postal relay system, and granted religious freedom to its subjects. Weatherford argues that these policies helped connect East and West in ways that shaped the modern world.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Weatherford argues that historians have exaggerated the violence of the Mongol conquests.","Weatherford argues that the Mongol Empire was the first state ever to issue paper money.","Weatherford argues that the Mongol Empire's postal relay system was its most important achievement, more significant than its trade routes or paper money.","Weatherford argues that, alongside its violence, the Mongol Empire adopted policies with lasting positive effects."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Weatherford acknowledges violence but highlights connecting policies. He "does not deny the violence," so the claim that he says historians exaggerated it is wrong, and the text doesn't call the empire the first to issue paper money or rank the postal relay above its other achievements.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h282$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying sleep in teenagers have found that the timing of the body's internal clock shifts during adolescence, so that teenagers naturally become sleepy later at night than younger children or adults. When high schools in several districts moved their start times from 7:30 to 8:30 a.m., students slept an average of 34 minutes more each night, and attendance and grades improved. The researchers conclude that early start times conflict with teenagers' biology.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Because adolescents' internal clocks shift later, later school start times can improve teenagers' sleep and performance.","Younger children become sleepy later at night than teenagers do.","High schools that moved their start times later saw attendance fall but grades improve.","Teenagers sleep less than adults mainly because they spend too much time using electronic devices at night, not because of any biological change."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text connects the biological shift in adolescence to the benefits of later start times. Devices aren't mentioned, attendance improved, and the choice beginning "Younger children become sleepy…" reverses the comparison.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h283$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The Japanese art of kintsugi repairs broken ceramics with lacquer mixed with gold, making the cracks shine. Rather than hiding damage, the method highlights it. Art historian Yuko Saito argues that kintsugi reflects a broader aesthetic in Japanese culture that values imperfection and impermanence, an idea sometimes called <i>wabi-sabi</i>. In this view, an object's history, including the ways it has been broken, is part of its beauty.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Saito argues that gold is the only material appropriate for repairing broken ceramics.","Saito argues that kintsugi was invented to make ceramics stronger than they were before they broke.","Saito argues that kintsugi expresses a cultural appreciation of imperfection and an object's history.","Saito argues that Japanese culture values new, flawless objects over older ones."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Saito links kintsugi to an aesthetic that values imperfection and history. Strength isn't discussed, gold's exclusivity isn't claimed, and the choice beginning "Saito argues that Japanese…" reverses her view.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h284$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The following text is adapted from a 1902 short story.<br><br>Old Petersen had fished the same stretch of coast for fifty years and knew every rock beneath the water. The young men laughed at his slow, careful ways and his refusal to buy a motor for his boat. Yet on the morning of the great storm, when the motorboats were scattered and two were lost, it was Petersen's small sailboat that came home first, threading through the reef by a channel only he remembered.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The young fishermen are more skilled than Old Petersen because they use motorboats, which allow them to return home faster even during storms.","Old Petersen decides to buy a motor for his boat after the great storm.","The young fishermen respect Old Petersen's careful methods and imitate them.","The fisherman's deep knowledge of the coast proves more valuable than the newer technology others mocked him for rejecting."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Petersen's knowledge saves him while motorboats are lost, vindicating his ways. The young men laughed at him, and the text doesn't mention him buying a motor.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h285$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Ornithologists have documented that some city-dwelling birds have begun using cigarette butts in their nests. House sparrows and house finches in Mexico City, researchers found, lined their nests with used butts, and nests containing more butts had fewer parasitic mites. Nicotine and other chemicals in the butts appear to repel the parasites. However, the researchers also found signs of genetic damage in chicks from nests with many butts, suggesting the behavior carries costs as well as benefits.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Cigarette butts in nests have no measurable effect on the number of parasitic mites.","Researchers recommend placing cigarette butts near nests in Mexico City to protect house sparrows and house finches from parasitic mites.","Urban birds' use of cigarette butts in nests appears to reduce parasites but may also harm chicks.","Urban birds use cigarette butts in their nests because the butts provide extra warmth."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text presents both a benefit (fewer mites) and a cost (genetic damage). Warmth isn't mentioned, the choice beginning "Cigarette butts in nests…" contradicts the findings, and no recommendation is made.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h286$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many people believe that the English language is uniquely large, citing the hundreds of thousands of entries in major dictionaries. Linguist Sara Pérez cautions that such comparisons are misleading. Dictionaries in different languages follow different conventions—some list every compound and inflected form separately, others do not—and no dictionary captures every word speakers use. Counting words, Pérez argues, says more about lexicographers' choices than about the languages themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Pérez argues that dictionary counts are not a reliable way to compare the sizes of languages.","Pérez argues that English has more words than any other language in the world.","Pérez argues that dictionaries should list every compound and inflected form separately.","Pérez argues that most English speakers know only a small fraction of the words in dictionaries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Pérez says dictionary conventions differ, so word counts reflect choices rather than language size. She doesn't rank English, prescribe a convention, or discuss speakers' vocabularies.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h287$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In 1977, NASA attached golden phonograph records to the twin Voyager spacecraft, containing greetings, music, and images meant for any intelligent life that might find them. The spacecraft will not come near another star for tens of thousands of years, and the chance of discovery is tiny. Writer Mara Ellison, who has studied the project's history, suggests that the records were never really addressed to aliens. Their true audience, she argues, was humanity itself, prompted to consider how it wished to represent itself to the universe.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Ellison suggests that the Voyager records should have contained more scientific information about Earth.","Ellison suggests that the Voyager records mattered chiefly for what creating them led people on Earth to reflect upon.","Ellison suggests that NASA attached the records to the spacecraft mainly to test new recording technology.","Ellison suggests that the Voyager records are likely to be discovered by alien life within a few thousand years, sooner than NASA originally expected."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Ellison argues the records' real audience was humanity and its self-reflection. The text says discovery is unlikely for tens of thousands of years, and it doesn't discuss scientific content or technology testing.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h288$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Researchers once believed that the brain's structure was largely fixed after childhood. Studies of London taxi drivers challenged this view. To earn a license, drivers must memorize the city's roughly 25,000 streets, a process that takes years. Brain scans showed that experienced drivers had a larger posterior hippocampus, a region involved in spatial memory, than other people, and that the region grew as drivers trained.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Studies of taxi drivers suggest that the adult brain can change in response to intensive learning.","The hippocampus is the only region of the brain involved in memory.","London taxi drivers must memorize about 25,000 streets to earn a license.","Taxi drivers in London are born with a larger posterior hippocampus than other people, which explains why they can memorize 25,000 streets."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The growth during training shows adult brain plasticity, which challenges the fixed-structure view. The choice beginning "Taxi drivers in London…" contradicts "grew as drivers trained," the choice beginning "The hippocampus is the…" overgeneralizes, and the choice beginning "London taxi drivers must…" is a detail.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h289$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Historian Marisol Vega studied the diaries of women who traveled west on the Oregon Trail in the 1840s and 1850s. Popular images of the trail emphasize dramatic dangers such as attacks and river crossings. Vega found that the diaries dwelled far more on daily labor—cooking over buffalo-chip fires, washing clothes in streams, nursing sick children—and on the illnesses, especially cholera, that caused most deaths on the trail.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Vega's research suggests that women's diaries present a more routine and disease-centered picture of the trail than popular images do.","Vega's research suggests that women rarely kept diaries during their journeys on the Oregon Trail, so popular images of the trail come mainly from men's accounts.","Vega's research suggests that attacks were the leading cause of death on the Oregon Trail.","Vega's research suggests that river crossings were the most dangerous part of the journey west."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The diaries focus on labor and illness rather than the dramatic dangers of popular images. The text says cholera caused most deaths, and it doesn't support the other claims.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h290$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Some species of fig wasps and fig trees have depended on each other for more than 60 million years. Each fig species is pollinated by its own wasp species, which lays its eggs inside the fig's flowers. Biologist Kofi Asante has found, however, that this one-to-one pairing is less strict than once believed: in many regions, two or more wasp species pollinate the same fig, and wasps occasionally switch to new fig hosts.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Asante has found that fig wasps and fig trees no longer depend on each other for reproduction after more than 60 million years of partnership.","Asante has found that each fig species is pollinated by exactly one species of wasp.","Asante has found that fig wasps began pollinating fig trees only recently.","Asante has found that the relationship between fig trees and their wasp pollinators is more flexible than previously thought."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Asante shows that pairings aren't strictly one-to-one. The partnership still exists, the choice beginning "Asante has found that each…" states the old view he challenged, and the text describes a 60-million-year relationship.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h291$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1923 novel.<br><br>Clara had expected that winning the scholarship would make her happy, and for an hour it did. She read the letter twice, then a third time. But as evening came, she found herself thinking of the train ticket, the strange city, the rooms full of people who had never heard of her town. By the time she went to bed, the letter lay folded on the table, and she had not yet told her mother.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Clara is disappointed that she did not win the scholarship she had hoped for, and she hides the letter so that her mother will not find out.","Clara's mother is upset that Clara has decided to accept the scholarship.","Clara's initial joy at winning the scholarship gives way to apprehension about the changes it will bring.","Clara is eager to leave her town and begin her studies in the city."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Her happiness fades into worry about leaving, and she hasn't told her mother. She did win, she isn't shown as eager, and her mother doesn't know yet.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h292$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many assume that the Industrial Revolution brought immediate gains in living standards. Economic historian Robert Allen notes, however, that in Britain, real wages for workers grew very little between 1780 and 1830, even as output per worker rose substantially. Most of the gains went to profits. Only after about 1830 did wages begin to rise in step with productivity.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Allen's research indicates that British workers' real wages rose rapidly beginning in 1780, keeping pace with the growth in output per worker.","Allen's research indicates that profits fell during the early Industrial Revolution.","Allen's research indicates that productivity in Britain fell between 1780 and 1830.","Allen's research indicates that the early Industrial Revolution's productivity gains did not at first translate into higher wages for workers."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Output rose while wages stagnated until about 1830, with gains going to profits. The other choices contradict these details.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h293$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Laila Noor studies urban coyotes in Chicago. Many residents assume that coyotes in the city survive mainly by eating garbage and pets. Analyzing coyote droppings, Noor found that their diets consist largely of rodents, rabbits, and deer fawns, with human food and pets making up a small fraction. She suggests that coyotes may help control populations of animals that residents consider pests.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Noor's research suggests that coyotes should be removed from Chicago to protect deer.","Noor's research suggests that urban coyotes survive mainly by eating garbage and pets.","Noor's research suggests that urban coyotes eat mostly wild prey rather than human food and pets, and may even benefit residents.","Noor's research suggests that coyotes in Chicago are more numerous than in rural areas because the city offers them plentiful garbage and pets to eat."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Noor's analysis contradicts the assumption and suggests pest control benefits. The choice beginning "Noor's research suggests that urban coyotes survive…" states the assumption she disproved, and the others aren't discussed.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h294$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Scholars of Shakespeare have long debated whether the playwright collaborated with others. Computer analysis of word patterns has strengthened the case that he did. Researchers comparing vocabulary and phrasing across hundreds of plays have identified passages in several of Shakespeare's works, including <i>Henry VI, Part 1</i>, that closely match the styles of contemporaries such as Christopher Marlowe. The findings suggest that collaboration, common among playwrights of the era, was part of Shakespeare's practice as well.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Computer analyses prove that Christopher Marlowe wrote most of Shakespeare's plays.","Computer analyses show that collaboration was rare among playwrights in Shakespeare's era.","Computer analyses show that Shakespeare's vocabulary was larger than that of his contemporaries, including Christopher Marlowe, in plays such as <i>Henry VI, Part 1</i>.","Computer analyses provide evidence that Shakespeare, like other playwrights of his time, sometimes worked with collaborators."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Matching passages support the view that Shakespeare collaborated, as was common then. "Most" of his plays overstates the findings, vocabulary size isn't discussed, and the choice beginning "Computer analyses show that collaboration…" contradicts "common."$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h295$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$For decades, economists measured national prosperity mainly through gross domestic product (GDP), the total value of goods and services produced. Economist Amara Diallo argues that GDP misses much of what makes life go well. It counts spending on cleaning up after disasters as growth, she notes, while ignoring unpaid caregiving, leisure, and environmental damage. Diallo advocates supplementing GDP with measures of health, education, and environmental quality.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Diallo argues that spending on disaster cleanup is the most accurate measure of prosperity.","Diallo argues that GDP is an incomplete measure of well-being that should be accompanied by other indicators.","Diallo argues that unpaid caregiving contributes nothing to a nation's well-being.","Diallo argues that GDP should be abandoned entirely and replaced by measures of leisure time, unpaid caregiving, and spending on disaster cleanup."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Diallo critiques GDP's blind spots and proposes supplementing it, not abandoning it. The choices beginning "Diallo argues that spending…" and "Diallo argues that unpaid…" reverse her points.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h296$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Most people think of glaciers as slow-moving, but in 2012, Greenland's Jakobshavn Glacier was measured flowing at more than 17 kilometers per year, about 46 meters per day. Glaciologist Tavita Leota explains that the glacier's speed increased after warm ocean water melted the floating ice shelf at its front, which had acted like a brake. Without that support, the glacier slid more rapidly into the sea.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Leota explains that the loss of a floating ice shelf allowed a Greenland glacier to accelerate dramatically.","Leota explains that warm air temperatures, rather than ocean water, were the main reason the glacier began moving more slowly into the sea.","Leota explains that the glacier slowed down after warm water melted its ice shelf.","Leota explains that all glaciers move at speeds of more than 17 kilometers per year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ice shelf acted as a brake, and its loss sped up the glacier. The choice beginning "Leota explains that all…" overgeneralizes, and the choices beginning "Leota explains that the glacier…" and "Leota explains that warm…" reverse the effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h297$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Bird migration has long been thought to be guided mostly by inherited instinct. A study of whooping cranes, however, found that the birds' ability to fly a direct route improved with age. Young cranes often strayed far off course, while older birds flew straighter paths. Notably, groups that included at least one experienced adult navigated better than groups made up only of young birds.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The study suggests that cranes migrate only when accompanied by birds of other species.","The study suggests that young cranes navigate more accurately than older cranes.","The study suggests that cranes' migration routes are determined entirely by inherited instinct, since even young birds eventually fly direct routes.","The study suggests that cranes' migratory skill develops with experience and can be passed on socially."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Improvement with age and the benefit of experienced group members point to learning and social transmission. The choice beginning "The study suggests that cranes' migration…" is the view the study complicates, the choice beginning "The study suggests that young…" reverses the finding, and other species aren't mentioned.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h298$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1909 novel.<br><br>The new schoolmistress arrived on a Tuesday with a single trunk and a great many opinions. By Friday she had rearranged the desks, banished the dunce's stool to the woodshed, and begun reading poetry aloud each afternoon. The parents were divided. Some declared her a menace; others, noticing that their children now ran to school instead of dragging their feet, were inclined to wait and see.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["A new teacher leaves the town after parents complain about her methods.","A new teacher arrives in town and is immediately welcomed by every family, since the children now run to school eagerly.","A new teacher quickly makes bold changes that provoke mixed reactions in the community.","A new teacher refuses to change any of the school's traditional practices."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The schoolmistress makes rapid changes, and parents are "divided." The other choices contradict the text's mixed reaction, her changes, or her continued presence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h299$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Economist Paolo Ricci studied the effects of a program that gave cash directly to poor households in rural Kenya, with no conditions on how it was spent. Critics had predicted that recipients would waste the money. Instead, Ricci found that households invested in livestock, metal roofs, and small businesses, and that spending on alcohol and tobacco did not increase. Local prices rose only slightly, and neighboring households that did not receive cash also saw modest gains.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Ricci found that cash transfers caused sharp price increases in local markets that harmed neighboring households that did not receive any cash.","Ricci found that most households receiving cash spent it on alcohol and tobacco.","Ricci found that cash transfers work only when strict conditions are placed on spending.","Ricci found that unconditional cash transfers led to productive uses of money and broader benefits, contrary to critics' expectations."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Recipients invested productively, harmful spending didn't rise, and neighbors also gained. The other choices contradict these findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h300$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many species of trees in a forest are connected underground by networks of fungi that link their roots. Some researchers have described these networks as a "wood wide web" through which trees share nutrients and warn one another of danger. Ecologist Justine Karst has reviewed the evidence and argues that such claims go beyond what field studies have shown: while fungal networks clearly exist, evidence that trees deliberately share resources through them in natural forests is limited and inconsistent.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Karst argues that fungal networks connecting tree roots do not actually exist in natural forests and have been observed only in laboratory experiments.","Karst argues that fungal networks are more common in laboratory settings than in forests.","Karst argues that popular claims about trees sharing resources through fungal networks are not well supported by field evidence.","Karst argues that trees use fungal networks to warn one another of insect attacks."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Karst accepts that the networks exist but says evidence for deliberate sharing in the wild is limited. She doesn't deny their existence or endorse the warning claim, and she makes no lab-versus-forest comparison.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h301$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$In the 1800s, many observers believed that the poor health of factory workers was due to their own habits, such as drinking. Physician Rudolf Virchow, sent to investigate a typhus epidemic in Upper Silesia in 1848, reached a different conclusion. He reported that poverty, poor housing, and a lack of education and political rights had made the population vulnerable. Medicine, Virchow argued, must address social conditions, not just treat individual patients.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Virchow concluded that drinking and other personal habits of factory workers were the main cause of the typhus epidemic in Upper Silesia.","Virchow concluded that doctors should focus only on treating individual patients.","Virchow concluded that social conditions, rather than individual habits, were central to the population's poor health.","Virchow concluded that the typhus epidemic was caused by contaminated water supplies."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Virchow blamed poverty, housing, and lack of rights, and he called for medicine to address social conditions. The other choices contradict him or introduce an unmentioned cause.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h302$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many art historians have treated quilts made by women in Gee's Bend, Alabama, as folk crafts. When the quilts were exhibited at major art museums in the early 2000s, critics compared their bold geometric designs to the work of abstract painters such as Paul Klee. Curator Bill Arnett argued that the quilts should be understood as significant works of American art in their own right, not merely as household objects.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The quilts of Gee's Bend were made primarily for display in art museums.","The quilts of Gee's Bend came to be recognized by some as important works of art rather than only as crafts.","Art historians have always regarded the quilts of Gee's Bend as works of fine art comparable to the abstract paintings of Paul Klee.","The quilts of Gee's Bend were inspired directly by the paintings of Paul Klee."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes a shift from treating the quilts as folk crafts to recognizing them as art. Klee is a comparison, not an influence, the choice beginning "Art historians have always…" contradicts "folk crafts," and the quilts were household objects.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h303$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Studies of human memory show that each time people recall an event, they reconstruct it rather than replaying a fixed recording. Psychologist Elizabeth Loftus found that subtle wording can alter memories: participants who were asked how fast cars were going when they "smashed" into each other later reported seeing broken glass that was not in the video, more often than those asked about cars that "hit" each other.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Research suggests that people remember car accidents more accurately than other events.","Research suggests that broken glass is the detail most often remembered after car accidents, regardless of how questions about them are worded.","Research suggests that memories are reconstructed and can be altered by the way questions are asked.","Research suggests that memories are fixed recordings that remain unchanged over time."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text says memories are reconstructed and shows wording altering recall. The choice beginning "Research suggests that memories are fixed…" contradicts this, and the others misread Loftus's specific example.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h304$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The city of Medellín, Colombia, once known for violence, built cable cars and outdoor escalators in the early 2000s to connect hillside neighborhoods to the city center. Residents who had spent hours walking or riding buses could now reach jobs and services in minutes. Researchers found that violent crime declined more in neighborhoods near the new stations than in similar neighborhoods farther away, though they caution that other programs launched at the same time may also have contributed.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Medellín's cable cars and outdoor escalators were built mainly to attract tourists to the city's hillside neighborhoods rather than to serve residents.","Crime in Medellín increased after the city built cable cars and outdoor escalators.","Researchers proved that cable cars alone caused the decline in violent crime in Medellín.","Improved transit in Medellín was associated with reduced crime in nearby neighborhoods, though other factors may have played a role."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text links the transit to greater crime declines but notes that other programs may have contributed. Tourism isn't mentioned, crime fell, and the researchers' caution rules out "proved" and "alone."$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h305$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The following text is from a 1917 poem.<br><br>I have walked the city's length at midnight<br>When every window wore its shade,<br>And heard behind each quiet curtain<br>The small sounds lonely people made.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The speaker describes a crowded celebration in the city at midnight.","The speaker looks forward to returning home to family after a long walk.","The speaker perceives the hidden loneliness of others while passing through a quiet city.","The speaker complains about the noise from crowded windows that keeps the city awake at midnight, long after the shades are drawn."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Walking the city, the speaker senses "the small sounds lonely people made." The city is quiet, there's no celebration or complaint, and no family is mentioned.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h306$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Biologists long considered the naked mole rat an oddity: it lives in large underground colonies with a single breeding queen, much like bees or ants. Researchers have since found that the animal is also remarkably resistant to cancer and can live more than thirty years, roughly ten times longer than a mouse of similar size. Scientists are now studying its cells in hopes of learning how it avoids the diseases of aging.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Naked mole rats live in small family groups in which every female breeds.","The naked mole rat's unusual traits, especially its resistance to cancer and long life, have made it a focus of research into aging.","Scientists have already discovered how naked mole rats avoid cancer and all the other diseases of aging by studying the animals' cells.","Naked mole rats live about as long as mice of similar size."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text describes the mole rat's longevity and cancer resistance and the research they inspire. The choices beginning "Naked mole rats live in…" and "Naked mole rats live about…" contradict the text, and scientists are still studying the question.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h307$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Historians once believed that the Vikings were purely raiders who plundered European coasts. Excavations of Viking settlements, however, have revealed extensive trade networks reaching from Baghdad to Newfoundland. Coins from the Islamic world, silk from Byzantium, and glass from the Rhineland have all been found at Scandinavian sites. Historian Ingrid Solberg argues that trade, not raiding, was the main source of Viking wealth.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Archaeological evidence shows that the Vikings were the first Europeans to reach Baghdad.","Archaeological evidence shows that most Viking wealth came from silk and glass produced in Scandinavia and sold to Byzantium and the Islamic world.","Archaeological evidence shows that the Vikings never conducted raids on European coasts.","Archaeological evidence has led Solberg to argue that the Vikings were primarily traders rather than raiders."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Trade goods from distant regions support Solberg's view that trade was the main source of wealth. She doesn't deny raids, the text makes no "first" claim, and the silk came from Byzantium.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h308$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Physicians have traditionally prescribed rest for patients recovering from cancer treatment, assuming that exercise would worsen fatigue. Research led by Kerry Courneya has found the opposite: patients who followed structured exercise programs reported less fatigue, better mood, and improved physical function than those who rested. Some studies have also found lower rates of cancer recurrence among physically active patients.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Research indicates that exercise greatly increases fatigue in patients recovering from cancer, which is why physicians prescribe rest.","Research indicates that exercise has no effect on the mood of cancer patients.","Research indicates that exercise, rather than rest, may benefit patients recovering from cancer treatment.","Research indicates that rest is the most effective way to prevent cancer recurrence."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Exercise reduced fatigue, improved mood, and was linked to lower recurrence. The other choices contradict the findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h309$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The Basque language, spoken in parts of northern Spain and southwestern France, is unrelated to any other known language. While nearly all European languages descend from a common ancestor called Proto-Indo-European, Basque does not. Linguists believe it is a survivor of languages spoken in Europe before Indo-European speakers arrived thousands of years ago.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Basque is closely related to Spanish and French, the other languages spoken in its region of northern Spain and southwestern France.","Basque was brought to Europe by recent immigrants from outside the continent.","Basque is a linguistic isolate that likely predates the arrival of Indo-European languages in Europe.","Basque descends from Proto-Indo-European, like nearly all other European languages."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Basque is unrelated to other languages and likely predates Indo-European. The other choices contradict the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h310$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying the decline of honeybees initially searched for a single cause. Over time, a more complex picture emerged. Bees exposed to low levels of certain pesticides, which alone did not kill them, became more vulnerable to parasites and viruses. Poor nutrition from fields planted with a single crop further weakened colonies. Most scientists now believe that these stresses act together.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Researchers have found that parasites have no effect on honeybee colonies.","Researchers have found that fields planted with a single crop provide ideal nutrition for bees, protecting colonies from parasites and viruses.","Researchers now attribute honeybee decline to multiple interacting stresses rather than a single cause.","Researchers have determined that pesticides alone are responsible for honeybee decline."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text describes a shift from seeking one cause to recognizing combined stresses. The other choices contradict specific points: pesticides alone didn't kill bees, parasites matter, and monoculture weakens colonies.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h311$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The Great Smog of London in December 1952 lasted five days and was blamed at the time for about 4,000 deaths. Later analyses, examining death records over the following months, estimated that the true toll may have been closer to 12,000. The disaster led Parliament to pass the Clean Air Act of 1956, which restricted the burning of coal in cities.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["The 1952 London smog led Parliament to ban all use of coal throughout Britain, though officials underestimated the number of deaths.","The 1952 London smog caused fewer deaths than officials initially reported.","The 1952 London smog lasted for several months before Parliament took action.","The 1952 London smog was deadlier than first recognized and prompted major clean-air legislation."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Later estimates tripled the toll, and the disaster led to the Clean Air Act. The other choices contradict the numbers, overstate the law's scope, or misstate the duration.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h312$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Many people assume that languages without writing systems change faster than written ones. Linguist Marta Okafor tested this by comparing changes in oral and written languages over several centuries. She found no consistent difference in rates of change. Writing, Okafor concludes, does not by itself slow a language's evolution, though standardized written forms may influence which changes become accepted in formal settings.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Okafor found that languages without writing systems disappear more quickly.","Okafor found that having a writing system does not in itself slow language change.","Okafor found that written languages never change once they have been standardized.","Okafor found that oral languages change much faster than written ones."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Okafor found no consistent difference and concludes that writing doesn't by itself slow change. The other choices contradict her finding or go beyond the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h313$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Engineers in the Netherlands have experimented with "sand motors," huge deposits of sand placed just offshore. Instead of repeatedly adding sand directly to eroding beaches, they let waves and currents spread the deposit along the coast over many years. Monitoring shows that the sand has gradually widened beaches and dunes for kilometers in each direction. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["sand motors cause beaches to narrow in the areas surrounding the deposit.","adding sand directly to eroding beaches each year remains the only reliable way to prevent coastal erosion over time.","working with natural coastal processes can be an effective way to protect shorelines over time.","waves and currents cannot move large quantities of sand along a coastline."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Letting natural forces distribute sand successfully widened beaches, which supports working with natural processes. The other choices contradict the monitoring results.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h314$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study, researchers gave participants a series of difficult anagrams to solve. Before starting, some participants were told that the ability to solve anagrams was fixed and could not be improved; others were told it could be developed through practice. Both groups solved about the same number of anagrams at first. But after encountering a series of especially hard puzzles, participants in the "developed through practice" group persisted longer on later puzzles. This finding suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["beliefs about whether an ability can improve may affect how people respond to difficulty.","practice does not improve people's ability to solve anagrams.","the difficulty of a puzzle has no effect on how long people persist in trying to solve it.","people who believe ability is fixed solve more anagrams than people who believe it can improve."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The groups differed only in belief and diverged in persistence after hard puzzles, which suggests that beliefs affect responses to difficulty. The choice beginning "people who believe ability…" contradicts the equal initial performance, and the others aren't supported.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h315$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Ancient Egyptian texts frequently mention a beverage called <i>hqt</i>, brewed from barley and emmer wheat. Archaeologists have found residues of this beer in jars from workers' villages near the pyramids at Giza, and written records list beer among the daily rations issued to laborers. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["beer was a routine part of the diet of workers who built the pyramids.","the workers at Giza were not given rations of any kind.","barley and emmer wheat were unknown in ancient Egypt.","beer was reserved exclusively for Egyptian royalty, priests, and the officials who supervised the workers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Residues in workers' villages and ration lists show beer was a regular part of laborers' diets. The other choices contradict this evidence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h316$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Astronomers studying a distant star noticed that its brightness dipped by about 1 percent every 3.5 days. Such regular dips often indicate that a planet is passing in front of the star. However, follow-up observations found that the star wobbled slightly with the same 3.5-day period, a motion caused by the gravitational pull of an orbiting body. Combined, the two observations suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the star has no planets orbiting it.","a planet orbits the star every 3.5 days.","the star's brightness is unrelated to any orbiting object.","the dips in brightness are caused by the star's own rotation."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Matching dips and wobble with the same period strongly indicate an orbiting planet. The other choices ignore that the two independent observations agree.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h317$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In many cities, researchers have found that neighborhoods with more trees tend to have lower summer temperatures. In one study, a team planted trees in randomly selected blocks of a city and left comparable blocks unplanted. Five years later, summer afternoon temperatures in the planted blocks averaged 2°C lower than in the unplanted blocks. Because the blocks were selected randomly, the researchers can conclude that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["wealthier neighborhoods naturally have both more trees and lower temperatures.","the trees likely caused at least part of the temperature difference.","the unplanted blocks were warmer before the study began.","trees have no effect on summer temperatures in cities."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Random assignment makes it likely that trees, not preexisting differences, caused the gap. Random selection is what rules out the wealth explanation, and the other choices contradict the result or its logic.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h318$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The Svalbard Global Seed Vault stores backup copies of seeds from crop collections around the world, deep inside a mountain in the Arctic. In 2015, researchers in Syria, whose seed bank near Aleppo had become inaccessible during the country's civil war, withdrew seeds from the vault to rebuild their collection in Lebanon and Morocco. This episode suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the seed bank near Aleppo was never affected by the civil war.","the vault can serve its intended purpose of preserving crop diversity when other collections are lost.","the vault is accessible only to researchers working in Arctic regions, who must travel there to withdraw seeds.","seeds stored in the vault cannot be used to grow new plants."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The withdrawal to rebuild a lost collection shows the vault working as a backup. The other choices contradict the episode.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h319$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Paleontologists have found that many species of dinosaurs had hollow bones, a feature also found in modern birds. In birds, hollow bones are connected to air sacs that are part of a highly efficient breathing system. Fossil evidence of similar air sacs in some dinosaurs suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["those dinosaurs may have had a breathing system resembling that of modern birds.","hollow bones made those dinosaurs too fragile to support their own weight without the help of air sacs.","modern birds evolved hollow bones independently of dinosaurs.","dinosaurs breathed less efficiently than any other animals."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Similar air sacs connected to hollow bones suggest a bird-like breathing system. The other choices aren't supported by, or contradict, the evidence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h320$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Linguists have noted that languages spoken in mountainous regions with high altitudes are somewhat more likely than others to use ejective consonants, sounds produced with a burst of air compressed in the throat rather than the lungs. Linguist Caleb Everett has proposed that lower air pressure at high altitude makes these sounds easier to produce. If Everett's hypothesis is correct, then ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["ejective consonants should be equally common at all altitudes.","ejective consonants should be less common in languages spoken in low-lying regions.","air pressure should have no effect on how sounds are produced.","languages spoken at high altitudes should never use ejective consonants, since thin air makes them harder to produce."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$If high altitude makes ejectives easier, they should be rarer in low-lying regions. The other choices contradict the hypothesis or the pattern described.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h321$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Scientists studying a population of lizards on a Caribbean island measured the animals' leg length before a major hurricane. After the storm, they measured the survivors and found that, on average, they had longer front legs and shorter back legs than the lizards before the storm. In experiments, lizards with longer front legs clung more tightly to branches in strong winds. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["lizards with short front legs and long back legs were more likely to survive the hurricane than other lizards.","the hurricane caused surviving lizards' front legs to grow longer.","the hurricane favored lizards with traits that helped them hold on during high winds.","leg length has no effect on lizards' ability to cling to branches."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Survivors had longer front legs, which helped them cling in wind, so the storm selected for that trait. The legs didn't grow because of the storm, and the other choices contradict the findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h322$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In the 1800s, many European doctors refused to believe Ignaz Semmelweis's finding that handwashing reduced deaths among new mothers, partly because he could not explain why it worked. Only after Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch showed that microorganisms cause disease did handwashing become widely accepted. This history suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["Semmelweis was the first scientist to discover microorganisms.","evidence for a practice may be resisted until a convincing explanation for it becomes available.","handwashing has never been shown to reduce the spread of disease, which is why Semmelweis's contemporaries rejected it.","doctors in the 1800s readily accepted Semmelweis's findings."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Acceptance came only once germ theory explained why handwashing worked. The other choices contradict the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h323$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Mariana Costa hypothesizes that a species of desert shrub releases chemicals from its roots that inhibit the germination of competing plants' seeds, explaining why bare patches surround each shrub.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Costa's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Seeds of competing plants germinated poorly in soil taken from beside the shrubs, but normally in the same soil after it was treated to remove root chemicals.","The shrubs' roots extended much farther horizontally than the roots of competing plants in the area.","Competing plants that germinated near the shrubs were often eaten by small animals that shelter beneath the shrubs during the hottest part of the day.","Bare patches around the shrubs were larger in years with lower rainfall than in years with higher rainfall."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the correct choice isolates root chemicals as the cause: germination failed in untreated soil but recovered once the chemicals were removed. The other findings suggest alternative explanations, such as water competition, grazing, or root spread.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h324$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Historian Leon Watts claims that a surge of new bookshops in eighteenth-century London was driven mainly by rising literacy among working-class people rather than by wealthier readers.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Watts's claim?$q$, $q$["Bookshops opened during the period were concentrated in working-class districts and sold mostly inexpensive pamphlets and chapbooks.","Printing costs in London fell sharply during the eighteenth century because of improvements in press design, allowing booksellers to open more shops.","The number of books owned by wealthy London families roughly doubled during the eighteenth century.","Many bookshops in eighteenth-century London also sold stationery, maps, and medicines."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$New shops in working-class areas selling cheap material point to working-class demand. The choice beginning "The number of books…" points to wealthy readers. The choice beginning "Many bookshops in eighteenth-century…" is irrelevant, and the choice beginning "Printing costs in London…" explains supply rather than who was buying.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h325$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Neuroscientist Ayaan Malik argues that songbirds learn their songs during a critical period early in life, after which their ability to learn new songs sharply declines.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Malik's argument?$q$, $q$["Adult birds of the species sang more frequently during the breeding season than at other times of year, repeating songs they had learned when young.","Adult birds of the species, exposed to unfamiliar songs for the first time, learned them as quickly and accurately as young birds did.","Young birds of the species that were raised in isolation produced abnormal songs as adults.","Young birds of the species learned songs more quickly when they could see the adult birds singing."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Adults learning new songs as well as juveniles contradicts a sharp decline after a critical period. Abnormal songs from isolated birds support the importance of early learning, and the other findings don't address adults' learning ability.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h326$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A team of economists claims that a city's new minimum-wage increase did not reduce employment in restaurants.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the economists' claim?$q$, $q$["Restaurant employment in the city grew at the same rate after the increase as in a comparable neighboring city that did not raise its minimum wage.","Restaurant workers in the city reported higher job satisfaction and fewer plans to quit after the minimum-wage increase took effect last year.","Employment in the city's retail sector declined slightly after the minimum-wage increase.","Several restaurants in the city raised menu prices by small amounts after the minimum-wage increase."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Matching employment growth in a comparison city suggests the increase didn't reduce jobs. Satisfaction and prices don't address employment, and retail isn't restaurants.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h327$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Priya Raman hypothesizes that a species of frog uses the pattern of rainfall sounds, rather than temperature or humidity, as its main cue to begin breeding.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Raman's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Frogs bred more frequently in years with above-average rainfall than in years with below-average rainfall, according to a decade of field surveys.","Frogs in the wild were most active at night, when temperatures were lower and humidity was higher.","Frogs exposed to high humidity without rain sounds began breeding calls within a few hours.","Frogs in a dry, warm enclosure began breeding calls when recordings of rainfall were played but not when white noise of the same volume was played."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Breeding triggered by rain sounds alone, with temperature and humidity held constant and a noise control, isolates the sound cue. Rainy-year breeding confounds sound with moisture, and breeding under humidity alone would weaken the hypothesis.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h328$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A researcher claims that students who take handwritten notes in lectures understand the material better than students who type notes on laptops, because handwriting forces them to summarize rather than copy verbatim.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researcher's explanation?$q$, $q$["Students who took handwritten notes reported enjoying the lectures more than students who typed notes, and they attended class more regularly as well.","Laptop users' notes contained far more word-for-word overlap with the lecture, and students whose notes had more overlap performed worse on conceptual questions.","Laptop users wrote down more words per minute than students who took notes by hand.","Students who took handwritten notes spent more time reviewing their notes before the test."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The correct choice ties verbatim copying to worse understanding, which supports the proposed mechanism. Words per minute alone doesn't link copying to understanding, enjoyment is irrelevant, and extra review time suggests a different explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h329$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Geologist Hana Mori claims that a certain lake formed when a landslide dammed a river about 1,200 years ago, rather than through gradual glacial processes.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Mori's claim?$q$, $q$["The lake is deeper at its northern end than at its southern end.","Radiocarbon dating of trees drowned in standing positions at the lake's bottom shows that all of them died about 1,200 years ago.","The lake contains several species of fish that are also found in the river that flows out of it, as well as some that live only in the lake itself.","Glaciers in the surrounding mountains have retreated substantially over the past century."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Trees drowned upright, all dying at the same time 1,200 years ago, indicate sudden flooding consistent with a landslide dam. The other findings don't distinguish between the two explanations.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h330$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Tomás Rivera argues that people who move frequently during childhood tend to have fewer close friendships as adults.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Rivera's argument?$q$, $q$["In a large survey, adults who had moved five or more times as children reported the same average number of close friends as adults who had never moved.","Adults who had moved frequently as children were more likely to live in large cities and to change jobs often than adults who had never moved.","Adults who had moved frequently as children were more likely to keep in touch with friends online.","Children who moved frequently reported feeling lonely during the first few months in each new school."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Equal numbers of close friends contradict Rivera's claim. Childhood loneliness is consistent with it, and the city and online findings don't measure close friendships.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h331$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologist Daniel Okoye claims that the inhabitants of an ancient coastal settlement relied primarily on fishing rather than farming for their food.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Okoye's claim?$q$, $q$["The settlement's houses were built from timber brought from forests inland.","Chemical analysis of the inhabitants' bones shows isotope ratios typical of diets rich in marine protein.","The settlement was located within a short walk of fertile soil suitable for growing grain.","Stone tools found at the settlement include blades suitable for cutting a variety of materials, from animal hides to plant fibers and wood."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Bone isotopes directly reflect what people ate, and marine-protein signatures support a fishing-based diet. Fertile soil nearby could support farming, and versatile tools and inland timber don't indicate diet.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h332$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Psychologist Lena Fischer claims that people's moods are influenced by the weather, with sunny days producing more positive moods.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Fischer's claim?$q$, $q$["People living in sunny climates reported higher life satisfaction than people living in cloudy climates, according to a survey of several countries.","People reported more positive moods on weekends than on weekdays, regardless of the weather.","In a study tracking thousands of people's daily mood reports over a year, mood showed no relationship to sunshine, temperature, or rainfall.","People reported spending more time outdoors on sunny days than on cloudy days."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$No relationship between mood and weather in a large daily study directly contradicts Fischer. Weekend effects don't rule out weather effects, and the other findings either support her or are unrelated to mood.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h333$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Marine biologist Rafael Duarte hypothesizes that a species of reef fish changes sex from female to male when the dominant male in its group disappears.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Duarte's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Females of the species were observed to lay more eggs during the warmest months of the year, when the dominant male was most active in defending the group.","When researchers removed the only male from groups of the fish, the largest female in each group developed male characteristics within weeks.","Groups of the fish that contained more than one male were found on larger reefs.","Males of the species were larger, on average, than females."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Removing the male and observing a female transform tests the hypothesis directly. The other findings describe group size, egg-laying, or body size without showing a sex change.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h334$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Literary scholar Grace Oduya argues that a nineteenth-century novelist revised her novel's ending in response to criticism from early readers.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Oduya's argument?$q$, $q$["Critics praised the novel's revised ending when the book was published.","The novelist revised several chapters in the middle of the novel as well as the ending.","Letters from friends who read the manuscript objected to the original ending, and the novelist's reply thanks them and describes the changes she would make.","The novelist's later novels contain endings similar to the revised ending of the earlier book, suggesting that her taste in endings changed over the course of her career."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Letters criticizing the ending, followed by the novelist's promise to change it, tie the revision directly to the criticism. Later praise, later novels, and other revisions don't show that criticism caused the change.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h335$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a new type of road surface reduces traffic noise more effectively than conventional asphalt.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["On two stretches of the same highway with similar traffic, noise measured beside the new surface was consistently lower than beside the conventional asphalt.","Drivers reported that the new road surface felt noticeably smoother than conventional asphalt, and engineers found that it lasted longer in cold climates.","The new road surface lasts longer than conventional asphalt in cold climates.","The new road surface is more expensive to install than conventional asphalt."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A controlled comparison of noise directly supports the claim. Cost, smoothness, and durability don't measure noise.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h336$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Anthropologist Sun-hee Park claims that the custom of gift exchange in a particular island community serves mainly to strengthen alliances between families rather than to distribute goods.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Park's claim?$q$, $q$["Families in the community that received the most gifts were also the families that owned the most land and held the most important positions in village councils.","The community's gift exchanges take place most often during the harvest season.","Gifts typically pass between families that are not related by marriage, and the same ceremonial objects circulate repeatedly without being used.","Most gifts exchanged in the community are foods that families need but cannot produce themselves."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ceremonial objects that circulate unused between unrelated families suggest exchange serves to build relationships, not to distribute useful goods. The choice beginning "Most gifts exchanged in…" points instead to distribution, and the others don't address the purpose.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h337$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Astronomer Kofi Mensah claims that a newly discovered exoplanet has a thick atmosphere.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Mensah's claim?$q$, $q$["When the planet passed in front of its star, starlight at certain wavelengths was absorbed more than expected for a bare, airless planet.","The planet is about twice the diameter of Earth.","The planet orbits its star once every eleven days.","The planet's star is cooler and smaller than the Sun, and the planet orbits close enough to it that its surface could be warm enough for liquid water."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Extra absorption at specific wavelengths during transit is a signature of an atmosphere. Orbital period, size, and the star's properties don't show whether an atmosphere exists.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h338$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Education researcher Mateo Silva claims that providing free breakfast at school improves students' attendance.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Silva's claim?$q$, $q$["In schools that randomly began offering free breakfast, attendance changed no more than in similar schools that did not offer it.","Students who ate school breakfast were more likely to arrive early.","Students who ate breakfast at school reported feeling more alert during morning classes and were less likely to visit the school nurse with complaints of hunger.","Schools that offered free breakfast tended to be in lower-income neighborhoods."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A randomized comparison showing no attendance difference directly weakens the claim. Alertness and early arrival don't measure attendance, and school location doesn't test the effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h339$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers hypothesize that octopuses recognize individual humans, treating familiar people differently from strangers.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Octopuses learned to open jars to get food inside after watching other octopuses do so, and some later opened jars faster than the keepers who had trained them.","Octopuses in the aquarium were more active at night than during the day.","Octopuses changed color when a new object was placed in their tank.","Octopuses consistently squirted water at one keeper who had repeatedly touched them with a bristly brush but approached another keeper who fed them, even when both wore identical uniforms."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Different responses to two people dressed identically show recognition of individuals. The other findings concern activity, learning, or reactions to objects.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h340$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian claims that a medieval map was copied from an earlier original rather than drawn from the mapmaker's own observations.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian's claim?$q$, $q$["The map shows several regions that the mapmaker is known to have visited.","The map was owned by a monastery for several centuries after it was made.","The map shows a coastline exactly as it appeared on an older map, including errors and a town that had been abandoned a century before the map was made.","The map's colors were made from pigments common in medieval Europe, and its parchment has been dated to roughly the same decade in which the mapmaker lived."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Reproducing an older map's errors and an abandoned town indicates copying. Accurate depictions of visited regions point to observation, and pigments and ownership are irrelevant.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h341$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Nutrition researcher Aisha Bello claims that eating meals late at night leads to weight gain independent of the total number of calories consumed.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Bello's claim?$q$, $q$["Participants who ate late at night tended to consume more calories overall than those who did not, and they also reported eating more snacks between meals.","Participants who ate identical meals, with the same calories, gained more weight when the meals were eaten late at night than when they were eaten earlier in the day.","Participants who ate late at night reported sleeping less than those who ate earlier.","Participants who skipped breakfast tended to eat larger dinners."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Holding calories constant and varying only timing isolates the effect of timing. The choice beginning "Participants who ate late at night tended…" suggests that extra calories, not timing, explain the gain, and the others don't address weight.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h342$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Political scientist Julia Brandt claims that voters in a particular country base their choices more on candidates' personalities than on policy positions.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Brandt's claim?$q$, $q$["Survey responses showed that voters' choices were predicted much better by their agreement with candidates' policy positions than by their ratings of the candidates' personalities.","Candidates in the country spent more on advertising that featured their families and personal stories than on advertising that described their policy positions.","Voter turnout was higher in elections with well-known candidates.","Voters reported watching televised debates more often than reading party platforms."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If policy agreement predicts choices better than personality ratings, Brandt's claim is undercut. The other findings concern media habits, campaign spending, or turnout, not the basis of voters' choices.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h343$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Elena Petrova hypothesizes that a species of plant produces bitter compounds in its leaves mainly in response to being eaten, rather than continuously.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Petrova's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["The plant grows more rapidly in sunny locations than in shaded ones.","Insects avoided the plant's leaves more often than the leaves of other plants in the area, and leaves of every age contained some of the bitter compounds.","The plant's bitter compounds are similar to those found in related species.","Leaves on plants that were damaged by insects contained much higher levels of the bitter compounds than leaves on undamaged plants grown nearby."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Higher levels in damaged plants than in undamaged neighbors show production in response to feeding. Insect avoidance is consistent with continuous production, and the other findings are unrelated.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h344$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Economist Haruto Sato claims that a city's new congestion charge, a fee for driving downtown at peak hours, reduced traffic without harming downtown businesses.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Sato's claim?$q$, $q$["After the charge took effect, peak-hour traffic downtown fell by 20 percent, while downtown retail sales grew at the same rate as before.","After the charge took effect, many drivers switched to public transit for their commutes, and the city used the revenue to expand bus service downtown.","Downtown businesses initially opposed the congestion charge in public hearings.","The congestion charge raised millions of dollars that the city spent on bus service."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The correct choice supports both parts of the claim: less traffic and unchanged business performance. Transit switching addresses traffic alone, and the others concern opinions or revenue.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h345$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that crows can plan for future needs, choosing tools they will need later rather than only those that bring an immediate reward.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["Crows preferred larger treats to smaller treats when both were available immediately, and they used tools to reach treats placed inside narrow tubes.","Crows used tools more frequently in the wild than in captivity.","Crows chose a tool that could be used to open a food box fifteen minutes later over a small treat available immediately, even when the box was not yet present.","Crows quickly learned to use a new tool after watching another crow use it."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Forgoing an immediate treat for a tool useful only later shows planning for the future. The other findings concern frequency of tool use, social learning, or preferences without delay.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h346$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian argues that a sixteenth-century merchant's account books show he traded regularly with partners in West Africa.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["The merchant's will left money to several churches in his home city.","The account books record dozens of shipments over twenty years, each listing goods, prices, and the names of agents in West African ports.","Other merchants in the same city were known to trade with partners in Asia and Africa, and some of their account books also survive in local archives.","The merchant owned a large house near the city's harbor."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Detailed, repeated records naming West African agents show regular trade. The other findings don't concern his West African trade.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h347$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a certain bacterium in the human gut helps protect against food allergies.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["People with food allergies reported eating fewer vegetables and more processed foods than people without allergies, according to a large national survey.","Mice raised without gut bacteria developed allergic reactions to peanuts, but mice given only that bacterium did not.","The bacterium is found in the guts of most healthy adults.","Food allergies have become more common in many countries over the past several decades."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Adding only the bacterium prevented allergies in otherwise germ-free mice, which isolates its protective effect. The other findings don't connect the bacterium to allergy protection.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h348$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Daily Steps by Commute Mode</caption><thead><tr><th>Commute mode</th><th>Weekdays</th><th>Weekends</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Car</td><td>5,200</td><td>6,100</td></tr><tr><td>Bus or train</td><td>8,400</td><td>6,300</td></tr><tr><td>Bicycle</td><td>7,100</td><td>6,800</td></tr><tr><td>Walking</td><td>11,600</td><td>7,000</td></tr></tbody></table>Researcher Anika Rao argues that commute mode, rather than people's general activity preferences, explains most of the difference in daily steps among workers. She notes that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["workers who commute by walking take more steps on weekdays (11,600) than workers in any other group.","workers who commute by bicycle take a similar number of steps on weekdays (7,100) and weekends (6,800), while car commuters take more steps on weekends than weekdays.","workers who commute by car take fewer steps on weekdays (5,200) than on weekends (6,100).","the groups' weekday step counts differ widely, from 5,200 to 11,600, but their weekend counts are much closer, ranging only from 6,100 to 7,000."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$If differences shrink on weekends, when no one commutes, commuting rather than general preference explains the weekday gap. The other choices are accurate but describe single groups and don't compare weekday and weekend spreads.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h349$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rates of Seeds Stored at Different Temperatures</caption><thead><tr><th>Storage temperature</th><th>After 1 year</th><th>After 5 years</th><th>After 10 years</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>20°C</td><td>88%</td><td>41%</td><td>9%</td></tr><tr><td>5°C</td><td>91%</td><td>78%</td><td>52%</td></tr><tr><td>−18°C</td><td>92%</td><td>90%</td><td>86%</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist claims that the benefit of cold storage for seed viability becomes more pronounced the longer seeds are stored.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the botanist's claim?$q$, $q$["After 10 years, seeds stored at −18°C still had a germination rate of 86%, the highest of any group, while seeds stored at 20°C had a rate of only 9%.","Seeds stored at 20°C had a germination rate of 88% after 1 year but only 9% after 10 years.","After 1 year, germination differed by only 4 percentage points between the warmest and coldest storage, but after 10 years the difference was 77 points.","At every storage temperature, germination rates declined between 1 year and 10 years."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The claim is that the gap widens over time. Only the correct choice compares the gap at two time points (4 versus 77 points). The others describe a single group or a general decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h350$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Share of Electricity from Wind and Solar, Selected Countries</caption><thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>2012</th><th>2022</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Country A</td><td>4%</td><td>21%</td></tr><tr><td>Country B</td><td>11%</td><td>18%</td></tr><tr><td>Country C</td><td>1%</td><td>14%</td></tr><tr><td>Country D</td><td>23%</td><td>27%</td></tr></tbody></table>An analyst notes that the country with the largest share of wind and solar electricity in 2012 was not the one whose share grew the most over the following decade: ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["Country C had the smallest share of wind and solar electricity in both 2012 and 2022.","Country D had the largest share of wind and solar electricity of the four countries in both 2012 (23%) and 2022 (27%).","Country A's share of wind and solar electricity rose from 4% in 2012 to 21% in 2022.","Country D led in 2012 with 23%, but its share grew by only 4 percentage points, while Country A's grew by 17."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The statement needs the 2012 leader (Country D) and a comparison of growth showing another country grew more. Only the correct answer provides both. The choice about Country D leading in both years and the choice about Country A's rise each cover only one part, and the choice about Country C's small share is irrelevant.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h351$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Survey: Hours per Week Spent Reading, by Format</caption><thead><tr><th>Age group</th><th>Print books</th><th>E-books</th><th>Audiobooks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>18–29</td><td>1.8</td><td>1.1</td><td>1.6</td></tr><tr><td>30–49</td><td>2.1</td><td>1.4</td><td>1.9</td></tr><tr><td>50–64</td><td>2.9</td><td>1.2</td><td>0.8</td></tr><tr><td>65+</td><td>4.2</td><td>0.9</td><td>0.6</td></tr></tbody></table>A publisher claims that print books remain the most-used format across all age groups, even among the youngest readers.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the publisher's claim?$q$, $q$["In every age group, including ages 18–29, respondents spent more hours on print books than on either e-books or audiobooks.","Respondents aged 65 and older spent 4.2 hours per week on print books, more than any other age group and more than four times their time on e-books.","Respondents aged 18–29 spent 1.6 hours per week on audiobooks, more than older respondents did.","Respondents aged 30–49 spent 2.1 hours per week on print books and 1.4 on e-books."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim concerns print leading in every group, especially the youngest, and the correct choice states exactly that. The others describe single groups or a different format.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h352$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Nest Success of a Songbird by Habitat</caption><thead><tr><th>Habitat</th><th>Nests monitored</th><th>Nests fledging young</th><th>Success rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Forest interior</td><td>120</td><td>78</td><td>65%</td></tr><tr><td>Forest edge</td><td>140</td><td>56</td><td>40%</td></tr><tr><td>Suburban yards</td><td>95</td><td>24</td><td>25%</td></tr></tbody></table>Ecologists hypothesize that nest predators, which are more common near human development, reduce the songbird's breeding success. If the hypothesis is correct, the data are consistent with it because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["success rates fall steadily from 65% in forest interiors to 40% at forest edges to 25% in suburban yards.","fewer nests were monitored in suburban yards (95) than in forest interiors (120).","78 nests in forest interiors fledged young, more than in any other habitat.","more nests were monitored at forest edges (140) than in either forest interiors (120) or suburban yards (95)."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The hypothesis predicts lower success closer to development, and success declines from interior to edge to suburbs. Raw counts of nests monitored or fledged don't account for different sample sizes.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h353$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Test Scores Before and After a Tutoring Program</caption><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Fall average</th><th>Spring average</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tutored students</td><td>62</td><td>74</td></tr><tr><td>Non-tutored students</td><td>71</td><td>75</td></tr></tbody></table>A school administrator notes that non-tutored students had a higher spring average than tutored students and concludes that the tutoring program was ineffective. A colleague disagrees, pointing out that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["non-tutored students' spring average (75) was only 1 point higher than tutored students' (74).","tutored students had a lower fall average (62) than non-tutored students (71).","both groups had higher averages in the spring than in the fall.","tutored students' average rose by 12 points, three times the 4-point gain of non-tutored students."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The colleague's best rebuttal compares gains: tutored students improved 12 points, non-tutored only 4. The choices beginning "non-tutored students' spring average…" and "tutored students had a…" give single comparisons without the gains, and the choice beginning "both groups had higher…" doesn't distinguish the groups.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h354$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Population of Four Cities (thousands)</caption><thead><tr><th>City</th><th>1990</th><th>2005</th><th>2020</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ashford</td><td>210</td><td>260</td><td>250</td></tr><tr><td>Bexley</td><td>180</td><td>175</td><td>230</td></tr><tr><td>Corwin</td><td>320</td><td>300</td><td>270</td></tr><tr><td>Dunmore</td><td>90</td><td>140</td><td>195</td></tr></tbody></table>A historian notes that only one of the cities grew continuously over the thirty years shown: ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["Corwin, which had the largest population of the four cities in every year shown.","Bexley, whose population rose from 180,000 in 1990 to 230,000 in 2020.","Ashford, whose population rose from 210,000 in 1990 to 250,000 in 2020.","Dunmore, whose population rose from 90,000 to 140,000 to 195,000."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only Dunmore increased in both intervals. Bexley dipped in 2005 and Ashford fell after 2005, though both ended higher than they began. Corwin declined.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h355$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Pollinator Visits per Hour to Two Flower Varieties</caption><thead><tr><th>Pollinator</th><th>Variety X (red)</th><th>Variety Y (blue)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bees</td><td>3</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Hummingbirds</td><td>14</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Butterflies</td><td>6</td><td>7</td></tr></tbody></table>A biologist claims that the two flower varieties attract different pollinators, with color playing a key role.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the biologist's claim?$q$, $q$["Butterflies visited both varieties at similar rates (6 and 7 visits per hour).","Bees visited the blue variety far more often than the red (19 versus 3), while hummingbirds strongly favored the red (14 versus 2).","Bees made more visits per hour to Variety Y (19) than any other pollinator made to either variety, while butterflies visited both about equally.","Hummingbirds visited Variety X 14 times per hour."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The contrasting preferences of bees and hummingbirds show the varieties attract different pollinators. Butterflies show no preference, and the other choices give single figures without a contrast.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h356$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Commute Time (minutes), Before and After New Rail Line</caption><thead><tr><th>Neighborhood</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Near a new station</td><td>48</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>1–2 km from a station</td><td>45</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>More than 2 km away</td><td>44</td><td>45</td></tr></tbody></table>Transportation planners concluded that the rail line's benefits for commute times were concentrated close to the new stations.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that support the planners' conclusion?$q$, $q$["Commute times near the new stations fell from 48 to 31 minutes after the rail line opened.","Before the rail line opened, commute times were longest in neighborhoods near the new stations, at 48 minutes, compared with 44 or 45 elsewhere.","After the rail line opened, commute times more than 2 km away were 45 minutes.","Commute times fell by 17 minutes near stations, by 5 minutes 1–2 km away, and not at all more than 2 km away."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Showing that benefits are concentrated near stations requires comparing changes across distances, which only the correct choice does. The others describe one neighborhood or the "before" data.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h357$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Share of Respondents Who Trust Each News Source "a Great Deal"</caption><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>2010</th><th>2020</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Local newspapers</td><td>38%</td><td>35%</td></tr><tr><td>National TV news</td><td>34%</td><td>22%</td></tr><tr><td>Social media</td><td>9%</td><td>11%</td></tr><tr><td>Radio</td><td>29%</td><td>27%</td></tr></tbody></table>A media researcher argues that the decline in trust over the decade was not uniform across sources but was concentrated in one source in particular: ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["trust in social media rose from 9% to 11%, the only increase among the sources.","trust in radio fell from 29% in 2010 to 27% in 2020.","trust in national TV news fell by 12 percentage points, while trust in each of the other sources changed by 3 points or less.","trust in local newspapers was higher than trust in any other source in both 2010 and 2020, though it dipped slightly from 38% to 35%."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The claim needs one source with a large decline compared with small changes elsewhere, which the correct choice provides. The others describe rankings, the one increase, or a small decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h358$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Yield of a Wheat Variety (tons per hectare)</caption><thead><tr><th>Treatment</th><th>Dry year</th><th>Wet year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No cover crop</td><td>2.1</td><td>4.3</td></tr><tr><td>Cover crop</td><td>2.9</td><td>4.4</td></tr></tbody></table>Researchers suggest that cover crops, which improve the soil's ability to hold water, are especially valuable in dry conditions.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the researchers' suggestion?$q$, $q$["Yields were higher in the wet year than in the dry year whether or not fields had cover crops, by at least 1.5 tons per hectare.","In the wet year, fields with cover crops yielded 4.4 tons per hectare.","Without cover crops, yields were 2.1 tons per hectare in the dry year.","Cover crops raised yields by 0.8 tons per hectare in the dry year but by only 0.1 in the wet year."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The suggestion is that the benefit is larger in dry conditions, which requires comparing the cover crops' effect in both years. The others give single values or a general wet-versus-dry difference.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h359$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Museum Visitors by Day of Week (average)</caption><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Before free Thursdays</th><th>After free Thursdays</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>820</td><td>790</td></tr><tr><td>Thursday</td><td>760</td><td>2,140</td></tr><tr><td>Saturday</td><td>2,300</td><td>2,250</td></tr></tbody></table>When a museum introduced free admission on Thursdays, some staff worried that visitors would simply shift from other days rather than new visitors coming. The data suggest that this concern was largely unfounded, because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["Thursday attendance rose by about 1,380, while attendance on Tuesday and Saturday fell by only about 30 and 50.","Tuesday attendance fell from 820 to 790 after free Thursdays began.","Thursday attendance after the change (2,140) was nearly as high as Saturday attendance (2,250), which had long been the museum's busiest day.","Saturday remained the busiest day both before and after free Thursdays began."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If Thursday gained far more visitors than other days lost, most were new rather than shifted. Only the correct choice compares the gain with the losses. The choice beginning "Tuesday attendance fell from…" shows a small decline but not the comparison.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h360$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Lifespan (years) of Pet Dogs by Size</caption><thead><tr><th>Size category</th><th>Average weight (kg)</th><th>Average lifespan</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Small</td><td>6</td><td>13.9</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>17</td><td>12.4</td></tr><tr><td>Large</td><td>32</td><td>10.8</td></tr><tr><td>Giant</td><td>55</td><td>8.6</td></tr></tbody></table>Among many animal species, larger species tend to live longer than smaller ones. Within dogs, however, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["large dogs weigh an average of 32 kilograms, more than five times as much as small dogs, and live an average of 10.8 years.","giant dogs weigh about nine times as much as small dogs, on average.","the pattern is reversed, with average lifespan falling from 13.9 years for small dogs to 8.6 years for giant dogs.","medium dogs live an average of 12.4 years."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"However" signals a contrast with the general pattern, and the correct choice shows that larger dogs live shorter lives. The others give isolated figures without showing the reversal.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h361$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Percentage of Coral Cover on Four Reefs</caption><thead><tr><th>Reef</th><th>2014</th><th>2017 (after heat wave)</th><th>2022</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reef 1 (protected)</td><td>42%</td><td>18%</td><td>36%</td></tr><tr><td>Reef 2 (protected)</td><td>38%</td><td>15%</td><td>31%</td></tr><tr><td>Reef 3 (fished)</td><td>40%</td><td>17%</td><td>21%</td></tr><tr><td>Reef 4 (fished)</td><td>35%</td><td>14%</td><td>18%</td></tr></tbody></table>A marine scientist claims that protection from fishing did not prevent coral loss during the heat wave but did aid recovery afterward.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the scientist's claim?$q$, $q$["Coral cover on Reef 3, a fished reef, rose from 17% in 2017 to 21% in 2022.","The fished reefs had lower coral cover in 2022 than in 2014.","All four reefs lost more than half their coral by 2017, but by 2022 the protected reefs had regained far more cover than the fished reefs.","Reef 1, a protected reef, had the highest coral cover of the four reefs in every year shown, including 18% in 2017 and 36% in 2022."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The claim has two parts: similar losses and different recovery. Only the correct choice addresses both. The others give one reef's data or omit the comparison of recovery.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h362$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Monthly Spending on Streaming Services, by Household Income</caption><thead><tr><th>Household income</th><th>Average spending</th><th>Share of income</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Under $30,000</td><td>$22</td><td>1.1%</td></tr><tr><td>$30,000–$74,999</td><td>$31</td><td>0.7%</td></tr><tr><td>$75,000 and above</td><td>$44</td><td>0.4%</td></tr></tbody></table>An economist argues that although higher-income households spend more on streaming, these services take up a larger portion of the budgets of lower-income households.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the economist's argument?$q$, $q$["Households earning under $30,000 spent $22 a month, 1.1% of income, while those earning $75,000 and above spent $44, only 0.4% of income.","Average spending on streaming rose with each higher income group, from $22 to $31 to $44.","Households earning $30,000–$74,999 spent 0.7% of their income on streaming services.","Households earning $75,000 and above spent an average of $44 a month on streaming services, twice what households earning under $30,000 spent."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The argument contrasts dollar amounts with shares of income, and the correct choice provides both for the extreme groups. The third supports only the first half of the argument.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h363$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Results of a Memory Experiment</caption><thead><tr><th>Study method</th><th>Recall after 1 day</th><th>Recall after 1 week</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rereading</td><td>75%</td><td>40%</td></tr><tr><td>Practice testing</td><td>70%</td><td>61%</td></tr></tbody></table>Psychologists note that the advantage of one study method over the other depends on when memory is measured: ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["rereading produced the highest recall of any method at any time, 75% after 1 day.","both methods produced lower recall after 1 week than after 1 day.","rereading produced slightly better recall after 1 day, but practice testing produced much better recall after 1 week.","practice testing produced 61% recall after 1 week, compared with 70% after 1 day, a decline of 9 percentage points over six days."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The statement needs the advantage to flip between time points, which only the correct choice shows. The others describe one method or a shared decline.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h364$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Annual Rainfall and Tree Height in Four Forests</caption><thead><tr><th>Forest</th><th>Rainfall (mm)</th><th>Average tree height (m)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Forest P</td><td>800</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>Forest Q</td><td>1,400</td><td>27</td></tr><tr><td>Forest R</td><td>2,100</td><td>34</td></tr><tr><td>Forest S</td><td>2,900</td><td>33</td></tr></tbody></table>A researcher suggests that additional rainfall increases tree height only up to a point.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that support the researcher's suggestion?$q$, $q$["Tree height rose with rainfall from Forest P to Forest R but did not increase further in Forest S despite 800 mm more rain.","Forest P had both the lowest rainfall, 800 mm, and the shortest trees, 18 meters, of the four forests, while Forest R had the tallest trees.","Forest S received the most rainfall of the four forests, 2,900 mm per year.","Average tree height in Forest Q was 27 meters, 9 meters taller than in Forest P."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The suggestion is that height rises with rainfall but then levels off, which only the correct choice shows. The others describe the rising portion or a single forest.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h365$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Volunteer Hours at a Food Bank</caption><thead><tr><th>Quarter</th><th>Number of volunteers</th><th>Total hours</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Q1</td><td>120</td><td>1,440</td></tr><tr><td>Q2</td><td>150</td><td>1,500</td></tr><tr><td>Q3</td><td>200</td><td>1,600</td></tr><tr><td>Q4</td><td>240</td><td>1,680</td></tr></tbody></table>The food bank's director was pleased that the number of volunteers doubled over the year, but a staff member pointed out that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["the number of volunteers in Q4 was 240, the most of any quarter.","average hours per volunteer fell from 12 in Q1 to 7 in Q4, so total hours grew only modestly.","total volunteer hours increased in every quarter, from 1,440 in Q1 to 1,680 in Q4, as the number of volunteers doubled.","the largest increase in volunteers occurred between Q2 and Q3."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"But" signals a qualification of the good news, and the drop in hours per volunteer explains why doubling volunteers didn't double hours. The other choices are positive or neutral facts.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h366$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Customer Ratings of Two Phone Models (1–5 scale)</caption><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Model J</th><th>Model K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Battery life</td><td>4.6</td><td>3.2</td></tr><tr><td>Camera</td><td>3.4</td><td>4.7</td></tr><tr><td>Screen</td><td>4.1</td><td>4.2</td></tr><tr><td>Price value</td><td>4.0</td><td>3.1</td></tr></tbody></table>A reviewer recommends Model K for photographers, noting that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["both phones received ratings of at least 3.1 on every feature, with Model K's lowest rating being for price value.","its camera rating of 4.7 is well above Model J's 3.4.","Model J earned higher ratings than Model K for both battery life (4.6 versus 3.2) and price value (4.0 versus 3.1).","its screen rating (4.2) is slightly higher than Model J's (4.1), and its camera, screen, and battery ratings average about 4.0."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Photographers care about the camera, and Model K's camera rating is much higher. The choice beginning "its screen rating (4.2)…" stresses a negligible screen difference, the third favors Model J, and the choice beginning "both phones received ratings…" doesn't distinguish the phones' cameras.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h367$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Percentage of Adults Who Can Swim, by Country</caption><thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Men</th><th>Women</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Country 1</td><td>82%</td><td>79%</td></tr><tr><td>Country 2</td><td>64%</td><td>41%</td></tr><tr><td>Country 3</td><td>91%</td><td>90%</td></tr><tr><td>Country 4</td><td>55%</td><td>29%</td></tr></tbody></table>Researchers found that the gender gap in swimming ability was largest in countries where overall swimming ability was lowest. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["in every country shown, a higher percentage of men than women could swim, ranging from 1 to 26 points.","in Country 3, 91% of men and 90% of women could swim, the highest rates of any country shown in the table.","Country 2 had a 23-point gap between men (64%) and women (41%), which was larger than the gap in Country 1, where 82% of men could swim.","Country 4 had the lowest rates and a 26-point gap, whereas Country 3 had the highest rates and a gap of only 1 point."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The example must link low overall rates with a large gap and contrast this with a high-rate country. The correct choice does that. The choice beginning "Country 2 had a…" compares gaps but doesn't establish which country has the lowest overall rates, and the others describe one country or a general pattern.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h368$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Annual Visitors to a National Park (thousands)</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Visitors</th><th>Entrance fee</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2018</td><td>840</td><td>$25</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>870</td><td>$25</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>910</td><td>$35</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>960</td><td>$35</td></tr></tbody></table>Some officials worried that raising the park's entrance fee in 2020 would reduce attendance. The data do not support this concern, since ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["the park received 840,000 visitors in 2018, when the entrance fee was still $25, fewer than in any later year.","visitors continued to increase after the fee rose, reaching 960,000 in 2021.","the entrance fee rose from $25 in 2019 to $35 in 2020, an increase of $10, or 40 percent, in a single year.","attendance rose by 30,000 between 2018 and 2019, before the entrance fee was changed by park officials."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Attendance kept rising after the fee increase, which counters the concern. The other choices describe the fee itself or the period before the change.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h369$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Hours of Sleep by Age Group</caption><thead><tr><th>Age group</th><th>School nights</th><th>Weekends</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10–12</td><td>9.1</td><td>9.6</td></tr><tr><td>13–15</td><td>7.9</td><td>9.4</td></tr><tr><td>16–18</td><td>7.0</td><td>9.3</td></tr></tbody></table>Sleep researchers argue that older teenagers are especially likely to build up a "sleep debt" during the school week that they try to repay on weekends. They point out that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["students aged 10–12 slept more on school nights (9.1 hours) than students in either of the two older age groups did.","weekend sleep was similar across all three age groups, ranging only from 9.3 to 9.6 hours on average.","students aged 16–18 slept 9.3 hours on weekends, only slightly less than students aged 13–15, who slept 9.4 hours on weekends.","the gap between weekend and school-night sleep grows from 0.5 hours at ages 10–12 to 2.3 hours at ages 16–18."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Sleep debt repaid on weekends would show up as a growing gap between weekend and school-night sleep, which the correct choice shows. The others give weekend or school-night figures alone.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h370$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Recycling Rates for Four Materials in a City</caption><thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>2015</th><th>2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Paper</td><td>61%</td><td>66%</td></tr><tr><td>Glass</td><td>34%</td><td>31%</td></tr><tr><td>Aluminum</td><td>48%</td><td>57%</td></tr><tr><td>Plastic</td><td>12%</td><td>9%</td></tr></tbody></table>A city report states that recycling rates improved between 2015 and 2023. A council member objects that this statement is misleading because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["plastic had the lowest recycling rate in both years shown, at 12% in 2015 and 9% in 2023, well below every other material.","aluminum's recycling rate rose by 9 percentage points, from 48% in 2015 to 57% in 2023, the largest gain of any material.","rates for glass and plastic actually declined during that period.","paper had the highest recycling rate of the four materials in both 2015 (61%) and 2023 (66%), well ahead of the others."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The objection must show that the improvement wasn't universal, and declines in glass and plastic do so. Aluminum's and paper's figures support the report, and the choice beginning "plastic had the lowest…" notes plastic's low rate without stressing that it fell.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h371$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Weight Gain of Calves (kg per day)</caption><thead><tr><th>Feed type</th><th>Weeks 1–4</th><th>Weeks 5–8</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standard feed</td><td>0.62</td><td>0.80</td></tr><tr><td>Feed with seaweed</td><td>0.61</td><td>0.79</td></tr></tbody></table>Researchers are testing whether adding seaweed to cattle feed can reduce methane emissions. Farmers worry that the additive might slow calves' growth. The data suggest this concern may be unfounded, because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["calves on the two feeds gained weight at nearly identical rates in both periods.","calves on standard feed gained slightly more weight than calves on seaweed feed in both of the periods measured.","calves on standard feed gained 0.80 kg per day in weeks 5–8, up from 0.62 kg per day in weeks 1–4, an increase of 0.18.","calves on seaweed feed gained more weight per day in weeks 5–8 (0.79 kg) than they had in weeks 1–4 (0.61 kg)."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Nearly identical gains show that seaweed didn't slow growth. The choices tracking standard-feed calves from 0.62 to 0.80 kg per day and seaweed-feed calves from 0.61 to 0.79 kg per day describe changes over time within one group, and the choice saying standard-feed calves gained slightly more emphasizes a tiny difference that sounds like support for the concern.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h372$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Languages Spoken at Home by Students in a School District</caption><thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>2010</th><th>2020</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>English only</td><td>71%</td><td>58%</td></tr><tr><td>Spanish</td><td>18%</td><td>24%</td></tr><tr><td>Vietnamese</td><td>4%</td><td>7%</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>7%</td><td>11%</td></tr></tbody></table>The district's superintendent argues for hiring more multilingual staff, noting that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["Vietnamese was spoken at home by 4% of students in 2010 and by 7% of students in 2020, nearly doubling.","Spanish was the most commonly spoken language at home after English in both 2010 (18%) and 2020 (24%).","the share of students speaking a language other than English at home rose from 29% to 42%.","English only remained the most common home language among students in the district in both years shown."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The overall rise in non-English home languages (100 − 71 = 29% to 100 − 58 = 42%) best supports hiring multilingual staff broadly. The other choices describe single languages or a fact that weakens the argument.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h373$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Test Scores by Hours of Weekly Practice</caption><thead><tr><th>Weekly practice</th><th>Average score</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0–1 hours</td><td>58</td></tr><tr><td>2–3 hours</td><td>71</td></tr><tr><td>4–5 hours</td><td>79</td></tr><tr><td>6–7 hours</td><td>81</td></tr><tr><td>8+ hours</td><td>82</td></tr></tbody></table>A coach suggests that the benefits of practice diminish beyond a certain amount.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that support the coach's suggestion?$q$, $q$["Average scores increased with every additional category of weekly practice time, from 58 points up to 82 points.","Students who practiced 0–1 hours per week had an average score of 58, the lowest of the five practice groups shown.","Scores rose by 13 points from 0–1 to 2–3 hours but by only 1 point from 6–7 to 8+ hours.","Students who practiced 8 or more hours per week had the highest average score, 82, of any of the five groups."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Diminishing benefits means smaller gains at higher levels, which only the correct choice shows. The others present overall increases or single groups.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h374$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Share of Commuters Using Each Mode in a City</caption><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>2000</th><th>2020</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Drive alone</td><td>68%</td><td>61%</td></tr><tr><td>Public transit</td><td>14%</td><td>15%</td></tr><tr><td>Bicycle</td><td>1%</td><td>6%</td></tr><tr><td>Work from home</td><td>3%</td><td>12%</td></tr></tbody></table>An urban planner claims that the decline in solo driving was driven more by changes in where people work than by improvements in public transit.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the planner's claim?$q$, $q$["Public transit's share of commuters rose slightly, from 14% in 2000 to 15% in 2020, a gain of 1 percentage point.","Bicycle commuting rose from 1% to 6%, a sixfold increase that was the largest relative change of any mode in the table.","Working from home rose by 9 percentage points, while public transit use rose by only 1.","Driving alone remained by far the most common mode of commuting in both 2000 (68%) and 2020 (61%)."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The claim compares working from home with transit, and the correct choice contrasts their gains. Bicycling and the overall rank of driving don't address the comparison, and the choice beginning "Public transit's share of…" gives transit alone.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h375$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Price of a Dozen Eggs at Four Stores</caption><thead><tr><th>Store</th><th>January</th><th>June</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Store W</td><td>$3.20</td><td>$4.10</td></tr><tr><td>Store X</td><td>$2.90</td><td>$3.80</td></tr><tr><td>Store Y</td><td>$3.50</td><td>$3.60</td></tr><tr><td>Store Z</td><td>$3.10</td><td>$4.00</td></tr></tbody></table>A reporter writes that one store largely avoided the price increase seen elsewhere between January and June: ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["Store W had the highest price of any store in June, $4.10, which was $0.90 more than its January price of $3.20.","Store X had the lowest price of the four stores in both January ($2.90) and June ($3.80), making it the cheapest option.","Store Y had the highest price of any store in January, $3.50, before the other stores raised their prices.","Store Y's price rose by only $0.10, while the other stores' prices each rose by $0.90."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Avoiding the increase means Store Y's tiny rise compared with the others' larger rises, which only the correct choice gives. The others describe rankings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h376$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Results of a Hand-Washing Campaign in Schools</caption><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Sick days per student, before</th><th>Sick days per student, after</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Schools with campaign</td><td>4.8</td><td>3.1</td></tr><tr><td>Schools without campaign</td><td>4.6</td><td>4.4</td></tr></tbody></table>Health officials concluded that the campaign likely reduced student illness.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that support this conclusion?$q$, $q$["Sick days fell by 1.7 per student in schools with the campaign but by only 0.2 in schools without it.","Schools with the campaign had 3.1 sick days per student afterward, compared with 4.8 sick days before it began.","Schools without the campaign had fewer sick days per student before the campaign (4.6) than schools with the campaign (4.8).","Schools without the campaign had 4.4 sick days per student afterward, the highest figure of any group after the campaign."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Comparing the declines in the two groups supports the campaign's effect. The choice beginning "Schools with the campaign…" shows only a before-and-after change in one group, which other factors could explain.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h377$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Number of Species Observed in Garden Plots</caption><thead><tr><th>Plot type</th><th>Bees</th><th>Butterflies</th><th>Beetles</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lawn</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Native wildflowers</td><td>21</td><td>14</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Ornamental flowers</td><td>9</td><td>8</td><td>12</td></tr></tbody></table>Researchers recommend native wildflower plantings for supporting pollinator diversity. They note that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["ornamental flower plots had 9 species of bees, three times as many as lawn plots had, which recorded only 3 species.","beetles were the most diverse group in lawn plots and in ornamental flower plots, with 11 and 12 species respectively.","native wildflower plots had more species of bees and butterflies than either other type of plot.","lawn plots had 11 species of beetles, more than they had of bees and butterflies combined, which numbered only 5 species."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Bees and butterflies are pollinators, and native plots led in both. The other choices describe other plots or beetles.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h378$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Response Times of a City's Ambulances (minutes)</caption><thead><tr><th>District</th><th>2019</th><th>2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>North</td><td>8.2</td><td>7.9</td></tr><tr><td>South</td><td>11.6</td><td>8.4</td></tr><tr><td>East</td><td>9.0</td><td>8.8</td></tr><tr><td>West</td><td>8.5</td><td>8.6</td></tr></tbody></table>In 2020, the city opened a new ambulance station in one district. The data suggest that the station was most likely located in ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["the North district, which had the fastest response times of any district in both 2019 (8.2 minutes) and 2023 (7.9 minutes).","the West district, which was the only district whose response time got slightly worse between 2019 and 2023, rising to 8.6.","the East district, whose response time improved from 9.0 minutes in 2019 to 8.8 minutes in 2023, a small improvement.","the South district, where response times fell by more than 3 minutes while other districts changed by less than half a minute."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A new station would cause a large improvement in one district, and only South improved dramatically. The other choices describe minor changes or rankings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h379$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Share of Income Saved, by Age</caption><thead><tr><th>Age group</th><th>1995</th><th>2020</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>25–34</td><td>6%</td><td>9%</td></tr><tr><td>35–44</td><td>8%</td><td>8%</td></tr><tr><td>45–54</td><td>11%</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>55–64</td><td>13%</td><td>12%</td></tr></tbody></table>Economists noted that younger workers were the only group whose saving rate rose: ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["the saving rate for workers aged 35–44 was 8% in both 1995 and 2020, the only group whose rate was unchanged.","workers aged 55–64 saved the largest share of their income in both 1995 (13%) and 2020 (12%) of any age group.","in both years, older age groups saved a larger share of their income than younger age groups did, by up to 7 points.","the rate for ages 25–34 rose from 6% to 9%, while the rates for older groups stayed the same or fell."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The statement requires that only the youngest group's rate rose, and the correct choice shows this with a comparison to the other groups. The others describe older groups or rankings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h380$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Fish Counts Before and After Dam Removal</caption><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Before removal</th><th>3 years after</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Salmon</td><td>120</td><td>1,450</td></tr><tr><td>Trout</td><td>840</td><td>910</td></tr><tr><td>Lamprey</td><td>15</td><td>390</td></tr><tr><td>Bass</td><td>300</td><td>210</td></tr></tbody></table>Biologists predicted that removing the dam would especially benefit migratory species, such as salmon and lamprey, that travel between the river and the sea. The data support this prediction because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["bass counts fell from 300 before removal to 210 three years after removal, a decline of 90 fish, or 30 percent.","trout counts rose slightly after the dam was removed, from 840 before removal to 910 three years after removal.","salmon and lamprey counts each rose more than tenfold, far exceeding the change in other species.","salmon had the largest count of any species three years after removal, 1,450 fish, up from only 120 before removal."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The prediction concerns migratory species benefiting especially, so both salmon and lamprey must be compared with the others. The choice beginning "salmon had the largest…" covers salmon only.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h381$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Rent for a One-Bedroom Apartment</caption><thead><tr><th>Neighborhood</th><th>2015</th><th>2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Harbor</td><td>$1,200</td><td>$1,900</td></tr><tr><td>Midtown</td><td>$1,600</td><td>$1,950</td></tr><tr><td>Northside</td><td>$950</td><td>$1,450</td></tr><tr><td>Riverside</td><td>$1,100</td><td>$1,250</td></tr></tbody></table>A housing advocate notes that rents in Harbor, once well below those in Midtown, had nearly caught up by 2023: ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["Harbor's rent rose by $700, more than in any other neighborhood shown, while Riverside's rose by only $150 over the same period.","Northside's rent rose by $500 between 2015 and 2023, an increase of more than 50 percent over its 2015 level of $950.","Midtown had the highest rent of any neighborhood in both 2015 ($1,600) and 2023 ($1,950), ahead of the other three neighborhoods.","the gap between the two neighborhoods shrank from $400 to $50."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Nearly caught up" requires comparing the gap over time: $400 shrank to $50. The choice beginning "Harbor's rent rose by…" gives Harbor's increase but not the gap with Midtown.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h382$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Plant Growth Under Different Light Colors (cm after 30 days)</caption><thead><tr><th>Light color</th><th>Lettuce</th><th>Basil</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Red</td><td>14.2</td><td>18.5</td></tr><tr><td>Blue</td><td>11.8</td><td>12.1</td></tr><tr><td>Red and blue</td><td>16.5</td><td>17.9</td></tr><tr><td>White</td><td>13.0</td><td>15.2</td></tr></tbody></table>A grower claims that the best light color depends on the crop.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that support the grower's claim?$q$, $q$["Both lettuce and basil grew less under blue light than under any other color, at 11.8 cm and 12.1 cm respectively.","Lettuce grew 16.5 cm under red and blue light, more than it grew under white light, which produced 13.0 cm.","Lettuce grew tallest under red and blue light, while basil grew tallest under red light alone.","Basil grew taller than lettuce under every light color tested, by margins ranging from 0.3 cm to 4.3 cm."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Different crops doing best under different colors supports the claim. The others show patterns shared by both crops or describe one crop.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h383$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Bluefin tuna can keep their muscles several degrees warmer than the surrounding water, an unusual ability among fish. Warm muscles contract faster, allowing tuna to swim powerfully in cold water. Researchers tracking tagged tuna found that the fish regularly dive into deep, frigid water to hunt and then return to the warmer surface. Because most of their prey in deep water are cold-blooded and therefore sluggish, the researchers concluded that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the tuna avoids hunting in deep water because its muscles lose their warmth too quickly there to swim powerfully.","the tuna's prey in deep water are probably able to warm their muscles as effectively as the tuna can.","the tuna must spend most of its time in deep water to keep its muscles warm enough to swim powerfully at the surface.","the tuna's warm muscles likely give it a speed advantage over prey in cold depths."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Warm muscles in cold water, hunting sluggish cold-blooded prey, imply a speed advantage. The tuna returns to the surface, the prey are cold-blooded, and the tuna does hunt in deep water.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h384$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Historians studying a medieval English village found that tax records from 1340 list forty-two households, while records from 1360 list only nineteen. Records from 1350 are missing. Over the same period, a severe plague swept through much of Europe, killing a large share of the population in many regions. Unless other records indicate a different cause, the most plausible explanation for the decline in households is that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the village lost many of its residents to the plague.","the village's population actually increased between 1340 and 1360, and the later tax collectors simply counted households differently.","tax collectors stopped recording households in 1360 because the plague had ended and taxes were no longer needed.","residents moved away because the 1350 tax records were lost, leaving them uncertain about how much they owed the authorities."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A sharp drop in households coinciding with a deadly plague most plausibly reflects plague deaths. The other explanations aren't supported and the choice beginning "the village's population actually…" contradicts the records.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h385$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Psychologists asked participants to rate the quality of identical essays. Half of the participants were told the essay was written by a famous author; the other half were told it was written by a student. Participants in the first group rated the essay significantly higher. This finding suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["students write essays that are just as good as essays by famous authors in every respect that participants measured.","beliefs about who wrote a text can influence judgments of its quality.","essays by famous authors are generally of higher quality than those by students, even when readers are told nothing about the authors' identities.","participants in the two groups read different essays and judged each one fairly on its own merits rather than on the author's name."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Identical essays rated differently based only on the attributed author show that beliefs affect judgments. The essays were identical, so the choices beginning "essays by famous authors…" and "participants in the two…" don't follow, and the choice beginning "students write essays that…" goes beyond the study.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h386$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The Atacama Desert is so dry that some weather stations there have never recorded rain. Yet a species of fog-catching cactus thrives along its coast, where fog rolls in from the Pacific Ocean on many mornings. The cactus's spines are covered in tiny grooves that collect droplets and channel them toward the plant's base. It is reasonable to conclude that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the cactus's spines serve mainly to protect the plant from animals that would otherwise eat it during dry periods.","the cactus would grow better in inland areas of the desert, which receive less fog than the coast does.","the cactus obtains much of its water from fog rather than from rainfall.","the coastal Atacama receives frequent heavy rainfall that supports the cactus throughout the year."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In a rainless desert, spines that channel fog droplets suggest fog is the cactus's water source. Inland areas have less fog, the text focuses on the spines' water-collecting role, and the coast is described as rainless.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h387$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A city found that after it painted bike lanes green and added flexible posts separating them from car traffic, the number of cyclists on those streets doubled within a year. On similar streets with only painted white lines, cycling numbers did not change. One reasonable explanation for this difference is that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the streets with white lines were longer than the streets with green lanes, making them less convenient for most cyclists to use.","cyclists in the city strongly prefer the color white to the color green and choose streets accordingly when planning their routes.","the physical separation made cyclists feel safer, encouraging more people to ride.","cycling increased equally on all streets in the city during the year after the new lanes were installed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The key difference is physical separation, which plausibly increases perceived safety. Color preference and street length are unsupported, and cycling didn't increase on the white-line streets.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h388$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists excavating a 9,000-year-old village in Turkey found that houses were built so close together that there were no streets; residents entered through openings in the roofs. Inside, they found burials beneath the floors and walls decorated with paintings that were plastered over and repainted many times. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the village had a central temple that was separate from residents' homes and was used for all religious activities.","the villagers rarely spent time inside their homes, preferring to gather in open streets between the houses.","daily domestic life and ritual practices were closely intertwined in the village.","the paintings inside the houses were created all at once and never altered after the houses were first built."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Burials and repeatedly repainted art inside homes suggest that ritual was part of domestic life. The village had no streets, the paintings were repeatedly redone, and no separate temple is mentioned.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h389$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Economists studied a country that eliminated fees for primary school. Enrollment rose sharply, especially among girls from rural households. However, the number of teachers did not increase, and average class sizes nearly doubled. Test scores of students who had already been enrolled before the change declined slightly. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["test scores declined because the newly enrolled students lowered the quality of instruction directly through their own actions.","eliminating school fees reduced enrollment among girls from rural households, who had previously attended in large numbers.","class sizes decreased after school fees were eliminated, allowing teachers to give each student more attention.","expanding access without expanding resources may involve trade-offs for existing students."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Enrollment rose while teacher numbers stayed flat, classes grew, and existing students' scores dipped, which points to a trade-off. The other choices contradict the data or assert an unsupported mechanism.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h390$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Many plants in the Arctic are dark red or purple rather than green. These colors come from pigments called anthocyanins, which absorb sunlight and convert it to heat. In experiments, red Arctic plants had leaf temperatures several degrees higher than green plants of the same species growing nearby. It is reasonable to conclude that the pigments may help Arctic plants ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["survive in hot, dry climates far from the Arctic, where cooling leaves is essential.","stay warm enough to function in cold conditions.","avoid absorbing sunlight during the long Arctic summer days, when light is most intense.","attract animals that feed on green plants by making their leaves more visible in the snow."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Pigments that convert light to heat and raise leaf temperature would help plants function in the cold. The pigments absorb light, nothing is said about attracting animals, and the context is the Arctic.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h391$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Before the 1850s, many cities in Europe and North America drew drinking water from rivers that also received their sewage. Cholera outbreaks were frequent. After cities built sewers that carried waste downstream of water intakes and began filtering drinking water, cholera deaths dropped dramatically, even though doctors had no effective treatment for the disease. This history suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["cholera is spread mainly through the air rather than through contaminated drinking water supplies.","doctors developed an effective treatment for cholera during the 1850s, which explains the drop in deaths.","cholera deaths increased after cities began filtering their drinking water and building sewers.","preventing contamination of water supplies can reduce disease even without a cure."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Deaths fell after sanitation improved despite there being no treatment, which shows prevention worked. The other choices contradict the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h392$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a long-term study of chimpanzees, researchers found that young chimpanzees learned to crack nuts with stones mainly by watching their mothers. Young chimpanzees whose mothers cracked nuts frequently acquired the skill earlier than those whose mothers rarely did so. Chimpanzee groups in different regions also differed in whether they cracked nuts at all, even when suitable nuts and stones were available. Together, these findings suggest that nut cracking is ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["a learned behavior that is passed on socially within groups.","an instinctive behavior that all chimpanzees perform once they reach adulthood, whether or not they have watched others.","a behavior that young chimpanzees learn mainly from unrelated adults rather than from their own mothers.","a behavior that depends entirely on whether suitable nuts and stones are available in a group's region."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Learning from mothers and differences between groups despite available resources point to social learning. The other choices contradict these findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h393$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Linguists have found that in many languages, the words for "mother" begin with an <i>m</i> sound, and those for "father" often begin with <i>p</i>, <i>b</i>, <i>t</i>, or <i>d</i>. These are among the first consonants infants produce when babbling, because they are made with the lips or the tip of the tongue. Since the languages are not all related, a plausible explanation is that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["infants learn the words for \"mother\" and \"father\" before they produce any babbling sounds at all.","all of these languages descended from a single ancient language that used these specific words for parents.","parents in many cultures have interpreted infants' early babbling sounds as words for themselves.","the similarity is due to recent borrowing of these words from English into other languages around the world."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The text links the words to infants' earliest sounds in unrelated languages, which suggests parents adopted babbling as names. The languages aren't all related, babbling comes first, and borrowing from English is unsupported.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h394$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In one study, shoppers at a supermarket were offered free samples of jam. On some days, a table displayed 24 varieties; on other days, only 6. The larger display attracted more shoppers to stop, but shoppers who saw the smaller display were about ten times more likely to buy a jar. These results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["too many options can attract interest yet discourage people from making a choice.","shoppers prefer to see a large variety of jams and are therefore more likely to buy one when more varieties are shown.","shoppers who saw the smaller display were less interested in jam than shoppers who saw the larger one.","the number of varieties displayed had no effect on how many shoppers stopped at the table to taste samples."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$More shoppers stopped at the large display but fewer bought, suggesting that choice overload deters decisions. The other choices contradict the results.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h395$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Coffee prices on world markets rose sharply after a frost destroyed much of Brazil's crop in 2021. Brazil produces roughly a third of the world's coffee. In the following year, coffee farmers in several other producing countries expanded their plantings significantly. A likely reason for this expansion is that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["frost in those other countries had destroyed their coffee crops as well, forcing farmers there to replant all of their fields.","higher prices made growing coffee more profitable for farmers elsewhere.","coffee prices fell sharply after the frost, encouraging farmers in other countries to grow more coffee to make up the difference.","Brazil's share of world coffee production increased after the frost, encouraging other countries to compete for that share."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A supply shock raised prices, which would encourage expansion by other producers. The other choices aren't supported or contradict the text.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h396$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Astronomers have found that many stars similar to the Sun have planets orbiting them. However, the planetary systems discovered so far often look quite different from our solar system, frequently including large planets orbiting very close to their stars. Some researchers caution that the detection methods used so far find large, close-in planets most easily. If so, it would be reasonable to conclude that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["detection methods used so far are equally effective at finding planets of every size and distance from their stars.","large planets orbiting very close to their stars must be the most common type of planet in the galaxy.","our solar system is certainly unique among all the planetary systems in the galaxy, since no similar systems have been found.","systems resembling our own may be more common than current discoveries suggest."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$If methods favor large, close planets, systems like ours may be underrepresented, so they could be more common than discoveries suggest. The other choices ignore or contradict the caution about detection bias.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h397$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In some coastal communities in Japan, stone markers placed centuries ago warn residents not to build homes below them, because tsunamis have reached that height in the past. During the 2011 tsunami, some villages that had heeded these warnings suffered little damage, while others that had expanded below the markers were devastated. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["villages that built below the markers were better protected because they were closer to the sea and its defenses.","the stone markers were placed incorrectly and did not reflect the actual heights reached by past tsunamis in the region.","historical knowledge preserved in local monuments can help reduce disaster risk.","the 2011 tsunami was smaller than all previous tsunamis that had struck these coastal communities in Japan."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Villages that followed the markers' warnings fared better, which suggests the preserved knowledge reduced risk. The other choices contradict the outcome or lack support.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h398$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying a species of desert ant found that the ants could navigate back to their nest in a straight line after wandering in winding paths while foraging. When researchers attached tiny stilts to some ants' legs after they reached a food source, those ants overshot the nest on the return trip; ants whose legs were shortened stopped short of it. These results suggest that the ants ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["rely on landmarks near the nest, which stilts and shortened legs prevented them from seeing clearly.","navigate primarily by smell, following chemical trails they leave behind on their outbound journeys.","cannot find their way back to the nest after foraging unless they follow the same winding path.","keep track of distance by counting their steps."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Longer legs mean longer steps and overshooting, while shorter legs mean undershooting, so the ants apparently count steps. Landmarks and scent wouldn't produce this pattern, and the ants return in a straight line.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h399$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Scholars have noticed that in the earliest surviving copies of a medieval poem, a particular stanza is missing. The stanza appears only in copies made more than a century after the poem's composition, and its vocabulary includes several words not recorded in English until long after the poet's death. A reasonable conclusion is that the stanza ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["appeared in all copies of the poem, including the earliest ones that were made during the poet's lifetime.","contains the oldest language in the poem and was likely written before the rest of the poem was composed.","was probably added by a later writer rather than composed by the original poet.","was composed by the original poet but deliberately omitted from the earliest copies for political reasons."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Absence from early copies and anachronistic vocabulary point to a later addition. The other choices contradict the evidence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h400$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A company introduced a policy allowing employees to choose their own working hours, as long as they worked a set number per week. Afterward, employee turnover fell by a third, but the number of meetings that included the whole team also dropped sharply. Managers reported that coordinating group projects had become more difficult. Taken together, these results suggest that the policy ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["increased employee turnover while improving coordination of the company's group projects.","benefited employee retention but created challenges for teamwork.","had no effect on employees' decisions about whether to stay with the company or leave for other jobs.","made it easier for managers to coordinate group projects by reducing the number of meetings they needed to schedule."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Turnover fell (a retention benefit), but coordination became harder. The other choices contradict one or both results.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h401$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers placed two identical feeders in a garden, one near a dense shrub and one in an open lawn. Small birds visited the feeder near the shrub far more often. When the researchers placed a model of a hawk overhead, birds at the open feeder fled immediately, while those near the shrub often continued feeding. These observations suggest that the birds ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["cannot see predators when they are feeding near dense shrubs and therefore ignore any danger overhead.","prefer feeding where nearby cover offers protection from predators.","visit feeders near shrubs because the food in those feeders is of higher quality than the food in other feeders.","prefer feeding in open areas because they can see predators coming from a greater distance."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Birds preferred the shrub feeder and felt safe enough to keep feeding there despite a hawk, which suggests cover provides protection. The feeders were identical, the birds avoided open areas, and nothing indicates they couldn't see the model.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h402$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In the nineteenth century, the city of Chicago raised many of its downtown buildings by several feet, using thousands of jackscrews, to install a new sewer system beneath the streets. The city was built on low, flat land near Lake Michigan, and before the project, streets were frequently flooded with mud and standing water. It can most reasonably be inferred that the project was undertaken mainly to ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["move the city's downtown farther away from Lake Michigan to protect it from storms.","improve drainage in a city whose low elevation caused persistent flooding.","test new engineering techniques that would later be used to construct skyscrapers in other cities.","make buildings taller so that the city could accommodate a growing population in limited space."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Raising buildings to install sewers in a low, flooded city points to improving drainage. The other purposes aren't supported.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h403$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Volcanologists monitoring a volcano detected a rise in sulfur dioxide emissions and a swarm of small earthquakes beneath its summit. In past eruptions of this volcano, both signals appeared in the weeks before lava emerged. However, a similar pattern in 2009 was not followed by an eruption; the magma apparently stalled underground. Based on this record, the volcanologists would most likely conclude that the current signals ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["raise the likelihood of an eruption but do not guarantee one.","show that an eruption will almost certainly occur within the next few weeks, as it did before most past eruptions.","are unrelated to eruptions at this volcano, since one occurrence of the pattern was not followed by lava.","indicate that magma has stalled underground, as it did in 2009, so that an eruption is now unlikely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pattern usually preceded eruptions but once didn't, so it raises the likelihood without certainty. "Almost certainly" ignores 2009, the choice beginning "indicate that magma has…" treats the exception as the rule, and the choice beginning "are unrelated to eruptions…" ignores the usual association.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h404$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study of 400 small businesses, those that adopted a four-day workweek reported higher employee satisfaction and no drop in revenue. However, nearly all of the businesses that volunteered for the study were in office-based industries such as software and marketing; few were in retail, manufacturing, or health care. Given this, the findings most directly suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["a four-day week would improve satisfaction and maintain revenue for businesses in every industry, including retail and health care.","a four-day week reduces revenue for businesses in retail, manufacturing, and health care, unlike office-based firms.","employee satisfaction in office-based businesses is unaffected by the number of days employees work each week.","a four-day week may work well for office-based businesses, but its effects in other industries remain uncertain."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sample was mostly office-based, so the results apply there while other industries remain untested. The choice beginning "a four-day week would…" overgeneralizes, the choice beginning "a four-day week reduces…" claims harm that wasn't studied, and the choice beginning "employee satisfaction in office-based…" contradicts the satisfaction result.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h405$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying a lake found that its population of small fish had grown sharply in the five years since anglers began removing large numbers of pike, a predatory fish. During the same period, the lake's water became cloudier, because the small fish eat the tiny animals that would otherwise graze on algae. These observations suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the lake's water became cloudier because anglers' boats stirred up sediment while they fished for pike.","pike feed mainly on algae, so removing them caused the algae to multiply and the lake's water to grow cloudy.","the population of tiny algae-eating animals increased after pike were removed from the lake.","removing a top predator can have effects that reach well beyond its direct prey."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Removing pike increased small fish, which reduced grazers and allowed algae to cloud the water, a cascade of effects. Pike eat fish, boats aren't mentioned, and the grazers decreased.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h406$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Historian Nadia Farouk examined letters written by soldiers during a nineteenth-century war. Letters from the first year of the war frequently described the conflict as a noble adventure, while those from the final year rarely did so, instead dwelling on hunger, disease, and the deaths of friends. Because the same soldiers wrote letters in both years, Farouk can reasonably conclude that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the soldiers' attitudes toward the war likely changed as it went on.","hunger and disease were more common in the first year of the war than in its final year.","soldiers who enlisted later in the war viewed it more negatively than those who enlisted at the start.","the soldiers deliberately misled their families about the war in the first year in order to avoid worrying them."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because the same soldiers wrote in both years, the change in tone suggests their attitudes changed. The choice beginning "soldiers who enlisted later…" requires different soldiers, deception isn't supported, and the choice beginning "hunger and disease were…" reverses the timeline.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h407$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A species of bird nests on remote islands that historically had no land predators. Researchers found that birds on islands where rats had been introduced spent much more time guarding their nests and less time foraging than birds on rat-free islands. Chicks on the rat-infested islands also grew more slowly. One explanation consistent with these findings is that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["birds on rat-free islands spent more time guarding their nests because their chicks grew so quickly.","rats directly ate the food that parents would otherwise have brought back to their chicks on rat-infested islands.","parents' increased vigilance came at the cost of food for their chicks.","chicks on rat-infested islands grew more slowly because they were genetically different from chicks elsewhere."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$More guarding meant less foraging, which plausibly explains slower chick growth. Rats eating food isn't mentioned, rat-free birds guarded less, and genetic differences aren't suggested.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h408$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Mathematician Lucia Ortega surveyed hundreds of research papers and found that results proven using computer assistance were cited less often in their first five years than comparable results proven by hand. However, after ten years, citation rates for the two groups were nearly identical. This pattern suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["mathematicians have always cited computer-assisted proofs as often as proofs done by hand.","proofs done by hand become less useful to mathematicians over time and are cited less often after ten years.","computer-assisted proofs are generally less accurate than proofs done by hand and are therefore cited less often.","mathematicians may have been slow to accept computer-assisted proofs but eventually came to rely on them."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Initially lower citation that catches up over time suggests delayed acceptance. Accuracy isn't addressed, nothing suggests hand proofs declined, and early citations did differ.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h409$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Engineers testing a new bridge cable found that it resisted corrosion far better than conventional steel cables in saltwater tanks. In the laboratory, however, the tanks were kept at constant temperature, while real bridges experience repeated freezing and thawing, which can crack protective coatings. The engineers would therefore most likely recommend that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the cable be installed immediately on bridges in cold climates, where corrosion is the main threat.","the cable be tested under freeze-thaw conditions before it is widely used.","the laboratory tests be discarded, since saltwater does not affect bridges in real conditions.","conventional steel cables be replaced with the new cable on all existing bridges right away."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Because the lab didn't test freezing and thawing, further testing under those conditions is the logical next step. Immediate widespread installation ignores this gap, and saltwater is a real concern.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h410$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Linguist Rafael Santos compared how children in two communities learned words for directions. In one community, people describe locations using "left" and "right"; in the other, they use compass directions such as "north" and "east." Children in the compass-direction community mastered their system several years later than children in the other community mastered theirs, but as adults they were far better at keeping track of their orientation in unfamiliar places. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the age at which children learn direction words has no relationship to the language their community speaks.","adults who use \"left\" and \"right\" are better at orienting themselves in unfamiliar places than other adults are.","a direction system that takes longer to learn may still offer advantages later in life.","children everywhere learn compass directions more quickly than they learn the words \"left\" and \"right.\""]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The compass system was learned later but conferred better orientation, a delayed advantage. The choices beginning "children everywhere learn compass…" and "adults who use "left"…" reverse the findings, and the choice beginning "the age at which…" contradicts the difference between communities.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h411$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Many hospitals have introduced checklists that surgical teams review before each operation. In a study of eight hospitals, complications fell by about a third after checklists were adopted. Researchers noted, however, that the hospitals also began holding team briefings at the same time, so ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the checklists alone may not account for the entire reduction in complications.","the team briefings must have increased complications, offsetting some of the checklists' benefits.","complications would have fallen by two-thirds if the hospitals had not held team briefings.","the checklists clearly caused the entire reduction in complications at all eight hospitals in the study."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Because briefings started at the same time, the drop can't be attributed solely to checklists. The other choices make unsupported causal claims.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h412$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Bats that hunt insects use echolocation, emitting calls and listening for echoes. Some moths have evolved ears that detect bat calls; when they hear one, they dive or fly erratically. Researchers found that a few bat species call at frequencies outside the range moths can hear. It is reasonable to infer that these bats ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["cannot detect moths at all, since moths are unable to hear calls at these frequencies.","cause moths to dive or fly erratically more often than other bat species do.","rely on sight rather than echolocation to find moths in the dark.","may be able to approach moths without triggering the moths' escape behavior."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$If moths can't hear the calls, they won't take evasive action, giving these bats a stealth advantage. Moths' hearing doesn't affect bats' detection, sight isn't mentioned, and the choice beginning "cause moths to dive…" reverses the logic.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h413$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A survey asked residents of a city how they felt about a proposed new stadium. Among residents who lived within one kilometer of the site, 72 percent opposed it; among those who lived more than five kilometers away, 64 percent supported it. City officials who want to understand the opposition would most reasonably investigate ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["why residents who live far from the site are more likely to oppose the stadium than those nearby.","why most residents in the city strongly support building the new stadium near their homes.","whether residents across the city share the same opinion about the stadium regardless of distance.","concerns specific to living near the site, such as traffic and noise."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Opposition is concentrated near the site, so concerns tied to proximity are the logical focus. The other choices contradict the survey results.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h414$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The Dead Sea has been shrinking for decades, its surface dropping by about a meter per year. Most of the water that once flowed into it from the Jordan River is now diverted upstream for farming and drinking. Evaporation in the region's hot, dry climate continues at a high rate. Based on this information, it can be inferred that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the Dead Sea is losing water faster than it is being replenished.","the Dead Sea is shrinking mainly because rainfall in the region has increased over recent decades.","diverting water from the Jordan River has had no effect on the level of the Dead Sea.","the Dead Sea will begin to grow once evaporation in the region stops completely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$High evaporation and reduced inflow mean losses exceed inputs. Increased rainfall would add water, evaporation won't stop, and diversion clearly affected the inflow.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h415$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a classic experiment, researchers gave children a choice between one marshmallow now or two if they waited. Later studies found that children's willingness to wait depended partly on whether they trusted the researcher: children who had previously seen the researcher break a promise waited much less. This finding suggests that a child's decision not to wait ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["is unrelated to the child's past experiences with the adults who conducted the experiment.","proves that children never trust adults who offer them rewards for waiting.","may reflect a reasonable response to an unreliable environment rather than simply a lack of self-control.","shows that the child lacks self-control and is likely to struggle with self-discipline throughout life, as the classic experiment proposed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Children who saw broken promises waited less, so not waiting can be a sensible response to unreliability. The choice beginning "shows that the child…" ignores this, the choice beginning "is unrelated to the…" contradicts it, and the choice beginning "proves that children never…" overgeneralizes.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h416$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Dendrochronologists date wooden objects by matching the pattern of wide and narrow tree rings, which reflect wet and dry years, to master records built from many trees. A wooden beam from an old church shows a ring pattern that matches the master record for 1402 to 1488, with the outermost ring corresponding to 1488. If the beam's outer layers were not trimmed away, it is reasonable to conclude that the tree ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["was cut down before 1402, when its oldest ring formed.","grew only during unusually wet years between 1402 and 1488.","was cut down in or soon after 1488.","began growing in 1488 and was cut down about eighty-six years later, in 1574."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the outer layers are intact, the last ring marks the final growing year, so the tree was felled around 1488. The other choices misread the ring sequence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h417$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Anya Petrov studied neighborhoods where community gardens were established on vacant lots. Over five years, reported crime fell more in those neighborhoods than in similar neighborhoods without gardens. Petrov notes that residents of garden neighborhoods also reported spending more time outdoors and knowing more of their neighbors. A plausible explanation for the drop in crime is that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["crime fell equally in all neighborhoods, whether or not they had community gardens.","residents of garden neighborhoods moved away, reducing the number of people who could commit crimes.","the gardens increased residents' presence and connections, discouraging crime.","the gardens attracted police officers who patrolled the neighborhoods more frequently than before."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$More time outdoors and stronger neighbor ties provide more informal supervision. Police patrols and moving away aren't mentioned, and crime fell more in garden neighborhoods.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h418$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers recorded the songs of a population of birds in a city park in 1990 and again in 2020. Over this period, traffic noise near the park increased substantially. The 2020 songs were noticeably higher in pitch than the 1990 songs. Recordings of the same species in a quiet rural area showed no such change. These findings suggest that the city birds' songs ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["may have shifted in pitch in response to increased noise.","changed because of a change in the birds' diet that occurred throughout the region.","became higher in pitch in rural areas as well as in cities over the period.","were unaffected by traffic noise, since the songs changed in the city park."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pitch rose only where noise increased, which suggests a response to noise. Diet isn't mentioned, rural songs didn't change, and the choice beginning "were unaffected by traffic…" is illogical.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h419$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists found that pottery from a coastal settlement became much more uniform in shape and decoration around 1200 BCE, and that large kilns, capable of firing hundreds of pots at once, appeared at the same time. Before then, pottery varied widely and was fired in small household pits. These changes suggest that around 1200 BCE, pottery production ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["moved from large kilns to small household pits that could fire only a few pots.","became more varied because many different households experimented with new styles.","stopped entirely, as residents began importing all of their pottery from distant regions.","shifted from household production toward a more centralized or specialized system."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Standardized pottery and large kilns indicate a shift to specialized production. The other choices contradict the evidence.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h420$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers found that people who were told a wine cost $90 reported enjoying it more than people who were told the same wine cost $10. Brain scans showed greater activity in a region associated with pleasure when participants believed the wine was expensive. These results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["participants could accurately identify which wines were expensive based on taste alone.","the $90 wine contained different ingredients from the $10 wine and therefore tasted better.","expectations about a product can shape the experience of consuming it.","price has no effect on how much people enjoy a product they are consuming."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The same wine produced more enjoyment and pleasure-related brain activity when believed expensive, showing that expectations shape experience. The wines were identical, and the other choices contradict the findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h421$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In many cities, the number of daily bus riders dropped after ride-hailing services such as taxis summoned by app became widely available. Researchers found, however, that rail ridership in the same cities was largely unaffected. One possible explanation is that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["ride-hailing competes more directly with buses, which often serve similar short trips, than with rail.","bus riders and rail riders in these cities are identical populations with identical travel needs.","rail systems in these cities lowered their fares significantly when ride-hailing services became available.","ride-hailing services were unavailable in the areas served by the cities' rail systems."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ride-hailing hurt buses but not rail, which suggests it substitutes for the shorter trips buses serve. Fare cuts and unavailability aren't mentioned, and identical populations would make the difference hard to explain.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h422$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A team analyzing ancient DNA from skeletons in a European cemetery found that the people buried there in 3000 BCE were genetically distinct from those buried in 2500 BCE, who shared ancestry with populations from the steppes to the east. Burial customs and pottery styles also changed during this period. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people from the region migrated east to the steppes, replacing the steppe populations.","burial customs in the region stayed the same despite a change in the population.","the population buried in the cemetery remained the same while only its pottery styles changed.","a significant migration of people from the east may have occurred during this period."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$New steppe-related ancestry along with cultural changes points to migration from the east. The other choices contradict the genetic or cultural evidence or reverse the direction of movement.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h423$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Ecologists found that a particular species of tree produces large crops of seeds in some years and almost none in others, a pattern called masting. In years after a heavy seed crop, populations of seed-eating mice rose sharply, but in years with almost no seeds, they crashed. Seedlings of the tree were most abundant after mast years that followed several lean years. This pattern suggests that masting may help the tree by ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["attracting more mice to the forest, which then plant the tree's seeds in the soil.","providing a steady, predictable food supply that keeps mouse populations stable from year to year.","reducing the number of seeds that survive to become seedlings in every year.","producing seeds when seed-eaters are too few to consume most of them."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$After lean years, mouse populations are low, so a sudden seed glut overwhelms them and many seeds survive. Masting is unpredictable, not steady, and the other choices contradict the seedling pattern.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h424$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Historian Teodor Novak argues that a seventeenth-century Dutch painting, long attributed to a famous master, was actually produced by one of his students.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Novak's argument?$q$, $q$["X-ray images reveal an underdrawing with techniques that match the student's documented works and differ from the master's.","The painting's subject, a merchant's family, was common in Dutch art of the seventeenth century, appearing in many works.","The painting was sold at auction for a record price in the twentieth century, when it was attributed to the famous master.","The master is known to have trained several students in his workshop and to have supervised their work closely there."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Underdrawing techniques matching the student's distinctive methods point to the student's hand. A sale price, the existence of a workshop, and a common subject don't distinguish between the two painters.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h425$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Hana Kimura claims that a species of jumping spider can recognize its own reflection in a mirror as an image rather than as another spider.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Kimura's claim?$q$, $q$["Spiders presented with mirrors spent more time near the mirror than spiders presented with a blank board of the same size and shape, especially in the first few minutes.","Spiders presented with mirrors consistently performed courtship or threat displays toward their reflections, as they do toward other spiders.","Jumping spiders of this species have excellent eyesight compared with most other spiders and can see fine details at a distance.","Spiders of this species are known to recognize prey from images displayed on screens and will stalk the images as if real."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Treating reflections as other spiders contradicts recognizing them as images. Spending time near the mirror is ambiguous, and good eyesight or responding to prey images doesn't address self-recognition.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h426$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Economist Ingrid Larsen claims that a town's new farmers market increased sales at nearby restaurants and shops.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Larsen's claim?$q$, $q$["Several restaurants in the town reported that their sales rose during the summer after the market opened.","Many of the farmers who sold produce at the market also sold their goods to restaurants in the town during the rest of the week.","Sales at businesses within two blocks of the market rose on market days but not on other days, while sales farther away did not change.","The farmers market attracted more visitors in its second year than in its first year, especially on weekends in the summer months."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Higher sales only near the market and only on market days tie the effect to the market. A general summer rise could have other causes, and the other findings don't measure business sales.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h427$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A team of psychologists hypothesizes that people remember information better when they learn it shortly before sleeping than when they learn it in the morning, because sleep helps consolidate new memories.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the team's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Participants who learned word lists in the morning recalled them more accurately immediately after learning than evening learners did.","Participants who learned word lists in the evening and slept recalled more 12 hours later than participants who learned in the morning and stayed awake.","Participants who learned word lists in the evening reported feeling more tired during learning than participants who learned in the morning, and they rated the lists as harder.","Participants who slept more hours on average each night tended to have higher grades in school than those who slept fewer hours."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Better recall after sleep compared with wakefulness over the same interval supports consolidation during sleep. Fatigue, grades, and immediate recall don't test the hypothesis.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h428$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Marine ecologist Dario Rossi claims that sea otters help seagrass beds thrive by eating crabs, which otherwise consume the small grazers that keep seagrass free of algae.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Rossi's claim?$q$, $q$["Sea otters in the estuaries spend several hours each day diving for food, consuming up to a quarter of their body weight daily, much of it in crabs and clams.","In estuaries where otters returned, crab numbers fell, grazer numbers rose, and seagrass beds expanded, unlike in estuaries without otters.","Seagrass beds provide nursery habitat for many species of fish that are important to commercial fisheries in the region.","Crabs in estuaries without sea otters are larger on average than crabs in estuaries with sea otters, by about 10 percent."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The full chain (fewer crabs, more grazers, more seagrass) appearing where otters returned supports Rossi. Otter feeding habits, seagrass's value, and crab size don't establish the chain.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h429$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Political historian Grace Nakamura argues that a nineteenth-century newspaper's editorials shifted from supporting to opposing a railroad project after the newspaper's owner purchased land along a competing route.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Nakamura's argument?$q$, $q$["The railroad project was eventually completed several years later than originally planned, at a much higher cost.","The newspaper's circulation grew substantially during the years when its editorials opposed the railroad project.","Property records show the owner bought land along the competing route in March 1871, and the first editorial opposing the project appeared that April.","Other newspapers in the region also published editorials both supporting and opposing the railroad project at various times during the 1870s, often changing positions."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A land purchase immediately followed by a shift in editorial position supports the claimed link. Circulation, other papers' views, and the project's eventual outcome don't connect the purchase to the shift.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h430$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a new reading app improves elementary students' reading fluency.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["Students who used the app reported enjoying reading more than they had before, and many said they read at home more often, especially on weekends and holidays.","Teachers reported that the app was easy to set up and that students needed little help learning to use it in class.","Students randomly assigned to use the app for a semester showed the same gains in fluency as students randomly assigned to read printed books.","Students who used the app spent an average of twenty minutes per day reading on it during the school semester."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Equal gains with printed books in a randomized comparison show the app provided no added benefit. Enjoyment, ease of use, and time spent don't measure fluency gains relative to an alternative.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h431$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Glaciologist Mia Lindgren hypothesizes that dark dust deposited on a glacier's surface by wind has accelerated the glacier's melting.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Lindgren's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Sections of the glacier with heavy dust cover melted faster than nearby clean sections at the same altitude and slope.","The dust found on the glacier's surface has been traced to a desert several hundred kilometers away from the glacier.","The glacier has retreated by about two kilometers over the past fifty years, faster than many glaciers in the region.","Wind speeds in the region have increased slightly over the past several decades, according to weather station records."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Faster melting in dusty sections than in comparable clean sections links dust to melting. Overall retreat, wind speeds, and the dust's source don't show its effect on melting.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h432$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$An anthropologist claims that in a particular society, storytelling plays an important role in teaching children cooperative behavior.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the anthropologist's claim?$q$, $q$["Many of the society's stories describe the origins of nearby rivers, mountains, and animals, and they are told in a particular order.","Children in the society spend much of the day caring for younger siblings and helping adults gather food in the surrounding forest.","Adults in the society reported telling stories mostly in the evenings, after the day's work had been completed and the meal had been eaten.","Stories most often told to children feature characters rewarded for sharing, and children who heard more stories shared food more in experiments."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Story themes of sharing plus a link between hearing stories and sharing behavior support the claim. When stories are told, children's chores, and stories about origins don't connect storytelling to cooperation.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h433$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a particular species of wasp distinguishes between the faces of individual wasps in its colony.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["Wasps behaved aggressively toward a familiar nestmate whose facial markings had been altered with paint, but not toward nestmates painted on other body parts.","Wasps of this species live in colonies with several queens rather than a single queen, which compete for dominance.","Wasps spent more time grooming nestmates than grooming wasps from other colonies, especially during the breeding season.","Wasps of this species have more varied facial markings than related wasp species do, with patterns that differ among individuals even within the same colony."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Aggression only when a known nestmate's face is altered shows that wasps use faces to recognize individuals. Varied markings make recognition possible but don't show it, and the other findings don't isolate faces.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h434$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Literary historian Owen Price argues that a poet's popularity declined in the 1930s because her style seemed old-fashioned to a new generation of critics.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Price's argument?$q$, $q$["Several younger poets of the 1930s cited her as an important influence in interviews, though they wrote in very different, less traditional styles.","Sales of her books declined in the 1930s, a decade in which the economic depression reduced book sales generally.","She published fewer new poems in the 1930s than she had in the 1920s, focusing instead on essays and teaching.","Reviews of her books in the 1930s repeatedly criticized her rhyme and meter as outdated, though earlier reviewers had praised them."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Critics calling her style outdated directly supports the claim. Declining sales during a depression suggests another cause, publishing less is a different explanation, and citations from younger poets cut against it.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h435$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying a river claim that a factory upstream is the main source of high nitrate levels found downstream.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["Fish populations downstream of the factory have declined over the past decade, as nitrate levels have risen.","The factory's own records show that it releases some wastewater containing nitrates into the river every day.","Nitrate levels were already as high just above the factory as below it, and they rose most sharply downstream of farmland.","Nitrate levels in the river were highest during the spring months, when the factory's production was at its annual peak and its wastewater releases were largest."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If nitrates were already high above the factory and rose most near farmland, the factory isn't the main source. The other findings are consistent with the factory being responsible.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h436$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$An economist claims that a policy giving tax credits for home solar panels mainly benefited wealthier households.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the economist's claim?$q$, $q$["The number of home solar installations in the state roughly tripled in the five years after the tax credit was introduced, across both cities and rural areas.","Households in the top fifth of incomes claimed over 60 percent of the credits, while the bottom two-fifths claimed under 10 percent.","Households that installed solar panels saved an average of several hundred dollars per year on their electricity bills.","The price of solar panels fell substantially during the period in which the tax credit was available to households."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The distribution of credits across incomes directly shows who benefited. The other findings concern overall uptake, savings, or prices without regard to income.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h437$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Primatologist Sana Begum hypothesizes that capuchin monkeys understand fairness, reacting negatively when they receive a lesser reward than a partner for the same task.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Begum's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Monkeys preferred grapes to cucumber when offered a choice between the two foods, choosing grapes in nearly every trial regardless of what a partner received.","Monkeys that worked in pairs completed the task more often than monkeys that worked alone, regardless of reward.","Monkeys that received cucumber for a task readily accepted it alone, but often refused it when a partner received grapes for the same task.","Monkeys performed the task more quickly when the reward was a grape than when the reward was a piece of cucumber."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Refusing cucumber only when a partner got grapes shows sensitivity to unequal treatment. A preference for grapes or faster work for better rewards doesn't involve comparison with a partner.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h438$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian claims that a medieval trade fair attracted merchants from across Europe.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian's claim?$q$, $q$["Coins minted in more than a dozen distant cities, from Italy to Scandinavia, have been found at the fair's site.","Local records show that the town's population grew substantially during the years when the fair was most active.","The fair was held every year in the same town for more than two centuries, usually during the summer months.","The fair was famous for its cloth, which local weavers produced in large quantities during the months before it opened."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Coins from distant cities indicate the presence of merchants from across Europe. Longevity, local growth, and local cloth production don't show where visitors came from.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h439$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that urban heat islands, areas of cities that are warmer than their surroundings, cause trees in cities to leaf out earlier in spring.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["Within a city, trees of the same species leafed out earlier at warmer sites, matching the local temperature differences.","Spring temperatures across the region have risen by about one degree Celsius over the past fifty years.","Trees in cities grow more slowly than trees of the same species in rural forests, partly because of poorer soil and limited space.","Cities contain a greater variety of tree species than surrounding rural areas do, including many introduced species."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Leafing time tracking local temperature differences within the city links heat to early leafing. Growth rate, regional warming, and species variety don't isolate the heat-island effect.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h440$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Linguist Paulo Reyes claims that speakers of a particular language are dropping a grammatical feature, the use of special verb endings for the future tense, in favor of a simpler construction.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Reyes's claim?$q$, $q$["Recordings show that speakers under 30 use the special endings far less than speakers over 60, who use them in most future-tense sentences.","Older speakers of the language reported that they consider the special future-tense endings more polite.","The language's future-tense endings are similar to those of several related languages spoken in nearby regions, where the endings are also used by speakers of all ages.","Many grammar textbooks used in schools still teach the special future-tense verb endings as the correct form."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Much lower use among younger speakers indicates a change in progress. Textbooks, related languages, and politeness judgments don't show a decline in use.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h441$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Clara Mendes claims that a species of tree frog changes its call to avoid being heard by a predatory bat that hunts by listening for frog calls.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Mendes's claim?$q$, $q$["Male frogs with more complex calls attracted more females than males with simpler calls, in experiments in which researchers played recorded calls near ponds.","Frogs of this species call most often on rainy nights, when bats are also most active, near the edges of ponds.","The bat species feeds on several types of frogs as well as on large insects found near forest streams.","When recordings of the bat's echolocation calls were played, the frogs switched to shorter, simpler calls that bats were less able to locate."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Frogs simplifying calls in response to bat sounds, in a way that makes them harder to locate, shows predator-avoidance behavior. The other findings concern mating, bat diet, or timing without showing a change in calls.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h442$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Education researcher Leila Haddad claims that smaller class sizes benefit students from low-income families more than other students.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Haddad's claim?$q$, $q$["In a randomized experiment, smaller classes raised test scores for low-income students about twice as much as for their classmates.","Schools serving many low-income families tend to have larger class sizes than schools serving wealthier families, according to national surveys of school districts.","Students in smaller classes in the experiment reported liking school more than students in larger classes.","Teachers in smaller classes reported spending more time giving individual feedback to every student."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A bigger effect for low-income students than for others directly supports the claim. Existing class sizes, teacher reports, and liking school don't compare benefits across groups.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h443$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Astronomer Ravi Iyer hypothesizes that a faint glow observed around a distant galaxy is produced by stars that were stripped from it during a collision with another galaxy.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Iyer's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["The glow is fainter than the central region of the galaxy and can be detected only with long exposures.","The galaxy is located about 300 million light-years from Earth, in a region of space containing many galaxies.","The galaxy contains a supermassive black hole at its center, as most large galaxies do.","The glow forms long, curving streams extending toward a nearby galaxy, and its light has the spectrum of old stars."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Streams pointing to a neighboring galaxy and starlight in the glow fit stars stripped in a collision. Distance, black holes, and faintness don't indicate a collision origin.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h444$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$For centuries, Europeans regarded tomatoes with suspicion, and many grew them only as ornamental plants. Food historian Ana Ferreira notes that this was partly because tomatoes belong to the nightshade family, which includes poisonous species. Wealthy diners also ate from pewter plates, and acidic foods such as tomatoes could draw lead out of the pewter, causing illness that was blamed on the fruit itself.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Ferreira suggests that wealthy Europeans became ill from tomatoes more often than poor Europeans because tomatoes were too expensive.","Ferreira suggests that tomatoes are poisonous because they belong to the nightshade family, which contains several dangerous species.","Ferreira suggests that Europeans grew tomatoes only as ornamental plants because they disliked the fruit's taste and color.","Ferreira suggests that Europeans' distrust of tomatoes had understandable causes, including a real but misattributed source of illness."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Ferreira explains the distrust through the plant's family and lead poisoning wrongly blamed on tomatoes. The choice beginning "Ferreira suggests that tomatoes…" misreads the family link, the choice beginning "Ferreira suggests that wealthy…" gives the wrong reason for illness among the wealthy, and taste isn't mentioned.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h445$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$The ocean absorbs about a quarter of the carbon dioxide that humans release, slowing climate change. But as it absorbs this gas, seawater becomes more acidic. Marine chemist Leilani Kahale has found that in more acidic water, oysters, corals, and some plankton struggle to build their shells and skeletons from calcium carbonate. Thus the same process that moderates warming may threaten important marine life.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Marine plankton absorb about a quarter of the carbon dioxide released by humans and use it to build their shells.","Ocean acidification makes it easier for oysters and corals to build their shells and skeletons from calcium carbonate.","The ocean absorbs most of the carbon dioxide that humans release, which prevents climate change from occurring at all.","The ocean's absorption of carbon dioxide has a benefit for climate but a cost for some shell-building organisms."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The text presents absorption's climate benefit and its harm to shell builders. The ocean absorbs a quarter, not most, and slows rather than prevents climate change. The other choices reverse or garble the details.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h446$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$Tango is often seen as quintessentially Argentine. Musicologist Diego Ferraz notes, however, that the dance emerged in the late nineteenth century in the port neighborhoods of Buenos Aires and Montevideo, Uruguay, and drew on African-Argentine candombe rhythms, Cuban habanera, and the music of European immigrants. Ferraz argues that tango is best understood as a product of cultural mixing in a particular place rather than of a single national tradition.$q$, $q$Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Ferraz argues that tango originated in Cuba and was later brought to Argentina by European immigrants arriving in the late nineteenth century.","Ferraz argues that tango is purely an Argentine creation that owes nothing to the music of other countries.","Ferraz argues that tango arose from a mixture of influences in port cities rather than from one national tradition.","Ferraz argues that tango was first danced in Montevideo, Uruguay, and only later spread across the river to Buenos Aires."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ferraz emphasizes mixing in port neighborhoods of two cities. Cuba is one influence, not the origin, the choice beginning "Ferraz argues that tango is…" contradicts him, and he doesn't rank Montevideo first.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h447$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Distance Traveled by Tagged Sea Turtles (km per day)</caption><thead><tr><th>Life stage</th><th>Spring</th><th>Summer</th><th>Fall</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Juvenile</td><td>12</td><td>14</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Adult female</td><td>38</td><td>9</td><td>41</td></tr><tr><td>Adult male</td><td>35</td><td>18</td><td>33</td></tr></tbody></table>Researchers note that adult females move much less in summer, when they come ashore to nest, while juveniles travel at a similar rate year-round.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that support the researchers' observation?$q$, $q$["Juveniles traveled less per day than adult females in spring (12 versus 38 km) and in fall (13 versus 41 km).","Adult males traveled 35 km per day in spring and 33 km per day in fall, about twice their summer average of 18 km per day.","Adult females averaged 9 km per day in summer versus about 40 in spring and fall, while juveniles stayed between 12 and 14.","Adult females traveled farther per day in fall (41 km) than any other group in any season shown in the table."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The observation has two parts: females' summer drop and juveniles' steady rate. Only the correct choice addresses both.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h448$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Weekly Screen Time (hours) of Teenagers</caption><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>2015</th><th>2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Social media</td><td>9.2</td><td>13.5</td></tr><tr><td>Video streaming</td><td>8.1</td><td>11.0</td></tr><tr><td>Gaming</td><td>7.4</td><td>7.6</td></tr><tr><td>Homework</td><td>3.0</td><td>5.9</td></tr></tbody></table>A researcher argues that the growth in teenagers' screen time between 2015 and 2023 was not driven by gaming. She points out that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["gaming time rose by only 0.2 hours, while time spent on each of the other activities rose by about 3 hours or more.","social media had the largest weekly screen time of any activity in both 2015 (9.2 hours) and 2023 (13.5 hours).","teenagers spent more time on video streaming (11.0 hours) than on gaming (7.6 hours) in 2023, a difference of 3.4 hours.","time spent on homework on screens nearly doubled, from 3.0 hours in 2015 to 5.9 hours in 2023, the largest proportional increase."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Showing that gaming didn't drive growth requires comparing its tiny change with the larger changes in other activities. The other choices describe a single activity or a one-year comparison.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h449$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Wildfire Area Burned (thousand hectares) in a Region</caption><thead><tr><th>Decade</th><th>Area burned</th><th>Number of fires</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1980s</td><td>410</td><td>2,900</td></tr><tr><td>1990s</td><td>520</td><td>2,750</td></tr><tr><td>2000s</td><td>880</td><td>2,600</td></tr><tr><td>2010s</td><td>1,340</td><td>2,500</td></tr></tbody></table>Fire scientists argue that the region's wildfire problem has grown because individual fires have become larger, not because fires have become more frequent. They note that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["the number of fires fell in every decade shown, from 2,900 in the 1980s to 2,500 in the 2010s, a decline of 400 fires.","the area burned increased from 410 thousand hectares in the 1980s to 520 thousand hectares in the 1990s.","the area burned in the 2010s, 1,340 thousand hectares, was greater than in any other decade shown in the table.","the area burned more than tripled from the 1980s to the 2010s even as the number of fires declined."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Rising area alongside falling numbers means each fire is larger. Only the correct choice combines both trends.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h450$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Survey of Remote Workers: Change in Productivity</caption><thead><tr><th>Job type</th><th>Reported increase</th><th>No change</th><th>Reported decrease</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Software developers</td><td>58%</td><td>30%</td><td>12%</td></tr><tr><td>Customer service</td><td>22%</td><td>41%</td><td>37%</td></tr><tr><td>Graphic designers</td><td>49%</td><td>36%</td><td>15%</td></tr></tbody></table>An analyst concludes that the effect of remote work on productivity varies considerably by type of job.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the analyst's conclusion?$q$, $q$["58% of software developers reported an increase, compared with only 22% of customer service workers, 37% of whom reported a decrease.","Among customer service workers, the most common response was no change, reported by 41% of those surveyed.","Graphic designers were more likely to report an increase in productivity (49%) than to report no change (36%) or a decrease (15%).","In every job type shown, at least 12% of workers reported a decrease in their productivity while working remotely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Variation across jobs requires contrasting job types, which the correct choice does. The others describe one job type or a pattern common to all.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h451$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Mass of Food Waste per Household per Week (kg)</caption><thead><tr><th>Household size</th><th>Total waste</th><th>Waste per person</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 person</td><td>3.1</td><td>3.1</td></tr><tr><td>2 people</td><td>4.8</td><td>2.4</td></tr><tr><td>4 people</td><td>7.6</td><td>1.9</td></tr></tbody></table>A sustainability researcher notes that although larger households throw away more food in total, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["four-person households wasted 7.6 kg per week, more than twice the 3.1 kg wasted by one-person households.","they waste less per person, from 3.1 kg in one-person households to 1.9 kg in four-person households.","two-person households wasted 4.8 kg per week in total, which works out to 2.4 kg per person each week.","one-person households wasted 3.1 kg per week, the least total waste of any household size in the table."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Although" sets up a contrast with total waste, and per-person waste falling with household size provides it. The other choices restate totals or single households.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h452$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Time to Complete a Puzzle (seconds)</caption><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Trial 1</th><th>Trial 5</th><th>Trial 10</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Children</td><td>95</td><td>48</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>Adults</td><td>62</td><td>44</td><td>38</td></tr></tbody></table>Psychologists observed that although adults solved the puzzle faster at first, children improved more quickly with practice and ultimately outperformed them: ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["adults improved their completion time by 24 seconds between trial 1 and trial 10, from 62 seconds to 38 seconds.","by trial 5, children's average time (48 seconds) was only 4 seconds slower than adults' average time (44 seconds).","children went from 95 to 31 seconds, while adults went from 62 to 38, so by trial 10 children were faster.","children took 95 seconds on trial 1, 33 seconds longer than adults, who took only 62 seconds on their first trial."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The statement requires children's larger improvement and final advantage, which only the correct choice shows. The others describe one group or an intermediate point.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h453$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Number of Bird Species Recorded in a Restored Wetland</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Waterbirds</th><th>Songbirds</th><th>Raptors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Before restoration (2010)</td><td>8</td><td>22</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>2015</td><td>27</td><td>24</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>34</td><td>25</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody></table>Ecologists report that the wetland restoration benefited waterbirds far more than songbirds.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that support this report?$q$, $q$["Waterbird species rose from 8 to 34, while songbird species rose only from 22 to 25.","Raptor species doubled from 3 before restoration to 6 in 2020, the same proportional gain as songbirds over that period.","Songbirds were the most diverse group before restoration, with 22 species compared with 8 waterbird species and 3 raptors.","The total number of bird species recorded rose from 33 before restoration to 65 in 2020, nearly doubling."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The report compares waterbirds and songbirds, and the correct choice shows their very different gains. The choice beginning "Raptor species doubled from…" also misstates the songbird change.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h454$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Share of Households with Internet Access</caption><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>2010</th><th>2022</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Urban</td><td>78%</td><td>94%</td></tr><tr><td>Suburban</td><td>81%</td><td>95%</td></tr><tr><td>Rural</td><td>55%</td><td>82%</td></tr></tbody></table>A policy analyst argues that the gap in internet access between rural and other areas narrowed substantially over the period shown.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the analyst's argument?$q$, $q$["Rural households' internet access rose from 55% in 2010 to 82% in 2022, an increase of 27 percentage points.","Suburban households had the highest rate of internet access of the three areas in both 2010 and 2022.","Urban households' internet access rose from 78% to 94%, an increase of 16 percentage points over twelve years.","The gap between rural and urban households fell from 23 percentage points in 2010 to 12 in 2022."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A narrowing gap requires comparing the gap at both times, which only the correct choice does. The rural increase alone doesn't show the gap shrank.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h455$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Concentration of a Pollutant in River Water (micrograms per liter)</caption><thead><tr><th>Site</th><th>Before treatment plant</th><th>After treatment plant</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Upstream of city</td><td>2.1</td><td>2.0</td></tr><tr><td>Downstream of city</td><td>9.8</td><td>3.4</td></tr></tbody></table>Engineers concluded that the city's new treatment plant substantially reduced pollution entering the river from the city.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the engineers' conclusion?$q$, $q$["Upstream of the city, pollutant levels fell slightly, from 2.1 to 2.0 micrograms per liter, after the plant opened.","After the plant opened, downstream levels (3.4) remained higher than upstream levels (2.0) by 1.4 micrograms per liter.","Before the plant opened, downstream levels were more than four times as high as upstream levels.","Downstream levels fell from 9.8 to 3.4 after the plant opened, while upstream levels stayed about the same."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A sharp downstream drop with stable upstream levels attributes the improvement to the city's plant. The choices beginning "After the plant opened…" and "Before the plant opened…" describe differences without the change, and the choice beginning "Upstream of the city…" describes the upstream control site.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h456$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Votes for a Ballot Measure by Age Group</caption><thead><tr><th>Age group</th><th>Yes</th><th>No</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>18–34</td><td>68%</td><td>32%</td></tr><tr><td>35–54</td><td>51%</td><td>49%</td></tr><tr><td>55 and older</td><td>37%</td><td>63%</td></tr></tbody></table>A political analyst notes that support for the measure declined steadily with age: ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["more than two-thirds of voters aged 18–34 supported the measure, while fewer than one-third opposed it.","a majority of voters aged 55 and older (63%) voted no on the measure, the largest \"no\" share of any age group.","68% of voters aged 18–34 voted yes, compared with 51% of those aged 35–54 and 37% of those 55 and older.","voters aged 35–54 were nearly evenly split, with 51% voting yes and 49% voting no, a difference of 2 points."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A steady decline requires showing all three groups in order, which only the correct choice does.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h457$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Calories Burned per Hour by a 70-kg Person</caption><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Calories</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Walking (5 km/h)</td><td>250</td></tr><tr><td>Cycling (16 km/h)</td><td>420</td></tr><tr><td>Swimming (moderate)</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>Running (10 km/h)</td><td>700</td></tr></tbody></table>A trainer tells a client who has only 30 minutes for exercise that running would burn more calories than an hour of walking would. The data support this claim because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["running burns more calories per hour (700) than swimming (500), cycling (420), or walking (250).","running burns 700 calories per hour, nearly three times as many calories as walking burns in the same amount of time.","walking burns the fewest calories per hour of any activity listed in the table, only 250 calories.","30 minutes of running burns about 350 calories, more than the 250 calories burned by an hour of walking."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The claim compares half an hour of running with a full hour of walking, so the running figure must be halved: about 350 versus 250. The choices beginning "running burns 700 calories…" and "running burns more calories…" compare equal times, which doesn't address the claim.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h458$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Price per Kilogram of Two Crops</caption><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Crop A</th><th>Crop B</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2019</td><td>$1.20</td><td>$0.90</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>$1.25</td><td>$1.40</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>$1.30</td><td>$0.85</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>$1.35</td><td>$1.50</td></tr></tbody></table>A farmer deciding which crop to plant values predictable income. Based on the data, the farmer would most likely choose Crop A, since ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["its price rose by a steady $0.05 each year, while Crop B's price swung up and down by large amounts.","its price was higher than Crop B's in 2019 and 2021, the two odd-numbered years shown in the table.","Crop B's highest price, $1.50 in 2022, was greater than Crop A's highest price, $1.35, in the same year.","its average price over the four years, about $1.28, was higher than Crop B's average of about $1.16."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Predictability is about stability, and Crop A's steady rise contrasts with Crop B's swings. The choice beginning "its average price over…" compares averages, which addresses income level rather than predictability.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h459$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Reading Comprehension Scores After Different Study Conditions</caption><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Average score</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Silence</td><td>78</td></tr><tr><td>Instrumental music</td><td>77</td></tr><tr><td>Music with lyrics in a familiar language</td><td>66</td></tr><tr><td>Music with lyrics in an unfamiliar language</td><td>75</td></tr></tbody></table>Researchers hypothesize that music harms reading comprehension mainly when it contains words the reader can understand.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that support this hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Scores under every music condition were lower than scores in silence, by between 1 and 12 points.","Scores with lyrics in an unfamiliar language (75) were 3 points lower than scores in silence (78).","Scores were highest in silence (78), only slightly higher than the score with instrumental music (77) and 3 points higher than with unfamiliar lyrics.","Scores were far lower with lyrics in a familiar language (66) than with lyrics in an unfamiliar language (75) or with instrumental music (77)."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The hypothesis singles out understandable words, so the familiar-language condition must be compared with conditions lacking understandable words. The others don't isolate that factor.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h460$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Electric Vehicle Sales as a Share of New Car Sales</caption><thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>2018</th><th>2023</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Country P</td><td>2%</td><td>9%</td></tr><tr><td>Country Q</td><td>31%</td><td>82%</td></tr><tr><td>Country R</td><td>1%</td><td>18%</td></tr><tr><td>Country S</td><td>5%</td><td>7%</td></tr></tbody></table>A transportation researcher notes that the fastest growth in electric vehicle sales did not necessarily occur where adoption was already high. For example, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["Country R's share grew eighteenfold, from 1% to 18%, while Country Q's grew less than threefold, from 31% to 82%.","Country Q had the highest share of electric vehicle sales in both 2018 and 2023, far ahead of the other three countries.","Country S's share grew from 5% to only 7% over the period, the smallest increase of any country shown in the table.","Country P's share of new car sales that were electric rose from 2% in 2018 to 9% in 2023, an increase of 7 points."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The example must show faster relative growth in a country that started low compared with one that started high, as Country R versus Q does.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h461$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Annual Snowfall (cm) at Two Ski Resorts</caption><thead><tr><th>Decade</th><th>Resort 1 (1,200 m elevation)</th><th>Resort 2 (2,400 m elevation)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1990s</td><td>310</td><td>520</td></tr><tr><td>2000s</td><td>260</td><td>510</td></tr><tr><td>2010s</td><td>190</td><td>500</td></tr></tbody></table>Climate researchers predicted that warming would reduce snowfall most severely at lower elevations. The data are consistent with this prediction because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["the higher resort received more snowfall than the lower resort in every decade shown, by at least 210 cm.","snowfall at the lower resort fell by 120 cm, while at the higher resort it fell by only 20 cm.","snowfall declined at both resorts between the 1990s and the 2010s, by a combined total of 140 cm.","the lower resort received 190 cm of snowfall in the 2010s, its lowest total of the three decades shown."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The prediction compares declines by elevation, and the lower resort's decline was much larger. The other choices give levels or combined figures.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h462$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Library Program Attendance</caption><thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Sessions held</th><th>Total attendance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Children's story time</td><td>48</td><td>1,920</td></tr><tr><td>Coding workshop</td><td>12</td><td>660</td></tr><tr><td>Book club</td><td>24</td><td>360</td></tr><tr><td>Job-search help</td><td>20</td><td>500</td></tr></tbody></table>A librarian argues that, although children's story time had the highest total attendance, the coding workshop was actually the most popular program per session.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the librarian's argument?$q$, $q$["The coding workshop averaged 55 attendees per session, compared with 40 for story time, 25 for job-search help, and 15 for book club.","Children's story time had 1,920 total attendees across 48 sessions, nearly three times the coding workshop's total attendance of 660.","The coding workshop held only 12 sessions, the fewest of any program, while story time held 48 sessions.","The book club had both the lowest total attendance (360) and the lowest attendance per session of any program."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The argument is about attendance per session, which the correct choice calculates for every program. The others give totals or session counts.$q$, null),
  ($q$ii-h463$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Percentage of Seedlings Surviving One Year</caption><thead><tr><th>Treatment</th><th>Dry site</th><th>Wet site</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No mulch</td><td>34%</td><td>71%</td></tr><tr><td>Mulch</td><td>63%</td><td>74%</td></tr></tbody></table>Foresters hypothesize that mulch helps seedlings mainly by conserving soil moisture. If this hypothesis is correct, the data are consistent with it because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?$q$, $q$["mulch raised survival by 29 points at the dry site but by only 3 points at the wet site.","unmulched seedlings at the dry site had the lowest survival rate of any group, at only 34%.","seedlings survived better at the wet site than at the dry site, whether or not they were mulched.","mulched seedlings at the wet site had the highest survival rate of any group, at 74%."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If mulch works by conserving moisture, it should help most where soil is dry, which is the pattern in the correct choice. The others don't compare mulch's effect across sites.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The senator's speech was praised for its ______: she distilled a complex policy debate into three clear, memorable points without oversimplifying the stakes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["clarity","ambiguity","length","controversy"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The speech 'distilled a complex debate into three clear, memorable points,' which describes clarity, not ambiguity, length, or controversy.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Critics called the debut film ______, noting that its jarring shifts between comedy and tragedy left audiences unsure how to react.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["uneven","polished","predictable","silent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Jarring shifts' that left audiences 'unsure how to react' describes tonal inconsistency, which 'uneven' captures precisely.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The old bridge, though visually charming, was closed to traffic after an inspection revealed it to be structurally ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unsound","elegant","modern","popular"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The bridge was closed for safety reasons despite being charming, so the inspection must have found it structurally unsafe, or 'unsound.'$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Far from being ______, the committee's final report sparked a decade of heated public debate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["uncontroversial","lengthy","classified","published"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Far from being ___' signals a contrast with what follows: a report that sparked heated debate is the opposite of uncontroversial.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The chef's tasting menu was designed to be ______, guiding diners through flavors from delicate to bold over twelve small courses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["progressive","random","repetitive","brief"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A menu that moves deliberately 'from delicate to bold' across many courses is progressive in structure, not random, repetitive, or brief.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Investors grew ______ about the startup's prospects after its third consecutive quarter of missed revenue targets.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["skeptical","enthusiastic","indifferent","confident"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Three consecutive quarters of missed targets would logically make investors doubtful, or skeptical, rather than enthusiastic or confident.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The professor's lecture style was famously ______; she wove in personal anecdotes, historical tangents, and pop-culture references until the fifty-minute class felt like ten.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["digressive","monotonous","concise","formal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Weaving in 'tangents' and unrelated references describes a digressive style, the opposite of concise or formal.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Rather than resolving the mystery, the novel's final chapter only deepens the ______ surrounding the narrator's true identity.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ambiguity","clarity","resolution","certainty"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A final chapter that fails to resolve a mystery and instead 'deepens' the confusion is increasing ambiguity, not clarity or certainty.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The city's new noise ordinance proved remarkably ______, cutting complaints in half within a single month of enforcement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["effective","controversial","expensive","confusing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cutting complaints in half quickly is a mark of success, so 'effective' fits; the other choices aren't supported by the given result.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Though the two economists ______ on almost every policy question, they found unexpected common ground on the issue of infrastructure spending.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disagreed","collaborated","agreed","lectured"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Though...unexpected common ground' signals a contrast: the economists must generally disagree for finding agreement to be notable.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The sculpture's ______ form — all sharp angles and exposed steel — stood in deliberate contrast to the soft curves of the surrounding garden.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["angular","organic","fragile","invisible"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Sharp angles and exposed steel' describes an angular form, which the sentence explicitly contrasts with the garden's 'soft curves.'$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Editors praised the memoir for its ______ honesty, noting the author's willingness to describe her own failures in unflinching detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unflinching","guarded","selective","comedic"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence itself defines the quality: honesty shown by describing failures 'in unflinching detail,' so 'unflinching' matches directly.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The negotiations remained ______ for months, with neither side willing to make the first concession.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["stalled","swift","cordial","public"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Neither side making a concession 'for months' describes negotiations that are stalled, not swift.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Unlike her predecessor's cautious approach, the new CEO's strategy was decidedly ______, entering three new markets within her first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["aggressive","timid","secretive","gradual"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Entering three new markets in one year, in contrast to a 'cautious' predecessor, describes an aggressive strategy, not a timid or gradual one.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The ancient manuscript, water-damaged and missing several pages, offers only a ______ account of the kingdom's final years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fragmentary","comprehensive","vivid","official"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A manuscript that is 'water-damaged and missing several pages' would provide an incomplete, or fragmentary, account — not a comprehensive one.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Ants are often assumed to navigate purely by scent trails left by other ants. To test this, researchers blocked all scent trails on a foraging path and found that ants still returned to the nest efficiently, apparently relying on a form of visual memory of landmarks.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that challenges the assumption stated in the first sentence.","It restates the assumption from the first sentence in different words.","It introduces an unrelated topic about ant behavior.","It summarizes the passage's conclusion before any evidence is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The experiment described in the second sentence shows ants navigating without scent trails, directly complicating the assumption introduced in the first sentence.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Many assume that the invention of the printing press instantly made books cheap and widely available. In fact, for its first several decades, printed books remained luxury items, often more expensive than hand-copied manuscripts made by efficient scriptoria.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To complicate a popular assumption about the printing press's immediate effects","To argue that hand-copied manuscripts were always superior to printed books","To describe the mechanical process of operating a printing press","To praise the individual credited with inventing the printing press"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text opens with a common assumption and then complicates it with a historical detail ('in fact...'), matching the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A recent essay opens by describing the smell of rain on dry pavement, then pivots to a discussion of the chemical compound petrichor, before closing with a reflection on why humans across cultures find the scent pleasant.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the essay?$q$, $q$["It moves from a sensory description to a scientific explanation to a broader reflection.","It presents two competing scientific theories and evaluates each in turn.","It argues against a widely held belief using historical evidence.","It compares two different chemical compounds found in nature."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay's stated progression — sensory description, then chemistry, then reflection — matches the correct answer exactly.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The report's introduction outlines the rising cost of shipping. The middle section presents three case studies of companies that redesigned their supply chains. The conclusion offers general recommendations based on those cases.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the middle section of the report?$q$, $q$["To provide concrete examples that support the recommendations offered later","To contradict the claims made in the report's introduction","To define technical terms related to shipping logistics","To criticize the companies discussed as case studies"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Case studies presented before the conclusion's recommendations function as supporting evidence for those recommendations, matching the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The author begins by praising the architect's early work, then spends the remainder of the essay detailing the ways his later buildings abandoned the principles that made the early work distinctive.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To trace a shift in the architect's style and suggest it represented a departure from his strengths","To argue that the architect's later work was universally superior to his early work","To argue that the architect's reputation never recovered from the shift described","To defend the architect against all criticism of his career"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay traces a decline from the architect's early strengths, but never claims his reputation was permanently ruined — only that his later work departed from what made the early work distinctive, matching the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A textbook chapter opens with a dramatic account of a shipwreck, then uses the incident throughout the chapter to illustrate principles of ocean currents and buoyancy.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the opening account in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a memorable, concrete example that anchors abstract scientific concepts discussed later.","It proves that shipwrecks are a common occurrence in the ocean.","It argues against the value of studying ocean currents.","It states the chapter's main conclusion before any explanation is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using a vivid narrative to introduce and later illustrate technical concepts is a common structural device, matching the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The op-ed acknowledges the strongest argument against its position before spending three paragraphs systematically responding to each part of that argument.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Addressing a counterargument directly in order to strengthen the author's own position","Ignoring opposing views entirely to avoid weakening the argument","Presenting only anecdotal evidence without any structured argument","Avoiding any clear thesis so that readers can draw their own conclusions"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Explicitly acknowledging and then systematically rebutting the strongest counterargument is a concession-and-rebuttal strategy, described accurately by the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Economist A argues that remote work permanently reduced downtown office demand, citing vacancy rates that remain elevated three years after 2020.

Text 2: Economist B argues that office demand is cyclical and will rebound as companies mandate return-to-office policies, citing recent upticks in leasing activity in several major cities.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Economist B most likely respond to Economist A's claim?$q$, $q$["By arguing that the elevated vacancy rates reflect a temporary cycle rather than a permanent shift","By agreeing that office demand has disappeared permanently","By dismissing all vacancy rate data as irrelevant to the discussion","By claiming remote work never affected office demand at all"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Economist B's position is that demand is cyclical, not permanently reduced, so B would frame A's elevated vacancy rates as part of a temporary cycle rather than a lasting shift.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Historian A contends that the fall of the trade city was primarily caused by a shift in maritime trade routes away from its harbor.

Text 2: Historian B contends that internal political corruption, documented in surviving tax records, was the primary cause of the city's decline, occurring even before trade routes shifted.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' views?$q$, $q$["Historian A emphasizes an external economic cause, while Historian B emphasizes an internal political cause.","Historian B treats the political corruption as a consequence of the trade route shift rather than an independent cause.","Historian B relies exclusively on maritime trade records.","Historian A blames the decline on political corruption."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A points to trade routes (an external, economic factor), while Historian B points to corruption that occurred 'even before trade routes shifted' — an independent, internal cause, not a downstream consequence — the contrast in the correct answer.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Nutrition researcher A found that participants on a high-protein breakfast reported feeling full longer than those on a high-carbohydrate breakfast.

Text 2: Nutrition researcher B found no significant difference in reported fullness between high-protein and high-carbohydrate breakfasts, though her study used a considerably smaller sample size.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two researchers?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's results do not confirm Researcher A's findings, though the smaller sample size may limit that comparison.","Researcher B's findings exactly replicate Researcher A's results.","Researcher B's larger sample size makes her null result more reliable than Researcher A's.","Researcher A found no difference between the two meal types."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Researcher B's null result differs from Researcher A's positive result, but the passage notes B's sample size was considerably smaller, not larger, a detail that qualifies rather than strengthens the comparison.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The critic praised the film's ______ pacing, noting that not a single scene felt rushed or drawn out.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["measured","erratic","frantic","careless"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pacing is described as never feeling 'rushed or drawn out,' meaning it was carefully controlled — 'measured' captures that balance.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The article opens by describing a single beekeeper's declining honey yields, then broadens to examine regional pollinator population data, and finally proposes several conservation policies.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a specific example to broader evidence to proposed solutions.","It presents two competing theories and evaluates each in turn.","It begins with a general policy proposal and ends with a specific anecdote.","It compares beekeeping practices in two different countries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text follows a clear progression: one beekeeper's story, then regional data, then policy proposals — moving from specific to general to solution-oriented.$q$, null),
