  ($q$conv-485$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The results of the clinical trial ______ that the treatment is effective.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has suggested","suggest","is suggesting","suggests"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Results" is plural, so it takes the plural verb "suggest."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-486$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Octopuses have three hearts______two of them pump blood to the gills.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; "," ",", ","; and "]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Both "Octopuses have three hearts" and "two of them pump blood to the gills" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-487$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The town's oldest oak, which has stood for nearly 300 years______was struck by lightning last week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",", ","; ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase between the subject and the verb is nonessential, so it must be set off by a pair of commas. The opening comma is already there; a closing comma is needed after the phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-488$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The inventor patented her design in 1931______it was not manufactured until a decade later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", "," ","; ","; and "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both "The inventor patented her design in 1931" and "it was not manufactured until a decade later" are independent clauses. They can be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on sentence, and a semicolon should not be combined with "and."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-489$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The stack of unpaid invoices on the manager's desk ______ taller every week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["grows","are growing","have grown","grow"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular "stack," not the plural "invoices" in the prepositional phrase, so the singular verb "grows" is needed.$q$, null),
