  ($q$pd-499$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot of the age (in years) of a used car and its resale price (in hundreds of dollars) has the line of best fit y = 340 − 6x, where x is the age and y is the predicted price. Which of the following is the best interpretation of the slope of this line?$q$, $q$["The predicted resale price of a new car is 6 hundred dollars.","For each additional year of age, the predicted resale price increases by 6 hundred dollars.","The predicted resale price is 340 hundred dollars when the car is 6 years old.","For each additional year of age, the predicted resale price decreases by 6 hundred dollars."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The slope −6 means that each 1-year increase in age corresponds to a 6 hundred dollar decrease in predicted price.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-500$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 300 residents was selected from a town of 2000 residents. In the sample, 90 residents said they ride the city bus at least once a week. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of residents in the town who ride the bus at least once a week?$q$, $q$["90","22","1400","600"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sample proportion is 90/300. Applying it to the whole town: 2000 × 90/300 = 600.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-501$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 300 residents was selected from a town of 6000 residents. In the sample, 30 residents said they ride the city bus at least once a week. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of residents in the town who ride the bus at least once a week?$q$, $q$["5400","30","600","200"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sample proportion is 30/300. Applying it to the whole town: 6000 × 30/300 = 600.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-502$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A car uses 1 gallon of gas for every 60 miles driven. Gas costs $5 per gallon. What is the cost of the gas, in dollars, for a 600-mile trip?$q$, $q$["25","250","300","50"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Gallons needed = 600/60 = 10. Cost = 10 × $5 = $50.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-503$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A researcher surveyed a random sample of 400 adults from one city about their commute and found that 62% drive alone. Which conclusion is best supported?$q$, $q$["It is reasonable to conclude that most adults in the country drive alone to work.","The survey proves that driving alone is the best commuting choice.","The survey shows that 62% of the adults in the sample who do not drive alone prefer other options.","It is reasonable to estimate that most adults in that city drive alone to work."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A random sample from the city supports a conclusion about the city, not the whole country, and a survey of habits cannot show which choice is "best."$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-504$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, $q$A survey of 176 people recorded the following. Among the juniors, 27 play a sport and 51 do not play a sport. Among the seniors, 56 play a sport and 42 do not play a sport.$q$, $q$If one of the seniors is selected at random, what is the probability that the person selected is among those who play a sport?$q$, $q$["3/7","7/22","56/83","4/7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Only the 98 seniors are considered. Of these, 56 play a sport, so the probability is 56/98 = 4/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-505$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, null, $q$At a constant price per item, 9 notebooks cost $63. How much, in dollars, would 19 notebooks cost?$q$, $q$["133","171","140","73"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The unit price is 63/9 = $7. For 19: 19 × 7 = $133.$q$, null),
