  ($q$pd-517$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A teacher surveyed only the students who stayed after school for tutoring and found that 90% felt confident about the exam. Which conclusion is best supported?$q$, $q$["Tutoring caused 90% of students to feel confident.","Students who did not attend tutoring felt less confident.","Most students who attend tutoring felt confident, but the result may not apply to all students.","90% of all students in the school felt confident about the exam."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sample was not random and included only tutoring attendees, so the result cannot be generalized to all students or used to infer causation.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-518$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$Data set P is 44, 49, 54, 59, 64. Data set Q is 53, 54, 54, 54, 55. Which statement about the standard deviations of the two data sets is true?$q$, $q$["The two data sets have equal standard deviations.","Data set P has the greater standard deviation.","Data set Q has the greater standard deviation.","The standard deviations cannot be compared without the medians."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Both sets have a mean of 54, but the values in P are spread much farther from the mean (up to 10 away) than those in Q (at most 1 away). The more spread-out set has the greater standard deviation.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-519$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 100 residents was selected from a town of 8000 residents. In the sample, 60 residents said they ride the city bus at least once a week. Based on the sample, which of the following is the best estimate of the number of residents in the town who ride the bus at least once a week?$q$, $q$["3200","133","60","4800"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sample proportion is 60/100. Applying it to the whole town: 8000 × 60/100 = 4800.$q$, null),
