  ($q$pd-707$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$Based on a random sample, a survey estimated that 42% of residents of a county approve of a proposal, with a margin of error of 5 percentage points. Which is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["Between 37% and 47% of the people in the sample approve of the proposal.","It is plausible that between 32% and 52% of all residents of a county approve of the proposal.","Exactly 42% of all residents of a county approve of the proposal.","It is plausible that between 37% and 47% of all residents of a county approve of the proposal."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The margin of error gives a range of plausible values for the population: 42 ± 5, or 37% to 47%. The sample's own percentage is known exactly, so the margin applies to the whole population, not the sample.$q$, null),
