  ($q$pd-712$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$A scatterplot shows the relationship between the number of hours studied, x, and the test score, y. The line of best fit is y = 2.2x + 4. Based on the line of best fit, what is the predicted value of y when x = 8?$q$, $q$["17.6","21.6","26.4","14.2"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Substitute x = 8: y = 2.2(8) + 4 = 17.6 + 4 = 21.6.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-713$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, null, $q$A bag contains 4 red marbles, 11 blue marbles, and 5 green marbles. If one marble is chosen at random, what is the probability that it is green?$q$, $q$["3/4","5/21","1/4","1/3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$There are 4 + 11 + 5 = 20 marbles, and 5 are green. The probability is 5/20 = 1/4.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-714$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, null, $q$The price of a jacket was increased by 30% and then the new price was decreased by 25%. Which describes the overall change from the original price?$q$, $q$["5/2% decrease","5/2% increase","55% increase","5% increase"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Multiply the multipliers: (1.3)(0.75) = 0.975. So the final price is 97.5% of the original, a 5/2% decrease.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-715$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 2, null, $q$<table class="data-table"><tr><th></th><th>Yes</th><th>No</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td>Group A</td><td>13</td><td>50</td><td>63</td></tr><tr><td>Group B</td><td>35</td><td>47</td><td>82</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>48</td><td>97</td><td>145</td></tr></table>The table summarizes the responses of 145 people in a survey. If one of the people in the group b row is selected at random, what is the probability that the answer recorded is “Yes”?$q$, $q$["35/82","7/29","35/48","47/82"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Only the 82 people in the Group B row are considered. Of them, 35 answered “Yes,” so the probability is 35/82.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-716$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, null, $q$The data set 93, 61, 37, 57, 94, 28, 42, 79 lists the scores of a team in its recent games. What is the range of the scores?$q$, $q$["−14","61","94","66"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The range is the maximum minus the minimum: 94 − 28 = 66.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-717$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, null, $q$A researcher randomly selected 300 adults from a city's voter list and asked whether they support a new park. Of them, 186 said yes. Which conclusion is best supported by this information?$q$, $q$["Exactly 62% of all adults in the country support new parks.","Every voter in the city supports the park.","Building the park will cause voter turnout to increase.","About 62% of the city's registered voters likely support the new park."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sample was randomly selected from the city's voter list, so the result (186/300 = 62%) can be generalized only to that population, and only approximately. The other conclusions overreach to a different population, claim certainty, or assert causation.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-718$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, null, $q$The mean score of 4 students on a quiz is 87. A new student takes the quiz and scores 62. What is the mean score of all 5 students?$q$, $q$["83","82","74.5","87"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The original total is 4 × 87 = 348. Adding 62 gives 410. The new mean is 410/5 = 82.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-719$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A metal sample has a density of 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter and a mass of 67.5 grams. What is the volume, in cubic centimeters, of the sample?$q$, $q$["182.25","0.04","25","70.2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Density = mass/volume, so volume = mass/density = 67.5/2.7 = 25 cubic centimeters.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-720$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, null, $q$A train travels at a constant speed of 60 miles per hour. How many minutes does it take the train to travel 450 miles?$q$, $q$["470","450","8","750"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Time = distance ÷ speed = 450/60 = 7.5 hours. Multiply by 60 minutes per hour: 7.5 × 60 = 450 minutes.$q$, null),
