  ($q$pd-725$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$Based on a random sample, a survey estimated that 46% of voters in a state approve of a proposal, with a margin of error of 3 percentage points. Which is the most appropriate conclusion?$q$, $q$["It is plausible that between 40% and 52% of all voters in a state approve of the proposal.","Exactly 46% of all voters in a state approve of the proposal.","Between 43% and 49% of the people in the sample approve of the proposal.","It is plausible that between 43% and 49% of all voters in a state approve of the proposal."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The margin of error gives a range of plausible values for the population: 46 ± 3, or 43% to 49%. The sample's own percentage is known exactly, so the margin applies to the whole population, not the sample.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-726$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, null, $q$A random sample of 100 season-ticket holders of a baseball team was surveyed, and 37 of them said they would buy a parking pass. The baseball team has 6,000 season-ticket holders in all. Based on the sample, which is the best estimate of the total number of season-ticket holders who would say this?$q$, $q$["370","2,320","2,220","3,780"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sample proportion is 37/100. Applying it to all 6,000 season-ticket holders: 6,000 × 37/100 = 2,220.$q$, null),
  ($q$pd-727$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, null, $q$Last year, the number of visitors to a museum was 80. This year it is 120. By what percent did it increase?$q$, $q$["40%","33.3%","150%","50%"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The increase is 120 − 80 = 40. As a percent of the original: 40/80 = 0.5, or 50%.$q$, null),
