  ($q$adv-bb21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$−x<sup>2</sup> + bx − 441 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, b is a positive integer. The equation has no real solution. What is the greatest possible value of b?$q$, $q$["20","41","21","42"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A quadratic equation has no real solution when its discriminant is negative. Here the discriminant is b<sup>2</sup> − 4(−1)(−441) = b<sup>2</sup> − 1764. So b<sup>2</sup> < 1764, which means b < 42 for positive b. The greatest integer less than 42 is 41. (b = 42 makes the discriminant 0, which gives exactly one real solution.)$q$, null),
