  ($q$geo-bb9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation (x − 6)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 3)<sup>2</sup> = 16 represents circle A. Circle B is obtained by shifting circle A right 3 units in the xy-plane. Which of the following equations represents circle B?$q$, $q$["(x − 9)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 3)<sup>2</sup> = 16","(x − 6)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 6)<sup>2</sup> = 16","(x − 6)<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 16","(x − 3)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 3)<sup>2</sup> = 16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Circle A has center (6, 3) and radius 4. Shifting it right 3 units moves the center to (9, 3) and leaves the radius unchanged, so circle B is (x − 9)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 3)<sup>2</sup> = 16. Remember that (x − h) and (y − k) use the opposite sign of the center's coordinates.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation (x − 1)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 6)<sup>2</sup> = 36 represents circle A. Circle B is obtained by shifting circle A right 5 units in the xy-plane. Which of the following equations represents circle B?$q$, $q$["(x − 1)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 1)<sup>2</sup> = 36","(x + 4)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 6)<sup>2</sup> = 36","(x − 1)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 11)<sup>2</sup> = 36","(x − 6)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 6)<sup>2</sup> = 36"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Circle A has center (1, 6) and radius 6. Shifting it right 5 units moves the center to (6, 6) and leaves the radius unchanged, so circle B is (x − 6)<sup>2</sup> + (y − 6)<sup>2</sup> = 36. Remember that (x − h) and (y − k) use the opposite sign of the center's coordinates.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (8k + 43)° and (8k + 73)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (8k + 43)°?$q$, $q$["75","32","105","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (8k + 43) + (8k + 73) = 180. That gives 16k + 116 = 180, so k = 4. Then 8(4) + 43 = 75. (105 is the other angle; k = 4 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (4k + 36)° and (8k + 72)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (4k + 36)°?$q$, $q$["60","6","120","36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (4k + 36) + (8k + 72) = 180. That gives 12k + 108 = 180, so k = 6. Then 4(6) + 36 = 60. (120 is the other angle; k = 6 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (4k + 114)° and (4k + 34)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (4k + 114)°?$q$, $q$["4","130","16","50"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (4k + 114) + (4k + 34) = 180. That gives 8k + 148 = 180, so k = 4. Then 4(4) + 114 = 130. (50 is the other angle; k = 4 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (6k + 12)° and (4k + 48)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (6k + 12)°?$q$, $q$["96","84","60","12"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (6k + 12) + (4k + 48) = 180. That gives 10k + 60 = 180, so k = 12. Then 6(12) + 12 = 84. (96 is the other angle; k = 12 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (3k + 66)° and (6k + 24)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (3k + 66)°?$q$, $q$["10","96","45","84"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (3k + 66) + (6k + 24) = 180. That gives 9k + 90 = 180, so k = 10. Then 3(10) + 66 = 96. (84 is the other angle; k = 10 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (3k + 50)° and (3k + 76)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (3k + 50)°?$q$, $q$["103","9","77","27"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (3k + 50) + (3k + 76) = 180. That gives 6k + 126 = 180, so k = 9. Then 3(9) + 50 = 77. (103 is the other angle; k = 9 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (9k + 44)° and (4k + 6)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (9k + 44)°?$q$, $q$["134","46","10","65"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (9k + 44) + (4k + 6) = 180. That gives 13k + 50 = 180, so k = 10. Then 9(10) + 44 = 134. (46 is the other angle; k = 10 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (9k − 48)° and (2k + 107)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (9k − 48)°?$q$, $q$["129","11","51","61"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (9k − 48) + (2k + 107) = 180. That gives 11k + 59 = 180, so k = 11. Then 9(11) − 48 = 51. (129 is the other angle; k = 11 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (2k + 80)° and (6k + 28)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (2k + 80)°?$q$, $q$["9","82","36","98"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (2k + 80) + (6k + 28) = 180. That gives 8k + 108 = 180, so k = 9. Then 2(9) + 80 = 98. (82 is the other angle; k = 9 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, null, $q$Lines m and n are parallel and are intersected by transversal t. Two same-side interior angles formed by these lines have measures (5k + 97)° and (5k + 3)°. What is the measure, in degrees, of the angle with measure (5k + 97)°?$q$, $q$["8","137","43","40"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Same-side interior angles formed by parallel lines are supplementary, so (5k + 97) + (5k + 3) = 180. That gives 10k + 100 = 180, so k = 8. Then 5(8) + 97 = 137. (43 is the other angle; k = 8 is not an angle measure.)$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 51 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (103/52)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["3","2","4","1"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(51) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 204x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 102, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(102) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 408x. So 52(2x<sup>2</sup> + 408x) = 103(2x<sup>2</sup> + 204x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 102x = 204, so x = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 41 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (88/47)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["12","6","24","13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(41) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 164x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 82, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(82) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 328x. So 47(2x<sup>2</sup> + 328x) = 88(2x<sup>2</sup> + 164x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 82x = 984, so x = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 53 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (110/57)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["16","9","4","8"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(53) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 212x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 106, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(106) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 424x. So 57(2x<sup>2</sup> + 424x) = 110(2x<sup>2</sup> + 212x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 106x = 848, so x = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 74 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (149/75)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["1","3","2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(74) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 296x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 148, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(148) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 592x. So 75(2x<sup>2</sup> + 592x) = 149(2x<sup>2</sup> + 296x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 148x = 296, so x = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 83 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (168/85)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["2","4","5","8"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(83) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 332x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 166, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(166) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 664x. So 85(2x<sup>2</sup> + 664x) = 168(2x<sup>2</sup> + 332x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 166x = 664, so x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 98 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (100/51)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["4","16","9","8"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(98) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 392x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 196, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(196) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 784x. So 51(2x<sup>2</sup> + 784x) = 100(2x<sup>2</sup> + 392x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 98x = 784, so x = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 24 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (33/17)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["3","2","4","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(24) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 96x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 48, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(48) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 192x. So 17(2x<sup>2</sup> + 192x) = 33(2x<sup>2</sup> + 96x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 32x = 96, so x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 16 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (9/5)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["16","8","9","4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(16) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 64x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 32, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(32) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 128x. So 5(2x<sup>2</sup> + 128x) = 9(2x<sup>2</sup> + 64x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 8x = 64, so x = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 35 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (76/41)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["13","12","24","6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(35) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 140x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 70, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(70) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 280x. So 41(2x<sup>2</sup> + 280x) = 76(2x<sup>2</sup> + 140x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 70x = 840, so x = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, null, $q$Two identical rectangular prisms each have a height of 76 centimeters (cm). The base of each prism is a square, and the surface area of each prism is K cm<sup>2</sup>. If the prisms are glued together along a square base, the resulting prism has a surface area of (77/39)K cm<sup>2</sup>. What is the side length, in cm, of each square base?$q$, $q$["8","5","2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let x be the side of each square base. Each prism has surface area K = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(76) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 304x. Gluing two prisms base to base makes one prism with height 152, whose surface area is 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x(152) = 2x<sup>2</sup> + 608x. So 39(2x<sup>2</sup> + 608x) = 77(2x<sup>2</sup> + 304x). Dividing by x and simplifying gives 76x = 304, so x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle JKL, angle J is a right angle and 39cos(K) = 15. What is the value of cos(L)?$q$, $q$["12/13","12/5","5/12","5/13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$From 39cos(K) = 15, cos(K) = 5/13. So the side adjacent to angle K is 5k and the hypotenuse is 13k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 12k. That leg is adjacent to angle L, so cos(L) = 12k/13k = 12/13. Equivalently, K and L are complementary, so cos(L) = sin(K).$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle JKL, angle J is a right angle and 68cos(K) = 32. What is the value of cos(L)?$q$, $q$["15/17","8/15","8/17","15/8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$From 68cos(K) = 32, cos(K) = 8/17. So the side adjacent to angle K is 8k and the hypotenuse is 17k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 15k. That leg is adjacent to angle L, so cos(L) = 15k/17k = 15/17. Equivalently, K and L are complementary, so cos(L) = sin(K).$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle DEF, angle D is a right angle and 111cos(E) = 105. What is the value of cos(F)?$q$, $q$["12/37","35/37","12/35","35/12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$From 111cos(E) = 105, cos(E) = 35/37. So the side adjacent to angle E is 35k and the hypotenuse is 37k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 12k. That leg is adjacent to angle F, so cos(F) = 12k/37k = 12/37. Equivalently, E and F are complementary, so cos(F) = sin(E).$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle A is a right angle and 37cos(B) = 35. What is the value of cos(C)?$q$, $q$["12/35","12/37","35/37","35/12"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$From 37cos(B) = 35, cos(B) = 35/37. So the side adjacent to angle B is 35k and the hypotenuse is 37k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 12k. That leg is adjacent to angle C, so cos(C) = 12k/37k = 12/37. Equivalently, B and C are complementary, so cos(C) = sin(B).$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle A is a right angle and 5cos(B) = 4. What is the value of cos(C)?$q$, $q$["3/4","3/5","4/5","4/3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$From 5cos(B) = 4, cos(B) = 4/5. So the side adjacent to angle B is 4k and the hypotenuse is 5k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 3k. That leg is adjacent to angle C, so cos(C) = 3k/5k = 3/5. Equivalently, B and C are complementary, so cos(C) = sin(B).$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle A is a right angle and 17cos(B) = 15. What is the value of cos(C)?$q$, $q$["8/17","8/15","15/8","15/17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$From 17cos(B) = 15, cos(B) = 15/17. So the side adjacent to angle B is 15k and the hypotenuse is 17k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 8k. That leg is adjacent to angle C, so cos(C) = 8k/17k = 8/17. Equivalently, B and C are complementary, so cos(C) = sin(B).$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle ABC, angle A is a right angle and 244cos(B) = 240. What is the value of cos(C)?$q$, $q$["60/11","11/60","60/61","11/61"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$From 244cos(B) = 240, cos(B) = 60/61. So the side adjacent to angle B is 60k and the hypotenuse is 61k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 11k. That leg is adjacent to angle C, so cos(C) = 11k/61k = 11/61. Equivalently, B and C are complementary, so cos(C) = sin(B).$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb38$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle DEF, angle D is a right angle and 5cos(E) = 4. What is the value of cos(F)?$q$, $q$["3/4","4/5","4/3","3/5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$From 5cos(E) = 4, cos(E) = 4/5. So the side adjacent to angle E is 4k and the hypotenuse is 5k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 3k. That leg is adjacent to angle F, so cos(F) = 3k/5k = 3/5. Equivalently, E and F are complementary, so cos(F) = sin(E).$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle JKL, angle J is a right angle and 123cos(K) = 120. What is the value of cos(L)?$q$, $q$["40/9","40/41","9/40","9/41"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$From 123cos(K) = 120, cos(K) = 40/41. So the side adjacent to angle K is 40k and the hypotenuse is 41k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 9k. That leg is adjacent to angle L, so cos(L) = 9k/41k = 9/41. Equivalently, K and L are complementary, so cos(L) = sin(K).$q$, null),
  ($q$geo-bb40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, null, $q$In triangle DEF, angle D is a right angle and 51cos(E) = 45. What is the value of cos(F)?$q$, $q$["8/17","8/15","15/17","15/8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$From 51cos(E) = 45, cos(E) = 15/17. So the side adjacent to angle E is 15k and the hypotenuse is 17k for some k > 0. By the Pythagorean theorem the remaining leg is 8k. That leg is adjacent to angle F, so cos(F) = 8k/17k = 8/17. Equivalently, E and F are complementary, so cos(F) = sin(E).$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-bb1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$For decades, historians treated the diary of Hannah Calloway, the wife of a nineteenth-century mill owner, as a straightforward record of life in a factory town. A recent study complicates that view. By comparing the diary with the town's payroll ledgers and newspaper reports, researchers found that Calloway described strikes and layoffs only when they affected her own household; disputes that disrupted workers' lives but left her family untouched rarely appear. The diary, the researchers conclude, is best read as a ______ account: accurate in what it includes but shaped by what its author chose to notice.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["selective","fabricated","exhaustive","impartial"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The researchers found the diary accurate but limited to events that touched Calloway's own household, so it is "selective": it includes some events and leaves out others. "Fabricated" contradicts the claim that the diary is accurate in what it includes. "Exhaustive" and "impartial" both contradict the finding that many disputes are missing and that the account reflects what its author chose to notice.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-bb2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Rosa Mbeki expected that removing an invasive grass from test plots would allow native wildflowers to return. In wet years, it did: native species recovered within two seasons. In dry years, however, the cleared plots were quickly recolonized by other invasive plants, and the wildflowers never became established. Mbeki concluded that the benefit of removing the grass was ______: whether it helped native species depended on conditions that the removal itself could not control.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["negligible","permanent","contingent","immediate"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The colon introduces a definition of the missing word: the benefit "depended on conditions" such as rainfall. "Contingent" means dependent on something else, which fits exactly. "Negligible" is tempting because removal failed in dry years, but it clearly helped in wet years, so the benefit wasn't insignificant. "Permanent" and "immediate" describe how long or how fast the benefit lasted or arrived, not whether it depended on conditions.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-bb3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Architect Lina Bo Bardi's design for the São Paulo Museum of Art lifts the entire gallery above street level on four red pillars, leaving a broad open plaza beneath it. Pedestrians walking along the avenue are arrested by the sudden gap in the city's dense row of buildings: the museum seems to hover, and the view runs straight through to the valley beyond. Many people pause there, in the shade, before ever going inside.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word "arrested" most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["Taken into custody","Brought to a stop","Slowed in development","Made uneasy"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The text says that many people "pause there" after noticing the gap beneath the museum, so "arrested" means brought to a stop. "Taken into custody" is the legal sense of the word and doesn't fit pedestrians admiring a building. "Slowed in development" applies to growth, not to people walking by. Nothing suggests the view makes people uneasy; the text presents the pause as a response to a striking view.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-bb4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$When the transportation committee released its report on the city's redesigned bus network, its endorsement was qualified. Members praised the shorter travel times on the main corridors, but they noted that several outlying neighborhoods had lost service entirely, and they recommended restoring at least two discontinued routes before the new network was made permanent.$q$, $q$As used in the text, what does the word "qualified" most nearly mean?$q$, $q$["Officially certified","Highly skilled","Limited by reservations","Openly enthusiastic"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The committee praised part of the redesign but raised concerns and attached a condition (restoring two routes), so its endorsement was "qualified" in the sense of limited by reservations. "Officially certified" and "highly skilled" are other common meanings of "qualified" that don't describe an endorsement. "Openly enthusiastic" ignores the concerns the committee raised.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-bb5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Critics initially dismissed the composer's early string quartets as ______, pointing to melodies and harmonies borrowed almost directly from the composers she had studied. Later scholars, however, found that the borrowings were deliberate: each quotation is altered in a way that comments on its source, turning imitation into a kind of argument.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inscrutable","unfinished","extravagant","derivative"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The critics' evidence was that the quartets borrowed melodies and harmonies "almost directly" from earlier composers, so they judged the music "derivative," or imitative of other work. The later scholars respond to exactly that charge by showing the imitation was deliberate. "Inscrutable" (hard to understand), "unfinished," and "extravagant" don't follow from evidence about borrowing.$q$, null),
  ($q$cs-bb6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Economist Teresa Alvarado was hopeful that a new regional trade agreement would benefit farmers across the region. Her optimism was ______ by her own data, however: although agricultural exports rose sharply in the agreement's first year, most of the gains went to a handful of large producers, and small farms saw little change in income.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["tempered","fueled","disguised","replaced"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"However" signals that the data worked against Alvarado's hope: exports rose, but small farms gained little. Her optimism was therefore "tempered," or moderated. "Fueled" would mean the data increased her optimism, which the mixed results don't support. "Disguised" makes no sense here, and "replaced" goes too far, since the data showed real gains and didn't eliminate every reason for hope.$q$, null)
